var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"generated/minimal_market/#Two-regions-and-a-dairy","page":"Two regions and a dairy (minimal)","text":"The smallest model that is still a market: two regions, two products, one transformation and one trade route. Everything else in this package is more of these same four things.\n\nThe Pasture has the cows; the City has the people. Milk is either drunk as it is or turned into cheese, in either place, and either milk or cheese can be carried between the two at a cost. Four questions follow, and none of them has an obvious answer: how much of the milk is drunk and how much goes to the dairy, where the dairy stands, what each region pays, and what travels. Nobody decides. The answer is whatever clears both markets at once.\n\nusing Markets, DataFrames","title":"Two regions and a dairy"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/minimal_market/#The-economy","page":"Two regions and a dairy (minimal)","text":"Two regions and two products. Quantities are tonnes, prices euros per tonne.\n\nregions  = [\"Pasture\", \"City\"]\nproducts = [\"milk\", \"cheese\"]\n\nMilk comes from the herds, on a curve through one observed point: last year the Pasture sold 2000 at €400 the tonne. The City keeps a few herds at its edges, where land is dear.\n\nsupply = [SupplyCurve(product = \"milk\", region = \"Pasture\", p0 = 400, q0 = 2000, elasticity = 0.6),\n          SupplyCurve(product = \"milk\", region = \"City\",    p0 = 450, q0 =  300, elasticity = 0.6)]\n\nNow the part that makes this more than an arithmetic exercise: milk is both a final product and an input. People drink it, and dairies turn it into cheese. Each use has its own demand, and neither is told how much milk it may have.\n\ndemand = [DemandCurve(product = \"milk\",   region = \"Pasture\", p0 =  400, q0 = 200, elasticity = 0.3),\n          DemandCurve(product = \"milk\",   region = \"City\",    p0 =  450, q0 = 600, elasticity = 0.3),\n          DemandCurve(product = \"cheese\", region = \"Pasture\", p0 = 5300, q0 =  30, elasticity = 0.5),\n          DemandCurve(product = \"cheese\", region = \"City\",    p0 = 5350, q0 = 120, elasticity = 0.5)]\n\nOne transformation, and it may run in either region. Cheesemaking is a fixed recipe — ten tonnes of milk to the tonne of cheese, the rest leaving as whey and water — and €1300 covers everything the model does not name: the rennet, the cellar, the cheesemaker.\n\nprocesses = [Process(name    = \"dairy\",\n                     inputs  = [leontief(product = \"milk\", coeff = 10.0)],\n                     outputs = [\"cheese\" => 1.0],\n                     vacost  = 1300)]\n\nBoth products can be carried either way, at €45 the tonne. Writing a route down is what makes a product tradable — anything with no route stays where it was made — and routes are directional, hence four entries for two products. Note that the rate is the same for both: nothing in the data says that one is cheaper to move than the other.\n\ntransport_costs = Dict((\"milk\",   \"Pasture\", \"City\") => 45.0, (\"milk\",   \"City\", \"Pasture\") => 45.0,\n                       (\"cheese\", \"Pasture\", \"City\") => 45.0, (\"cheese\", \"City\", \"Pasture\") => 45.0)","title":"The economy"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/minimal_market/#Looking-before-solving","page":"Two regions and a dairy (minimal)","text":"The pieces go into one MarketData. Typing its name gives back only how much it holds; printing it gives a summary of the economy that was actually written down: what each product does, how the processes chain, where goods may go and at what cost. It is the cheapest check there is that the data say what was meant.\n\neconomy = MarketData(; regions, products, demand, supply, processes, transport_costs)\nMarketData(2 regions, 2 products, 1 process)\n\nprint(economy)\nMarketData: 2 regions, 2 products, 1 process, 4 trade routes, 0 taxes\n  regions: Pasture, City\n\nProducts\n          supplied in  consumed in  made by  used by  routes\n  milk              2            2        ·        1       2\n  cheese            ·            2        1        ·       2\n  (regions supplying and consuming it, processes making and using it)\n\nTransformations\n  dairy  10 milk ─→ cheese  cost 1300\n\nTrade routes and their cost per unit\n  milk    Pasture⇄City 45\n  cheese  Pasture⇄City 45\n\nThe mathematical program it becomes can be read too, before anything is solved: jump_model returns the JuMP model that solve_market would hand to the solver, with every variable and constraint named after the region and product it belongs to, and latex_formulation typesets it.\n\nlatex_formulation(jump_model(economy))\n$$ \\begin{aligned}\n\\max\\quad & {\\left({\\left({\\left({\\left({{\\left({8.020162939098153 \\times 10^{9}} * {\\left({D_{Pasture,milk}} ^ {-2.3333333333333335}\\right)}\\right)} / {1}} + {{\\left({3.5134995028855756 \\times 10^{11}} * {\\left({D_{City,milk}} ^ {-2.3333333333333335}\\right)}\\right)} / {1}} + {{\\left({-4770000} * {\\left({D_{Pasture,cheese}} ^ {-1}\\right)}\\right)} / {1}} + {{\\left({-77040000} * {\\left({D_{City,cheese}} ^ {-1}\\right)}\\right)} / {1}}\\right)} - {\\left({{1} * {\\left({0.012551867814040673} * {\\left({S_{City,milk}} ^ {2.666666666666667}\\right)}\\right)}} + {{1} * {\\left({0.00047247039371057717} * {\\left({S_{Pasture,milk}} ^ {2.666666666666667}\\right)}\\right)}}\\right)}\\right)} - {\\left(1300 z_{Pasture,dairy} + 1300 z_{City,dairy}\\right)}\\right)} - {\\left(45 T_{milk,Pasture,City} + 45 T_{milk,City,Pasture} + 45 T_{cheese,City,Pasture} + 45 T_{cheese,Pasture,City}\\right)}\\right)} - {\\left(0\\right)}\\\\\n\\text{Subject to} \\quad & -D_{Pasture,milk} + S_{Pasture,milk} - 10 z_{Pasture,dairy} - T_{milk,Pasture,City} + T_{milk,City,Pasture} = 0\\\\\n & -D_{Pasture,cheese} + z_{Pasture,dairy} + T_{cheese,City,Pasture} - T_{cheese,Pasture,City} = 0\\\\\n & -D_{City,milk} + S_{City,milk} - 10 z_{City,dairy} + T_{milk,Pasture,City} - T_{milk,City,Pasture} = 0\\\\\n & -D_{City,cheese} + z_{City,dairy} - T_{cheese,City,Pasture} + T_{cheese,Pasture,City} = 0\\\\\n & D_{Pasture,milk} \\geq 0.00019999999999999998\\\\\n & D_{City,milk} \\geq 0.0006\\\\\n & D_{Pasture,cheese} \\geq 2.9999999999999997 \\times 10^{-5}\\\\\n & D_{City,cheese} \\geq 0.00011999999999999999\\\\\n & S_{Pasture,milk} \\geq 0\\\\\n & S_{City,milk} \\geq 0\\\\\n & z_{Pasture,dairy} \\geq 0\\\\\n & z_{City,dairy} \\geq 0\\\\\n & T_{milk,Pasture,City} \\geq 0\\\\\n & T_{milk,City,Pasture} \\geq 0\\\\\n & T_{cheese,City,Pasture} \\geq 0\\\\\n & T_{cheese,Pasture,City} \\geq 0\\\\\n\\end{aligned} $$\n\nwarning: Check the signs against `print`\nJuMP 1.32 drops the minus sign of a negative coefficient written in scientific notation when it typesets a model (JuMP.jl#4268). The two milk terms at the start of the objective above are such coefficients: both are negative, as print(jump_model(economy)) shows. Until the bug is fixed, read the signs from print.","title":"Looking before solving"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/minimal_market/#Reading-the-program","page":"Two regions and a dairy (minimal)","text":"There are four kinds of variable, and each is named after where it lives:\n\nD[region,product] is final demand, the quantity consumed: D[City,milk] is the milk the City drinks.\nS[region,product] is primary supply: S[Pasture,milk] is the milk the Pasture's herds give.\nz[region,process] is the activity level of a process in a region: z[Pasture,dairy] is how much the Pasture's dairy works, and z[City,dairy] how much the City's does. The first index is the location and the second names the process; neither is an input or an output. What the dairy takes and gives is in its recipe: each unit of activity takes 10 tonnes of milk and yields 1 tonne of cheese, so here one unit of activity is one tonne of cheese.\nT[product,from,to] is the quantity shipped on a route: T[milk,Pasture,City] is the milk carried from the Pasture to the City. There is one for each route declared in transport_costs, so four here.","title":"Reading the program"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/minimal_market/#Constraints","page":"Two regions and a dairy (minimal)","text":"The constraints are of two kinds. The first is a material balance, balance[region,product], for every product in every region: what is supplied, made and imported equals what is consumed, used up and exported.\n\nS_rp + sum_k y_kp z_rk + sum_o T_por\n  = D_rp + sum_k u_kp z_rk + sum_d T_prd\n\nk runs over the processes, and this economy has only one, dairy. For each process k and product p:\n\ny_kp is how much of p one unit of activity of k produces: its yield, from outputs;\nu_kp is how much of p one unit of activity of k uses up: its input requirement, from inputs.\n\nFor the dairy they are\n\nproduct y_textdairyp (out) u_textdairyp (in)\nmilk 0 10\ncheese 1 0\n\nso in region r, sum_k y_kp z_rk is everything the processes there make of p, and sum_k u_kp z_rk everything they use up of it. With one process each sum has a single term that is not zero, and the two balances of a region read\n\nmilk:    S + 0·z + imports = D + 10·z + exports\ncheese:  0 + 1·z + imports = D +  0·z + exports\n\nwhich is where the coefficients of z in the printed model come from: −10 in the milk balance of its region, +1 in the cheese balance.\n\nWhat is transformed is in neither S nor D. S is primary supply only, what enters from outside the modelled processes: the milk of the herds. D is final demand only, what leaves the economy to be consumed: the milk people drink and the cheese they eat. The milk the dairy takes is the u z term, and the cheese it makes the y z one.\n\nThat the balance is this simple comes from the dairy's recipe being fixed — a leontief input, ten tonnes of milk whatever the prices. Each u_kp is then a constant, and what a process uses is that constant times its activity. A process whose inputs substitute for each other chooses its mix as prices move, and the quantity it uses of each becomes a variable of its own; the forest example has those.\n\nThe dual value of each balance is the price of that product in that region.\n\nThe second kind of constraint is the bounds. S, z and T are only required to be non-negative. Each D is instead kept a little above zero, by a floor of a millionth of its own reference quantity — its q0, times its shift, which is 1 here:\n\nvariable q0 floor\nD[Pasture,milk] 200 0.0002\nD[City,milk] 600 0.0006\nD[Pasture,cheese] 30 0.00003\nD[City,cheese] 120 0.00012\n\nThe printed model shows them as 0.00019999999999999998 and the like, which is only floating point: a millionth of 200 has no exact binary form.\n\nThe floor is there because, with eta  1, the demand term of the objective below raises D to a negative power — D^-233 for milk, D^-1 for cheese — which is infinite at zero, and so is its slope. The solver evaluates both at every step it takes, so D must never get there, not even on the way to the answer. At a solution the floor never binds: as consumption shrinks towards zero its price grows without limit, and nobody buys so little. S needs no floor, because it is raised to a power above 1 (S^267), smooth at zero; nor do z and T, which enter only linearly.\n\nThe floor is per variable because what counts as close to zero depends on the quantity: a millionth of its own reference is negligible for each, whether a product trades in tens of tonnes or in millions. A single absolute floor would have to suit the smallest product, and would then be far smaller than needed for the largest. The qfloor keyword of jump_model and solve_market replaces them with one value for every variable.","title":"Constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/minimal_market/#The-objective","page":"Two regions and a dairy (minimal)","text":"What is maximised is the net social surplus: what consumers would pay for what they get, less what it costs to produce, make and carry. Written with its parameters rather than its numbers, it is\n\nmax_DSzT\n  sum_rp fraca_rp1 - 1eta_rp D_rp^1 - 1eta_rp\n  - sum_rp fracb_rp1 + 1varepsilon_rp S_rp^1 + 1varepsilon_rp\n  - sum_rk c_k z_rk\n  - sum_pod tau_pod T_pod\n\nEach of the four sums is one block of the objective printed above.\n\nGross consumer benefit. Each demand curve, with reference point (q_0 p_0), elasticity eta and shifter m, has the inverse P^D(D) = a D^-1eta, and the term is its antiderivative. Its slope at any D is the price consumers would pay for one more unit.\nCost of primary supply. Each supply curve, with elasticity varepsilon, has the inverse P^S(S) = b S^1varepsilon, and the term is the area under it: what the herds cost to keep, unit by unit.\nCost of making, c_k per unit of activity: the vacost of €1300 for each tonne the dairy makes, in either region.\nCost of carrying, tau per unit shipped: the €45 of each route.\n\nThe scales a and b are what put each curve through its reference point. The curve must give the price p_0 at the quantity m q_0, the reference quantity as shifted, so\n\na left(m q_0right)^-1eta = p_0\n  Rightarrow a = p_0 left(m q_0right)^1eta\nqquad\nb left(m q_0right)^1varepsilon = p_0\n  Rightarrow b = p_0 left(m q_0right)^-1varepsilon\n\nand the coefficient the printed objective shows in front of each power is that scale divided by the exponent. For milk in the Pasture, p_0 = 400, q_0 = 200, m = 1 and eta = 03 give a = 400 times 200^103 = 1871 times 10^10 and the exponent 1 - 103 = -233, so the coefficient is 1871 times 10^10  (-233) = -802 times 10^9. Every curve of the economy, worked out the same way:\n\nterm p_0 q_0 elasticity scale exponent coefficient\nD[Pasture,milk] 400 200 0.3 a = 1871 times 10^10 −2.33 -802 times 10^9\nD[City,milk] 450 600 0.3 a = 8198 times 10^11 −2.33 -351 times 10^11\nD[Pasture,cheese] 5300 30 0.5 a = 477 times 10^6 −1 -477 times 10^6\nD[City,cheese] 5350 120 0.5 a = 7704 times 10^7 −1 -7704 times 10^7\nS[Pasture,milk] 400 2000 0.6 b = 000126 2.67 0000472\nS[City,milk] 450 300 0.6 b = 00335 2.67 00126\n\nThe demand exponent and coefficient are both negative whenever eta  1, and the term still rises with D; for cheese, eta = 05 makes the exponent exactly −1. The sizes of the scales say nothing in themselves: a is a price times a quantity raised to 1eta, and is large because 1eta is.\n\nWhy maximising this gives the market is in the conditions for its optimum. With pi_rp the dual of each balance, they say that consumers buy until the price they would pay for one more unit, P^D(D), falls to pi_rp. Herds produce until their marginal cost P^S(S) rises to it. A dairy runs only where pi_rtextcheese = 10 pi_rtextmilk + 1300. A route is used only where the price gap across it is exactly tau. Those are the conditions of a competitive market, and the last two are checked by hand further down. The general program, with taxes, bounds and imperfect substitutes, is on the Code implementation page.","title":"The objective"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/minimal_market/#Solving","page":"Two regions and a dairy (minimal)","text":"result  = solve_market(economy)","title":"Solving"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/minimal_market/#What-comes-out","page":"Two regions and a dairy (minimal)","text":"Prices first. They are not assumptions: they are the shadow values of the material balances — what one more tonne of each product would be worth in each place.\n\nunstack(result.prices, :region, :product, :price)\n\u001b[1m2×3 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m cheese   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m milk     \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\n─────┼─────────────────────────────\n   1 │ City      5353.55   445.855\n   2 │ Pasture   5308.55   400.855\n\nThey come back within a euro or two of the points the curves were anchored on, because the data were mutually consistent to begin with. A consistent benchmark reproduces itself, which is all that Calibration means.\n\nThen what is made, what is used, and what moves:\n\nunstack(result.production, :region, :product, :quantity)\n\u001b[1m2×3 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m milk     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m cheese      \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64?    \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ Pasture  2002.56       149.936\n   2 │ City      298.339 \u001b[90m missing     \u001b[0m\n\nunstack(result.consumption, :region, :product, :quantity)\n\u001b[1m2×3 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m cheese   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m milk     \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\n─────┼─────────────────────────────\n   1 │ City     119.96     601.668\n   2 │ Pasture   29.9758   199.872\n\nresult.trade\n\u001b[1m2×6 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m product \u001b[0m\u001b[1m from    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m to     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m quantity \u001b[0m\u001b[1m capacity \u001b[0m\u001b[1m rent    \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\n─────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ cheese   Pasture  City     119.96        Inf      0.0\n   2 │ milk     Pasture  City     303.329       Inf      0.0\n\nThree things in those tables were never put in.\n\nThe milk splits itself. Of the 2301 tonnes the herds give, 801 are drunk and 1500 go to the dairy. Nothing in the data said how to divide them: the two uses bid against each other for the same milk, and the price that settles the contest is the one that makes every market balance at once.\n\nThe City makes no cheese at all — its entry in the production table is missing, meaning the dairy never runs there. Bringing in a tonne of cheese costs €45 of freight; bringing in the ten tonnes of milk behind it costs €450. So every cheese eaten in the City was made in the Pasture, and the model was not told that, it worked it out.\n\nMilk still crosses, but only the milk that is drunk. The City's own herds cover part of its thirst and the rest is carried in. Not one tonne of milk is carried in order to be made into cheese.","title":"What comes out"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/minimal_market/#Two-things-worth-checking-by-hand","page":"Two regions and a dairy (minimal)","text":"A model is worth no more than the conditions it satisfies, and in one this small each can be checked on a single line.\n\nThe dairy earns no profit. Where it runs, the cheese price must cover exactly ten tonnes of milk plus the €1300 — any more and another dairy would open, any less and this one would shut:\n\nprice(r, p) = only(result.prices[(result.prices.region .== r) .&\n                                 (result.prices.product .== p), :price])\n(cheese = price(\"Pasture\", \"cheese\"), milk_plus_making = 10 * price(\"Pasture\", \"milk\") + 1300)\n(cheese = 5308.545270473618, milk_plus_making = 5308.545270474511)\n\nCarrying earns no profit either. On a route that is used, the price gap between its two ends is exactly the freight — any wider and somebody would ship more until it closed. Both routes carry something here, so both gaps are €45:\n\n(milk   = price(\"City\", \"milk\")   - price(\"Pasture\", \"milk\"),\n cheese = price(\"City\", \"cheese\") - price(\"Pasture\", \"cheese\"))\n(milk = 44.99999999955929, cheese = 44.99999999888405)","title":"Two things worth checking by hand"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/minimal_market/#Asking-it-something","page":"Two regions and a dairy (minimal)","text":"The tables above are not the point — you could have measured those. The point is the question you cannot measure: what if something changed?\n\nSuppose the City takes to drinking milk, and wants a fifth more of it at every price. That is a shifter on the demand curve; the observed point the curve was fitted to stays exactly where it was.\n\nthirsty = [d.product == \"milk\" && d.region == \"City\" ?\n               DemandCurve(d; shift = 1.2) : d\n           for d in demand]\nthirst = solve_market(MarketData(; regions, products, demand = thirsty, supply,\n                                   processes, transport_costs))\n\nafter(r, p) = only(thirst.prices[(thirst.prices.region .== r) .&\n                                 (thirst.prices.product .== p), :price])\nDataFrame((region = r, product = p, before = round(price(r, p); digits = 1),\n           after  = round(after(r, p); digits = 1),\n           change_pct = round(100 * (after(r, p) / price(r, p) - 1); digits = 1))\n          for r in regions for p in products)\n\u001b[1m4×5 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m product \u001b[0m\u001b[1m before  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m after   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m change_pct \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64    \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ Pasture  milk       400.9    423.4         5.6\n   2 │ Pasture  cheese    5308.5   5534.0         4.2\n   3 │ City     milk       445.9    468.4         5.1\n   4 │ City     cheese    5353.5   5579.0         4.2\n\nNobody's taste for cheese changed, and yet cheese is dearer — in the Pasture as much as in the City. The two uses of milk compete for the same herds, so a City that drinks more leaves less to be made into cheese, and everyone who eats cheese pays for it. A model with one curve per product and no transformation tying them together could not have produced that at all: it is the dual use of milk, and nothing else, that carries the effect across.\n\nCheese also rises by much less, in percentage terms, than milk does — 4.2% against 5.6%. A good part of what a tonne of cheese costs is the making, and a thirstier City does not make a cheesemaker dearer.\n\nBehind the prices, the quantities tell the same story twice: the City drinks 602 tonnes of milk before and 711 after, while the dairy's output falls from 150 tonnes of cheese to 147. The extra glasses came out of the cheese.","title":"Asking it something"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/minimal_market/#Where-to-go-next","page":"Two regions and a dairy (minimal)","text":"Using the module for the rest of the data types: joint products, substitutable inputs, imperfect substitution between origins, bounds and taxes.\nThe forest sector: France in the world for the same four ideas at full size — eight regions, eleven products, and a storm.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/scope/#Accounts,-calibration-and-scope","page":"Accounts, calibration and scope","text":"","title":"Accounts, calibration and scope"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/scope/#What-the-model-does-not-constrain","page":"Accounts, calibration and scope","text":"Two limitations are inherent to the formulation rather than to the data, and both bear on how results should be read.\n\nProcessing location does not respond smoothly. Processes have constant returns to scale and a constant value-added cost, so a process has no supply response of its own: below a threshold set by freight it does not move at all, and above it the whole industry moves at once. Why processes, and not supply and demand per transformation measures this and explains where it bites — chiefly on products left homogeneous, since an Armington specification on the output restores a smooth response.\n\nOtherwise the remedy is to say what capacity exists. A Limit on the activity of a process bounds it, region by region if need be, and a binding bound earns a quasi-rent reported in Results.activity and Results.limits — what another unit of plant capacity would be worth there. Without such a bound, and without Armington on its output, read the geography of processing far more cautiously than that of prices or of final demand.\n\nPrices come from a continuous relaxation. All quantities are continuous, so the model knows nothing of indivisible investments, minimum efficient scale or shutdown decisions.\n\nLinear constraints on any of the model's quantities — a resource shared by several products, a quota, a minimum share — can be added with @limit without leaving the formulation: they keep the problem convex, and each comes back with its rent; see Limits on any quantity.","title":"What the model does not constrain"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/scope/#Physical-accounts","page":"Accounts, calibration and scope","text":"Every quantity the model solves for is physical — what each region produced, what it shipped to every other, what its households consumed, what its plants took in — and every balance between them is an equality. Two kinds of quantity are not sums, though, but CES aggregates:\n\nthe composite a region uses of an Armington product, made of the varieties of every origin;\nthe bundle a process uses from a ces nest, made of several inputs.\n\nA CES aggregate equals the sum of its members only at the mix it was calibrated at. Away from it, a mixed bundle counts for more, or less, than the physical quantity in it: a region's households and plants can draw a composite of 100 out of 104 m³ delivered, or a mill can turn 2.47 m³ of wood into a tonne of pellets where its recipe says 2.39. That is not an error of the solver but what a CES is — a production or preference function, with a gain from mixing built in — and it is the price of the smooth, scale-free substitution it gives.\n\nThe results therefore keep two accounts side by side:\n\nin composite units, the quantities the demand curves, the recipes and the prices refer to. This account is exact in value: the composite times the price its users pay is what was spent on the varieties, delivered.\nin physical units, the quantities that moved. The varieties a region received are its received, in Results.composites, and every use of the region's composite — final demand and each plant — is credited with the same physical content per unit, the ratio received / composite. That assumes every user in a region gets the same mix of origins, the one assumption the account makes. Final consumption has a physical column, process inputs are in Results.inputs, and Results.recipes sets the physical inputs of each nest against what its recipe requires: the gap is the quantity the aggregation used up or made up.\n\nWith them the physical account closes: production equals shipments, shipments equal receipts, receipts equal the physical uses, and a plant's physical inputs equal its recipe plus the gap. In the forest examples the gaps come to a few hundredths of a per cent of the world's wood, and up to a few per cent for one product in one region under a large shock — small, but worth reporting wherever the model feeds a physical balance, such as carbon or resource use.","title":"Physical accounts"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/scope/#What-it-would-take-to-make-the-composites-add-up","page":"Accounts, calibration and scope","text":"Where a physical identity has to hold exactly, there are exact aggregators: sigma = 0 (fixed proportions) and sigma = Inf (perfect substitutes, a plain sum), and, for inputs, a choice between alternative fixed recipes written as separate processes. Keeping imperfect substitution and an aggregate that is a plain sum is possible too, but each way of doing it gives up something else, and there are three properties to choose from:\n\n one convex program, prices as duals substitution depends on price ratios only the aggregate is a plain sum\nCES, as here ✓ ✓ \nlogit shares, with a variety term in the objective ✓  ✓\nadditive CES (van der Mensbrugghe & Peters, 2016)  ✓ ✓\n\nThe logit keeps the program and the plain sum, but its shares respond to price differences: doubling every price moves them, and users pay more per unit of the aggregate than was spent per unit. The additive CES keeps the plain sum and the ratios — its shares follow the usual CES rule, and the aggregate is priced at the average unit value — but it chooses its mix as if minimising the cost of a CES aggregate while counting the result as a sum, which no objective in the quantities alone describes; it belongs in an equilibrium model posed as a complementarity problem, the paradigm this package does not take. Its results also depend on how base-year values are split into prices and volumes, which those of the CES do not. Van der Mensbrugghe & Peters find the deviations of the standard form, for land in a global model, of 3.5% in the world and over 8% in some countries — the same order as here.","title":"What it would take to make the composites add up"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/scope/#Material-accounts","page":"Accounts, calibration and scope","text":"What the goods are made of — carbon, dry matter, a metal — can be traced through the same solution with material_accounts, following Lorang & Gerlagh (2026). A Material enters with primary supply at a given content per unit; it travels with the goods, each region's production carrying the content of what made it and its users the average content of what they received; and in a process it is either lost — burnt, emitted, discarded, at a given share — or carried into the outputs. How much of it a unit of a manufactured good carries is therefore not given but follows from the solution: a mill that changes its mix changes the content of what it makes, and the gap of the physical account becomes, in these terms, a change in the material intensity of the output rather than a quantity left unexplained.\n\nThese accounts close exactly — every region's material balance, and the world's: what was extracted was consumed or lost — and the consumed column is each region's footprint, the material embodied in its final consumption. They describe the solution and do not change it, as long as nothing in the model depends on the content of the goods. A tax per tonne of embodied material, or a cap on it, would make the intensities part of the equilibrium; that is not implemented.","title":"Material accounts"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/scope/#Calibration","page":"Accounts, calibration and scope","text":"A reasonable first reaction to all of this is: if I already know what was produced last year, and at what price, what is the model for?\n\nThe answer is that an observation is a point, and a point with an elasticity attached is a curve. The model never tells you the base year — you measured it. It tells you where the curves' intersection moves when something changes, and it does so for every product, region and process at once, with material balance, zero profit and no-arbitrage all holding simultaneously. That joint consistency is the thing no observation can give you, because it is a property of the system rather than of any one market.","title":"Calibration"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/scope/#A-consistent-benchmark-is-a-fixed-point","page":"Accounts, calibration and scope","text":"There is a sharp way to see what calibration does. If the data you anchor on are an equilibrium, the model reproduces them exactly and adds nothing:\n\nA  steel  price 182.3283 → 182.3283    relative difference 2.5e-13\nB  steel  price 190.3283 → 190.3283    relative difference 1.3e-13\nA  ore    price  63.9552 →  63.9552    relative difference 5.1e-13\n\nThat is benchmark replication, and reproducing it is the standard test that the data and the structure agree with each other.\n\nReal data are never an equilibrium — they carry measurement error, aggregation over products and periods, margins the model does not represent, and markets that were not clearing. Anchor one price 5% away from its consistent value and the model refuses to return it:\n\nA  steel  anchored at 191.445   model returns  183.64   gap 4.1%\nA  ore    anchored at  63.955   model returns   64.77   gap 1.3%\n\nIt splits the difference, because your numbers cannot all be true at once. That gap is not a nuisance: it is the model telling you so, and its size is a measure of how far your data are from being a coherent description of a market.","title":"A consistent benchmark is a fixed point"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/scope/#What-is-actually-calibrated","page":"Accounts, calibration and scope","text":"Entering p0 and q0 is not calibration, it is data entry. Calibration is choosing the parameters you do not observe so that the benchmark becomes an equilibrium:\n\nvacost of each process in each region, from the observed output prices less the observed cost of its inputs there — which makes zero profit hold exactly at base-year prices;\ntransport_costs on each route, from the observed price gap between its ends on routes that were actually used;\narmington_calibration for the trade shares and the scale of each composite, from the base-year bilateral matrix at base-year delivered prices, through every level of the origin groups — not from the raw observed shares, for the reason given under Imperfect substitution between origins (Armington); armington_shares does the shares alone for a flat CES;\nces_calibration for the shares of any other CES group — the substitutes in a DemandNest, the inputs of a ces nest — and for the p0 and q0 of a demand curve over a bundle, which are its CES price index and the base-year spending divided by that index;\nshift on the curves, to absorb what remains.\n\nThe forest examples do all of it from base-year data read from files: from partial, rounded statistics, where the model lands near the data rather than on them, and from complete, consistent data, where it returns them exactly.","title":"What is actually calibrated"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/scope/#Calibrating-with-partial-data","page":"Accounts, calibration and scope","text":"Data will be missing, and what is there will not quite agree. Neither stops a calibration, and neither matters as much as it seems, because scenario results are differences from the model's own reference run, never from the data. The question is not whether the reference run reproduces every observed number, but whether it is right about what the differences depend on.\n\nThat depends on the mechanics of the model:\n\nConstant elasticities give the same percentage response wherever the reference run sits. A curve shifted by 10% moves its price by the same percentage whether the price is 90 or 110, so much of a scenario is robust to a reference run that sits some way from the data.\nNothing protects a scenario from a reference run that is wrong about what the shock runs into. A capacity or a logistic limit that the reference run places nearer to binding than it was binds sooner, and changes the result in kind, not only in size. Market shares matter the same way — how strongly one region's price passes into another's is sigma times the share — and so do relative sizes, the margins of the processes, and where they run.\nThe detail of flows inside a group of regions matters little, as long as the totals around it are right: the group's trade with the outside, and each member's production and use.\n\nThe forest examples measure this on one economy and two scenarios. A reference run calibrated on partial data — every flow touching the regions of one country imputed from national totals — gives practically the results of one calibrated on complete data. One left uncalibrated, with a single price per product, trade shares from a rule and guessed costs, gets most of the results about right and doubles the main one, because it places a logistic limit closer to binding than the data do.\n\nThat says where to spend the effort:\n\nget right can be imputed or approximated\nproduction and use by region flows between regions of one country — impute_flows\ntrade at the level customs report it regional prices: the national price, or it adjusted by transport\na price level for each product, in each region where it differs the recipes of the processes: published conversion factors, one region's input mix\nthe constraints your scenarios will run into: capacities, logistic limits, policies consumer prices: derived from flows and producer prices\n\nimpute_flows fills in a trade matrix with the flows closest to a gravity pattern that meet every known total. It works best on the part that is actually unknown. When customs report a country's trade with each partner but not which of its regions it went through, impute only the country's corner of the matrix: its regions with their production and use, and each partner standing for its trade with the country as a whole — what it sent in, as an origin, and what it took out, as a destination:\n\n## for one product; `country` are the regions of the country, `partners` the rest\ncorner = impute_flows(\n    production = merge(Dict(r => production[r] for r in country),\n                       Dict(b => imports_from[b] for b in partners)),\n    use        = merge(Dict(r => use[r] for r in country),\n                       Dict(b => exports_to[b] for b in partners)),\n    distance = distance, decay = 1.4, home_bias = 3.0,\n    totals = Dict((partners, partners) => 0.0))   # their trade with each other is known\n\nThe rest of the matrix is what customs report between partners, and what each partner keeps of its own production; the whole goes to armington_calibration as if it had been observed. The second forest page does exactly this.\n\nInconsistent data need no special treatment: the reference run is their reconciliation, the one point where every balance holds, and its distance from the data says where the data disagree. Only two things are hard errors: a region exporting more than it produced, which leaves its own deliveries negative, and a process with a negative margin, which the model would close.","title":"Calibrating with partial data"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/scope/#Never-re-anchor-on-your-own-output","page":"Accounts, calibration and scope","text":"In a multi-period run the temptation is to re-base each period on the last: set q0 and p0 to the quantities and prices the model just produced, and carry on. Do not. Written out, that makes demand\n\nD_t = q_t-1left(fracP_tP_t-1right)^-eta\n\nwhich is precisely the lagged-ratio form this package avoids, arrived at from another direction. Its results depend on the path taken rather than the state reached, its errors compound, and the elasticity you estimated is no longer anchored at the level you estimated it for.\n\np0 and q0 stay at the calibration point for the whole run. Everything that moves between periods moves through shift and the limits, computed from state and from exogenous drivers. The discipline in one sentence:\n\nUse model output to update physical state, never behavioural parameters.\n\nUpdating a fish stock by stock + growth − catch is an accounting identity, and the catch may perfectly well come from the model. Updating q0 to last period's quantity is a silent re-estimation of behaviour from your own output, and is a different thing entirely.\n\nThe one re-anchoring that is legitimate is to new data: if observations for a later year arrive and you want to forecast from there, that is a fresh calibration against reality.","title":"Never re-anchor on your own output"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/scope/#Dynamics-are-somebody-else's-job","page":"Accounts, calibration and scope","text":"This package solves one equilibrium, at one point in time, and that is the whole of its remit. There is no time index anywhere in it and there will not be: a model that needs several periods runs solve_market once per period and carries the state between them itself. Keeping the boundary there is what lets the same engine sit under a fishery model with annual steps, a capacity-planning study with five-year ones, and a one-shot policy comparison with no time at all.\n\nWhat follows is therefore advice to whoever writes that loop, not a description of something Markets.jl does.\n\nThe approach worth taking is recursive dynamics: solve a full static equilibrium each period and link periods only through physical state, updated from the previous period's solution:\n\nresource stocks and their growth shift the primary supply curves;\ninstalled capacity bounds process activity;\nexogenous drivers (income, population, ...) shift the demand curves.\n\nDemand and supply remain level functions of price in every period, e.g. D_t(P) = alpha_t P^-eta, with the shifters of Everything other than the price carrying alpha_t. That is the intended seam: the loop updates shift and the limits between periods and never touches the fitted curves.\n\nThis deliberately avoids the form used in some recursive models, where demand in a period is a function of demand and price in the previous period, D_t  D_t-1 = (P_t  P_t-1)^eta. That form is a finite-difference proxy for an elasticity, so its results depend on the path taken and its errors compound across periods. (The ratio form is at least dimensionless, so the objection is about path dependence, not about units.) Anchoring each period on a static equilibrium with level functions keeps the dynamics interpretable.","title":"Dynamics are somebody else's job"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/scope/#Planned-extensions","page":"Accounts, calibration and scope","text":"These are things the engine itself might grow. A time loop is not among them, for the reason given under Dynamics are somebody else's job.\n\nArmington refinements: a separate composite per user (final demand and each process) instead of one per region, as GTAP does for the domestic-versus-imported margin; and a CET nest on the export side, so that redirecting output between destinations is costly for producers too, which is the mirror image of the import-side nest built here.\nAd valorem taxes without iteration, which would need the model restated as a complementarity problem rather than an optimisation.\nEstimation of the parameters from data.","title":"Planned extensions"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/processes/#Processes-and-substitution","page":"Processes and substitution","text":"","title":"Processes and substitution"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/processes/#Processes,-nests-and-input-substitution","page":"Processes and substitution","text":"A process is a transformation with constant returns to scale: per unit of activity it needs a fixed set of input requirements (its nests) and yields fixed quantities of one or more outputs.\n\nInputs in different nests are needed in fixed proportion to each other. If a process needs 1 unit of nest A and 0.2 units of nest B, it cannot use more of A to save on B.\nInputs in the same nest substitute for each other.","title":"Processes, nests and input substitution"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/processes/#Leontief-nests","page":"Processes and substitution","text":"A nest with a single product is a fixed coefficient (Leontief): exactly coeff units per unit of activity. Fixed proportions between several products are written as several Leontief nests.","title":"Leontief nests"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/processes/#CES-nests","page":"Processes and substitution","text":"A nest with several products is a constant-elasticity-of-substitution bundle, the function of Arrow, Chenery, Minhas & Solow (1961). The aim is smooth substitution: if one input becomes more expensive, the process uses proportionally less of it, rather than switching entirely from one input to another as a linear model with alternative processes would do. For instance, a steel mill that uses 30% scrap at equal prices may use 20% scrap when scrap becomes more expensive, and the share moves continuously with relative prices.\n\nThe cost-minimising mix satisfies\n\nfracq_iq_j = fracdelta_idelta_jleft(fracp_jp_iright)^sigma\n\nwhere:\n\nthe shares delta_i (normalised to sum to 1) fix the mix at equal input prices: at equal prices, input i makes up the fraction delta_i of the nest, both in quantity and in value. They are the calibration of where the substitution starts from. Without them all inputs would be used in equal amounts at equal prices.\nthe elasticity of substitution sigma fixes how fast the mix moves away from an input whose relative price rises. At 1 the mix is Cobb–Douglas (value shares stay constant), below 1 the inputs are poor substitutes, at 0 the mix does not move at all, and large values make the inputs nearly perfect substitutes.\n\nThe shares are written in calibrated form, so that the price of the CES composite equals the input price when all input prices are equal. As a result the equilibrium price of an output equals its true marginal cost of production.","title":"CES nests"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/processes/#Range-of-the-elasticity-of-substitution","page":"Processes and substitution","text":"sigma may be any value ge 0, here and in every other CES of the package — the Armington and OriginNest groups of origins and the DemandNest groups of final demand:\n\nsigma  1: good substitutes. When an input's relative price rises, its quantity falls by more than its price rose, so its value share falls.\nsigma = 1: Cobb–Douglas. Each input keeps the value share delta_i whatever the prices.\n0  sigma  1: poor substitutes, or complements. The quantity falls by less than the price rose, so the value share of the dearer input rises.\nsigma = 0: fixed proportions (Leontief). Input i is delta_i of the composite whatever the prices.\n\nThe aggregator is written with rho = (sigma - 1)sigma, which cannot be evaluated at sigma = 0 (rho = -infty), at sigma = 1 (rho = 0) or at sigma = infty. At those three values the engine writes its limit instead, and the price of the composite is the limit of the CES index:\n\nsigma composite of the q_i price of the composite\n0 min_i q_idelta_i sum_i delta_i p_i\n1 prod_i (q_idelta_i)^delta_i prod_i p_i^delta_i\ninfty sum_i q_i min_i p_i\n\nThese are the exact limits of the calibrated CES, so an elasticity just beside 0 or 1 gives nearly the same equilibrium as 0 or 1 itself. Every one of them is concave, so the program stays convex over the whole range.\n\nTwo practical points. Below 1 the exponent rho is negative, so q_i^rho grows without bound as an input goes to zero; the quantity floor keeps the solver away from zero, as it already does above 1. And very close to 0 — below about sigma = 001, depending on the data — q_i^rho leaves the range of floating-point numbers and the solve fails. Use sigma = 0 there: at sigma = 001 the equilibrium is already within a fraction of a percent of it.\n\nA nest of a single product has nothing to substitute and is a fixed coefficient whatever its sigma; leontief builds one and stores sigma = 0. A fixed-proportion requirement of several products is ces(...; sigma = 0).","title":"Range of the elasticity of substitution"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/processes/#Value-added-cost","page":"Processes and substitution","text":"vacost is the cost per unit of activity of everything not modelled as an explicit input: labour, energy, capital, chemicals. It is a constant, so these inputs have an exogenous price and cannot substitute for the modelled inputs. It may differ by region, as wages and energy do, and a vacost calibrated on base-year margins always will.\n\nAn input with an exogenous price that should substitute for the others — a chemical bought at a world price, say — is instead declared as a product with a flat supply curve, SupplyCurve(...; elasticity = Inf), in each region that uses it, and put in the nest beside them. Its price is then p0 whatever the quantity, and the quantity responds to the prices of the other inputs through the nest's sigma.","title":"Value-added cost"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/processes/#Why-processes,-and-not-supply-and-demand-per-transformation","page":"Processes and substitution","text":"There is another way to write the same economy. Instead of a transformation with fixed coefficients whose scale the material balances determine, each industry could carry its own estimated output supply and input demand functions — ideally derived from a profit function, so that Hotelling's lemma makes them consistent — and prices would become unknowns of a square system rather than duals of a balance. That is the behavioural or equation-system paradigm, and the choice between the two is old enough to have a standard reference in McCarl & Spreen (1980).\n\nWhich one a model uses is largely a matter of which literature it grew up in. Forest-sector, land-use and bioeconomy models are mostly process-based, as the review in Latta et al. (2013) shows; agricultural outlook and trade-policy models are mostly behavioural, as the classification in van Tongeren et al. (2001) shows; and the large models in both camps end up hybrids, because a behavioural model that does not tie its input demands to its output supplies by a fixed yield can imply impossible physical conversions.\n\nThis package takes the process route, and the reasons are worth stating plainly because they are also its limitations.\n\nWhat the choice buys. Every balance is an equality in physical units, and a recipe is a fixed yield: what goes into a plant comes out of it, as product or as residue, and no combination of estimated elasticities can violate that. The one qualification is aggregation: a CES bundle of inputs, or an Armington composite of origins, is an aggregate rather than a sum, and the physical account then needs one more line — see Physical accounts. The parameters are engineering coefficients, which exist for most transformations, where econometric estimates on intermediate markets usually do not. A technology with no history at all can be added by writing down its recipe. And because the problem stays a single convex optimisation, the equilibrium exists, the welfare measure means something, and one solve delivers both.\n\nWhat it costs. Constant returns to scale mean a process has no supply response of its own. At the zero-profit price it would run at any scale, so its level is pinned entirely by the balances around it — never by its own profitability. A transformation in this model does not decide how much to produce; the rest of the economy decides for it.","title":"Why processes, and not supply and demand per transformation"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/processes/#What-that-looks-like-in-practice","page":"Processes and substitution","text":"Sharper than \"the cheapest region takes everything\". Take two otherwise identical regions, each able to run the same process (ten units of input per unit of output, a value-added cost of 1300, freight of 5 on both products), and lower one region's value-added cost step by step:\n\ncost in A output in A output in B\n1300 79.8 79.8\n1247 80.4 79.6\n1245 104.6 55.4\n1243 160.0 0.0\n\nTwo failures, not one. Over the first 53 units of cost advantage — 4% — nothing happens at all; then the whole industry moves over a change of 4 units, or 0.3%. Processing location is a step function: numb, then total.\n\nThe width of the numb zone is not arbitrary. A local processor is protected by the freight on everything it would otherwise import, which is the freight on the inputs multiplied by the recipe coefficient, plus the freight on the output: 10 times 5 + 5 = 55 here, and the step duly falls at 1300 - 55. Raise the input freight to 60 and the protection becomes 605: a 46% cost advantage then buys nothing whatever. In a sector whose raw material is expensive to move and whose recipes take several units of input per unit of output — ore smelting, sugar refining, forestry — a model is normally deep inside the numb zone, so the practical symptom is inertia rather than volatility.","title":"What that looks like in practice"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/processes/#Why-this-is-usually-not-the-problem-it-appears-to-be","page":"Processes and substitution","text":"Because Armington restores the missing slope. If a product's regional varieties are imperfect substitutes, every region that makes it faces a downward-sloping demand for its own variety, and the step disappears. The same experiment, with sigma = 4 on the output:\n\ncost in A output in A output in B\n1255 80.3 79.6\n1243 80.4 79.6\n1200 81.8 78.4\n1100 85.7 75.2\n\nSmooth, and gentle: a 15% cost advantage moves the split from 50/50 to 53/47. The forest example declares an Armington specification for every product, so between its blocs it does not exhibit the behaviour above at all. Read the caveat as applying to products you have deliberately left homogeneous, which in practice means intermediates for which no trade shares were available, and groups of regions pooled as perfect substitutes — where, once costs are calibrated, it takes a sharper form still.","title":"Why this is usually not the problem it appears to be"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/processes/#Perfect-substitutes-and-constant-returns","page":"Processes and substitution","text":"Calibrating vacost on base-year margins puts every process that ran exactly at break-even, in every region. Between regions whose products are perfect substitutes — a homogeneous product, or an OriginNest with sigma = Inf — that turns the step above into a plateau: the location of processing inside the group is not decided at all.\n\nThe mechanism is simple. Take two regions of the group that both sell the input and the output into the same market — two regions of one country both exporting a raw material and the product made from it to the same neighbour, say. Move one unit of processing from the first to the second: the first exports the raw material it no longer processes, the second exports that much less, and the product changes hands the other way. Both swaps happen inside one market, at one delivered price, so the move costs exactly nothing, and so does the next one. Nothing in the model prefers one split to another; the solver stops wherever its path ends, and a cost difference of a hair moves the whole plateau. In a test on the forest economy of the examples with its French regions perfectly substitutable, a 0.1% change in the costs of the French mills emptied one region of sawmilling and nearly doubled it in another.\n\nWhether a base year sits on such a plateau depends on its trade pattern. When each region of the group sells its output to a different market, moving processing between them shifts quantities between markets, whose demands slope, and the split is pinned; when they share a market for both input and output, as regions of one country selling to their neighbours commonly do, it is not.\n\nThere are three ways out, in increasing order of what they ask for:\n\nA finite elasticity inside the group, however high. Each region's product then has a demand of its own, and the plateau tilts into a slope. The cost is that the group is no longer one market: products cross-haul between its regions, and producer prices may differ by more than the freight between them. The forest examples do this, with sigma = 30, and show both.\nA value-added cost rising with activity, the principled remedy described in the next section. Each region's mill then has a supply curve of its own, so the split is decided by costs rather than left open, and the group can stay perfectly substitutable.\nCapacities that bind at the base year. They pin the split, but only if set equal to observed output, which says every plant ran flat out — rarely true, and it forbids every plant from growing in any scenario. A capacity with room to spare leaves the plateau in place inside its bounds.","title":"Perfect substitutes and constant returns"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/processes/#The-fix-that-is-not-implemented,-and-why","page":"Processes and substitution","text":"The principled remedy, for the step and for the plateau alike, is not to abandon processes but to give them decreasing returns: replace the constant vacost with a cost rising in the activity level, calibrated so that the observed activity is what a processor would choose at observed margins. This is positive mathematical programming, the standard answer in agricultural sector models, and it would sit comfortably in this package — the objective term would take the same shape as the supply curves already do, the problem would stay convex, and an infinite elasticity would recover today's behaviour exactly. Between that limit and a Limit on the activity, which is the opposite limit of a perfectly inelastic process, the parameter would simply interpolate.\n\nIt is not implemented because of what it would need as data, not because of what it would need as code. Per process and per region it asks for a reference activity, a base-year margin — the difference between the output price and the input costs, which requires consistent price data on intermediate products in every region, the thinnest data in the sector — and an elasticity of processing supply, which is not an estimated quantity anywhere. Choosing it would mean replacing capacity bounds that were invented with an elasticity that was invented.\n\nThat trade may still be worth making, and for a model used to study where industry locates it probably is: a slope is a weaker assumption than a bound, it has an unambiguous direction, it can be swept in sensitivity analysis, and it makes the base year an interior optimum rather than a corner held in place by a constraint. But it should be made deliberately, and documented as a calibration choice rather than presented as measurement. Two details to get right if you do: the rising cost needs a positive intercept — an isoelastic cost alone makes the first unit free, so every region would produce a little of everything — and the inframarginal profit it creates has to enter the welfare measure, or welfare stops being comparable across model versions.\n\nA full switch to the behavioural paradigm is a different and much larger decision. Independently estimated response functions are generally not integrable, so no objective exists and the equilibrium has to be posed as a complementarity problem and solved as one — Mathiesen (1985), Rutherford (1995), Ferris & Pang (1997), and [C4] and [C5] for the tooling. That route would handle ad valorem wedges exactly instead of by iteration, and would accept asymmetric cross-price effects; it would also give up the guarantee of existence, the single welfare number, and the mass-balance consistency that is the main reason this package is built the way it is.","title":"The fix that is not implemented, and why"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/processes/#Joint-products-and-residues","page":"Processes and substitution","text":"A process can have several outputs. Residues (e.g. the slag of a blast furnace, or the fuel oil of a refinery) are ordinary products with their own material balance in each region, so they can be consumed, traded or used as inputs to other processes, and their price is determined by the competition between their uses.\n\nThe material balance is an equality: every unit of a by-product must be used, traded or consumed. There is no free disposal, and this has a consequence worth stating plainly. A by-product that nobody wants is a bad: somebody must be paid to take it, and its equilibrium price is negative. The model reports it that way, since the price is the signed dual of the balance.\n\nThat is the right answer to the question as posed, but it is rarely the question meant, and under a large supply shock it can take a local market somewhere surprising. If free disposal is what you want instead, give the by-product somewhere to go — disposal writes the process for you:\n\nprocesses = [refinery, steel_mill,\n             disposal(product = \"fuel_oil\"),          # free: price ≥ 0\n             disposal(product = \"slag\", cost = 12)]   # paid: price ≥ −12\n\nIts price then settles at zero rather than going negative, and a positive cost represents a real disposal charge — a landfill fee, an incinerator, a tailings dam. Deciding which by-products have a way out is a modelling choice the model cannot make for you, which is why there is no default. Writing the balances as inequalities, as a mixed-complementarity formulation would, is the other route to the same thing; it is not what this package does.","title":"Joint products and residues"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/processes/#Substitution-in-final-demand","page":"Processes and substitution","text":"Final demands are independent of each other by default: each product's curve responds to its own price alone. That is often too strong — butter and margarine go on the same bread, petrol and diesel cars do the same job on the road — and the question is how to let one product's price move another's demand.\n\nThe tempting answer, a matrix of cross-price elasticities, is not available, and for the same reason an arbitrary matrix of Armington elasticities is not. Demand here is the gradient of the consumer-benefit term of the objective; that is what makes the surplus-maximisation equivalence hold in the first place. A demand system that is not the gradient of anything — an arbitrary matrix is not, unless it satisfies the symmetry conditions — has no benefit function behind it, and the program it would be put into would no longer be the market it claims to represent.\n\nWhat is available is the same answer as for origins: a tree, through DemandNest. Products that substitute closely sit in a group with a high sigma; the group as a whole substitutes against the rest at the lower sigma of the level above; and the whole bundle faces one DemandCurve curve, which describes the composite. So\n\nDemandCurve(region = \"EU\", p0 = 30000, q0 = 12, elasticity = 0.5,\n           product = DemandNest(sigma = 1.6, products = [\"electric_car\"], shares = [0.25],\n                       nests = [DemandNest(sigma = 5.0, share = 0.75,\n                                           products = [\"petrol_car\", \"diesel_car\"],\n                                           shares   = [0.65, 0.35])]))\n\nsays that petrol and diesel cars are close substitutes, that an electric car is a more distant substitute for either, and that cars as a whole have an own-price elasticity of 0.5.\n\nThe cross-price effects this implies are the CES ones: within a group, the compensated cross-price elasticity of product i with respect to the price of j is sigma s_j, with s_j the value share of j. A large supplier therefore transmits its price more strongly than a small one at the same sigma — the shares matter as much as the elasticity, exactly as they do between origins.","title":"Substitution in final demand"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/processes/#Substitutes-outside-the-model","page":"Processes and substitution","text":"Cars compete with public transport, whose fares this model does not determine. That needs none of the machinery above: an exogenous price is a constant, so its effect on demand is a shift of the whole curve. Put the cross-price elasticity in exogenous_shift as the driver elasticity:\n\nDemandCurve(product = cars, region = \"EU\", p0 = ..., q0 = ...,\n           elasticity = 0.4,\n           shift = exogenous_shift(fares = (level = 3.2, reference = 2.0,\n                                            elasticity = 0.35)))\n\nFares 60% dearer than at calibration, with a cross-price elasticity of 0.35, raise demand for the car bundle by 16^035 approx 118. The endogenous substitution inside the bundle and the exogenous substitution against public transport are different mechanisms, and it is worth keeping them visibly apart.","title":"Substitutes outside the model"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Using-the-module","page":"Using the module","text":"Using Markets.jl takes three steps:\n\nDescribe the economy with the data types DemandCurve, SupplyCurve, Process (built from leontief and ces nests), collected in a MarketData.\nSolve it with solve_market.\nRead the results from the DataFrames in the returned Results.\n\nThe engine never hard-codes a product, process or region name: everything specific to a sector lives in the data.\n\nAll the types and builders below take keyword arguments only, so every number in a model description says what it is. Optional arguments have defaults; the others must be given.","title":"Using the module"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Describing-an-economy","page":"Using the module","text":"","title":"Describing an economy"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Regions-and-products","page":"Using the module","text":"Regions and products are named with strings:\n\nregions  = [\"EU\", \"US\", \"CN\"]\nproducts = [\"crude\", \"gasoline\", \"diesel\", \"fuel_oil\", \"scrap\", \"pig_iron\", \"steel\"]\n\nA name may be written as a Symbol instead — :EU is accepted wherever \"EU\" is — but it is converted on the way in, so what is stored, compared and reported back is always the string. The two spellings may be mixed within one economy, and a field read off a specification compares to the string that built it:\n\nSupplyCurve(product = :crude, region = :EU, p0 = 550, q0 = 60, elasticity = 0.4).region == \"EU\"\n\nEvery product gets a material balance in every region that makes, uses or carries it. A product declared but touched by nothing is allowed, and simply has no price anywhere.\n\nDemandCurve and SupplyCurve below are the two sides of one type, Curve{:demand} and Curve{:supply}: the same fields, the same two ways of giving the curve, and the same shifter, with the sign of the elasticity the only difference between them.","title":"Regions and products"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Final-demand","page":"Using the module","text":"A DemandCurve gives a constant-elasticity demand curve for one product in one region, calibrated to pass through a reference point: at price p0 the quantity demanded is q0. elasticity is the own-price elasticity eta, which need only be positive — inelastic demand, eta  1, is allowed and is what most fuels, foods and basic materials call for:\n\nDemandCurve(product = \"gasoline\", region = \"EU\", p0 = 900, q0 = 80, elasticity = 0.3)\n\nWith eta le 1 the objective value is no longer an absolute measure of welfare, only a basis for comparing scenarios; quantities and prices are unaffected. See Demand and supply curves.\n\nOnly products with a DemandCurve in a region are consumed there; other products are intermediates in that region.","title":"Final demand"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Products-that-substitute-for-each-other","page":"Using the module","text":"Give product a DemandNest instead of a single name and the curve describes a group of substitutes: the p0, q0 and elasticity then belong to the composite, while the nest says how its members trade off against each other.\n\nDemandCurve(region = \"EU\", p0 = 30000, q0 = 12, elasticity = 0.5,\n           product = DemandNest(sigma = 1.6, products = [\"electric_car\"], shares = [0.25],\n                       nests = [DemandNest(sigma = 5.0, share = 0.75,\n                                           products = [\"petrol_car\", \"diesel_car\"],\n                                           shares   = [0.65, 0.35])]))\n\nPetrol and diesel cars substitute readily here, electric cars less so with either.\n\nThe data seldom come in that form: they give a price and a quantity for each product, not a share for each and a reference point for the bundle. ces_calibration converts the one into the other, so that at the base-year prices the model buys exactly the base-year quantities:\n\nfuel = ces_calibration(quantities = [7.0, 1.4], prices = [55.0, 250.0], sigma = 3)\nDemandCurve(region = \"FR\", p0 = fuel.price, q0 = fuel.quantity, elasticity = 0.35,\n           product = DemandNest(sigma = 3, products = [\"firewood\", \"pellets\"],\n                                shares = fuel.shares))\n\nfuel.shares are the shares at equal prices, fuel.price is the CES price index of the bundle and fuel.quantity the base-year spending divided by it. A nested bundle is calibrated from the inside out: the price and quantity of an inner group make it one member of the level above. The forest example does this for wood in construction. The structure is a tree for the same reason the Armington one is: a free matrix of cross-price elasticities is not the gradient of any benefit function, and the model would stop being the market it claims to be. See Substitution in final demand.\n\nSubstitutes outside the model — public transport against cars — need no nest at all. An exogenous price is a constant, so put the cross-price elasticity into shift through exogenous_shift.\n\nEverything that moves demand other than its own price — income, population, the price of a substitute outside the model — goes in shift, which scales the whole curve and leaves p0 and q0 holding the calibration:\n\nDemandCurve(product = \"electricity\", region = \"EU\", p0 = 90, q0 = 2700, elasticity = 0.3,\n           shift = exogenous_shift(income = (level = 112.0, reference = 100.0,\n                                             elasticity = 0.8)))","title":"Products that substitute for each other"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Primary-supply","page":"Using the module","text":"A SupplyCurve gives a constant-elasticity supply curve for a product that enters the economy from outside the modelled processes — crude oil from a field, ore from a mine, fish from the sea, crops from land:\n\nSupplyCurve(product = \"crude\", region = \"US\", p0 = 550, q0 = 600, elasticity = 0.4)\n\nA further field, shift, carries the conditions of production: it scales the curve, exactly as for demand. The size of the resource, the roads and ports, the workers and machines available all belong here, and exogenous_shift turns driver levels and their elasticities into the multiplier. A hard ceiling that no price can pass — a fishing quota, a mining licence, an exhausted resource — is a Limit on the supply instead: it truncates the curve rather than scaling it, and the price then rises above the marginal cost on the curve by the scarcity rent of the resource.\n\nsupply = [SupplyCurve(product = \"cod\", region = \"EU\", p0 = 3000, q0 = 300, elasticity = 0.40,\n                     shift = exogenous_shift(stock = (level = 118.0, reference = 100.0,\n                                                      elasticity = 0.6)))]\nlimits = [@limit \"cod quota\" supply(region = \"EU\", product = \"cod\") <= 350]\n\nWhich of the two to use for which driver, and when to model the factors explicitly instead, is set out in Everything other than the price.","title":"Primary supply"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Straight-lines-and-flat-curves","page":"Using the module","text":"Demand and supply curves have constant elasticity by default. Either can be a straight line instead, given in one of two ways:\n\n# through a reference point, with the elasticity there: the tangent line\nDemandCurve(product = \"wheat\", region = \"EG\", p0 = 88, q0 = 7634, elasticity = 0.55,\n           form = :linear)\n# by its coefficients, D = alpha + beta·p and S = alpha + beta·p\nDemandCurve(product = \"wheat\", region = \"A\", alpha = 200, beta = -9)\nSupplyCurve(product = \"wheat\", region = \"A\", alpha = -50, beta = 10.5)\n\nThe coefficients carry their signs as a regression would estimate them: beta < 0 for demand, beta > 0 for supply, and for demand alpha > 0, the quantity demanded at a zero price. A supply alpha may have either sign: negative, nothing is supplied below the price -alpha/beta. Giving both a reference point and coefficients is an error, as is giving neither.\n\nIn either form, shift multiplies the quantity at every price, and exogenous_shift builds it from drivers as before. On a line it scales alpha and beta together, so the price at which the line meets zero stays put. A line estimated in levels, Q = alpha + beta·p + gamma·Z, carries its drivers in the intercept instead: give the moved line its new alpha. The calibration helpers (ces_calibration, armington_calibration) return a reference point, which serves either form.\n\nA scenario moves a curve by giving it a new shifter, whatever its form:\n\nsalvage = SupplyCurve(logs; shift = 3.0)     # the same curve, three times the quantity\n\nAn elasticity of Inf makes the curve flat, in either form: a price given from outside, at which any quantity is bought or sold. It is how a small country meets the world market, the region \"world\" selling at the import price and buying at the export price:\n\nsupply = [SupplyCurve(product = \"wheat\", region = \"world\", p0 = 96.8, q0 = 1, elasticity = Inf)]\ndemand = [DemandCurve(product = \"wheat\", region = \"world\", p0 = 88.0, q0 = 1, elasticity = Inf)]\n\nq0 plays no role on a flat curve but must still be positive, and a flat curve refuses any shift but 1: there is no quantity to scale, and moving it means changing p0. The import price has to be above the export price, transport included, or buying from the world to sell back to it would be a profit without end, and the market has no answer.","title":"Straight lines and flat curves"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Processes-and-input-nests","page":"Using the module","text":"A Process turns inputs into outputs at a level of activity z chosen by the model. It has:\n\na list of input nests. Each nest is one input requirement per unit of activity. Different nests are needed in fixed proportion to each other; products inside the same nest substitute for each other.\na list of outputs, as product => yield pairs per unit of activity. Several outputs make a joint-production process (a main product and its residues).\na value-added cost vacost per unit of activity, covering everything not modelled as an explicit input (labour, energy, capital, ...) — a single number for every region, or a region => cost dictionary covering each region the process operates in.\noptionally the regions where it may operate (default: all).\n\nThe installed capacity of the plants is a Limit on the activity, region by region:\n\nlimits = [@limit(\"steel plants $r\", activity(region = r, process = \"steel_mill\") <= cap)\n          for (r, cap) in installed]\n\nWithout one, a process with constant returns concentrates wherever production is cheapest; with one, the bound binds and earns a quasi-rent, reported in Results.activity as well as in Results.limits.\n\nThere are two kinds of nest:\n\nleontief: exactly coeff units of a single product per unit of activity.\nces: composite units of a CES bundle of several products. shares are the value shares of each input when all input prices are equal (equal shares if omitted; ces_calibration derives them from observed input quantities and prices), and sigma (≥ 0) is the elasticity of substitution: the higher it is, the more the mix moves away from the expensive input when relative prices change. sigma = 0 needs the inputs in fixed proportions, sigma = 1 is Cobb–Douglas, and sigma = Inf makes them perfect substitutes.\n\nFor example, a refinery that splits each tonne of crude into 0.45 t of petrol, 0.35 t of diesel and 0.15 t of fuel oil, and a steel mill that needs 1.1 t of a substitutable mix of scrap and pig iron per tonne of steel:\n\nProcess(name    = \"refinery\",\n        inputs  = [leontief(product = \"crude\", coeff = 1.0)],\n        outputs = [\"gasoline\" => 0.45, \"diesel\" => 0.35, \"fuel_oil\" => 0.15],\n        vacost  = 40)\n\nProcess(name    = \"steel_mill\",\n        inputs  = [ces(composite = 1.10,\n                       products  = [\"scrap\", \"pig_iron\"],\n                       shares    = [0.4, 0.6],\n                       sigma     = 2.0)],\n        outputs = [\"steel\" => 1.0],\n        vacost  = 150)\n\nA process with two nests, e.g.\n\ninputs = [ces(composite = 1.0, products = [\"a\", \"b\"], shares = [0.5, 0.5], sigma = 2),\n          leontief(product = \"c\", coeff = 0.2)]\n\nneeds one unit of the a/b bundle and 0.2 units of c per unit of activity.","title":"Processes and input nests"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Trade","page":"Using the module","text":"transport_costs maps each allowed route (product, from, to) to a unit transport cost, per unit of quantity shipped. It is also the whole declaration of trade: a route missing from it cannot be used, and a product with no route anywhere is simply not traded, so there is no separate list of tradable products to keep in step with it. Routes are directional, which is what lets a corridor cost more one way than the other, so a product that moves both ways needs an entry each way.\n\ntransport_costs = Dict((\"crude\", \"US\", \"EU\") => 25.0, (\"crude\", \"EU\", \"US\") => 25.0, ...)\n\nA free route is written => 0.0, not omitted.\n\nWhat may actually move is capped by a Limit on the flows: the capacity of a port or a corridor, or a quota. It can cap a single product on a single route or everything crossing a border, and weights convert the quantities into whatever unit the ceiling is written in:\n\nlimits = [@limit(\"steel quota\", flow(product = \"steel\", from = \"CN\", to = \"EU\") <= 8.0),\n          @limit(\"container port\", 2.0 * exports(region = \"CN\", product = \"cars\") +\n                                   0.04 * exports(region = \"CN\", product = \"wine\") <= 6.0)]\n\nA binding ceiling shows up in Results.limits with its congestion rent, which every flow it covers carries in Results.trade, and the price gap across the route it throttles becomes transport plus that rent.\n\nBy default a traded product is homogeneous: the same good wherever it comes from. Regional prices then differ by at most the transport cost, and two regions never ship it to each other both ways; a region on the cheapest path between two others may carry it through, importing and exporting the same quantity.","title":"Trade"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Imperfect-substitution-between-origins-(optional)","page":"Using the module","text":"Listing a product in Armington makes the varieties of the different origins imperfect substitutes instead: each region uses a CES composite of them, so it can import and export the same product at once and its price responds to supply and demand everywhere rather than only to the cheapest source.\n\narmington = [Armington(product = \"wine\", sigma = 4),              # equal shares\n             Armington(product = \"cars\", sigma = 6,\n                       shares = Dict((\"EU\", \"EU\") => 0.7, (\"US\", \"EU\") => 0.2, (\"CN\", \"EU\") => 0.1,\n                                     (\"US\", \"US\") => 0.8, (\"EU\", \"US\") => 0.1, (\"CN\", \"US\") => 0.1,\n                                     (\"CN\", \"CN\") => 0.9, (\"EU\", \"CN\") => 0.05, (\"US\", \"CN\") => 0.05))]\n\nsigma is the elasticity of substitution between origins, any value ≥ 0: the lower it is, the more buyers stick to their usual origin when prices move. Below 1 the varieties are poor substitutes, or complements; sigma = 1 is Cobb–Douglas (each origin keeps a fixed share of the spending) and sigma = 0 fixes the mix of origins whatever the prices.\nshares are keyed (origin, destination) and are the shares that would be observed if all delivered prices were equal, so they carry the home bias. They are not the observed shares: calibrate them from a base-year trade matrix with armington_shares, which inverts the CES demand condition for you — it is ces_calibration applied to each destination.\nshares = armington_shares(flows = base_year_quantities,   # (origin, dest) => quantity\n                          prices = delivered_prices,      # (origin, dest) => price\n                          sigma = 4)\nIf omitted, every origin available to a destination gets an equal share. An origin with no share (or a share of 0) is left out of that destination's composite, and so can never gain trade later.\nWith origin groups (below), armington_calibration builds the whole specification from the same two dictionaries — shares through every level of the tree, and the scale that keeps each composite in the physical units of the flows at the base year, so that base-year data balance exactly. Where flows are missing — between the regions of one country, typically — impute_flows fills them in from production, use and whatever totals are known; see Calibrating with partial data.\nsigma = Inf, and any product not listed, is the homogeneous case above. Inside an OriginNest it instead pools that group of origins into a single good, which is how a set of regions that form one internal market — the regions of a country, say — enters a world model as one variety.\n\nWith an Armington product, local producers and local users no longer face the same price, and both are reported — see Reading the results.\n\ntip: Shares or elasticity?\nUse the shares to say how much of an origin a market takes when prices are equal — that is where a home bias, or a regulatory penalty against an origin, belongs. Use sigma to say how readily buyers switch when prices move. A low sigma is not a barrier: it means buyers cannot get away from that origin, so an expensive one keeps its share rather than losing it.","title":"Imperfect substitution between origins (optional)"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Origins-that-are-closer-substitutes-than-others","page":"Using the module","text":"A single sigma makes every origin substitute equally well for every other. When some origins are near-interchangeable — shared grades, standards or certification — and others are not, group them in an OriginNest with its own, higher elasticity:\n\narmington = [Armington(product = \"cars\", sigma = 2.5,\n                       nests = [OriginNest(sigma = 12, origins = [\"EU\", \"US\"])],\n                       shares = shares)]\n\nBuyers then swap EU for US cars readily (σ = 12) and replace either with Chinese cars only slowly (σ = 2.5). Origins left out of every group are direct members of the composite, groups may contain groups, and a group must be at least as substitutable inside as it is with the outside — the engine rejects the reverse, which would be inconsistent.","title":"Origins that are closer substitutes than others"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Different-structures-in-different-markets","page":"Using the module","text":"Standards and non-tariff measures are set by the importer, so they are asymmetric. Give a specification a destination to apply it to those markets only, and keep one with destination = : (every destination, the default) as the fallback:\n\narmington = [\n    # the EU market keeps Chinese cars at arm's length, EU and US are alike\n    Armington(product = \"cars\", destination = \"EU\", sigma = 2.5,\n              nests = [OriginNest(sigma = 12, origins = [\"EU\", \"US\"])], shares = shares),\n    # every other market substitutes freely between all three origins\n    Armington(product = \"cars\", sigma = 8, shares = shares)]\n\nThe elasticity, the groups and the shares may all differ by destination. What may not differ is whether the product is Armington at all: it has one variety per origin everywhere, or none anywhere.","title":"Different structures in different markets"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Putting-it-together","page":"Using the module","text":"market = MarketData(regions   = regions,\n                    products  = products,\n                    demand    = demand,\n                    supply    = supply,\n                    processes = processes,\n                    transport_costs = transport_costs)\n\nOnly regions and products are required; demand, supply, processes and transport_costs all default to empty. When your variables already carry the field names, Julia's shorthand saves the repetition:\n\nmarket = MarketData(; regions, products, demand, supply, processes, transport_costs)","title":"Putting it together"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Taxes-and-subsidies","page":"Using the module","text":"A Tax drives a wedge between what one side pays and what the other receives, on production, on consumption, on a transformation or on a trade route. rate is an amount per unit, or a fraction of the base when relative = true; a subsidy is a negative rate.\n\ntaxes = [Tax(name = \"oil_royalty\",  on = \"supply\",  product = \"crude\", region = \"US\", rate = 4.0),\n         Tax(name = \"fuel_vat\",     on = \"demand\",  product = \"gasoline\", rate = 0.2,\n             relative = true),\n         Tax(name = \"green_steel\",  on = \"process\", process = \"steel_mill\", region = \"EU\",\n             rate = -15.0),\n         Tax(name = \"steel_tariff\", on = \"trade\",   product = \"steel\", to = \"US\", rate = 0.25,\n             relative = true)]\n\nSelectors left at : — the colon, the default — match everything, and several taxes may fall on one base. Results.taxes reports what each raises. A taxed objective counts the tax as a cost rather than a transfer and so is no longer welfare; Results.welfare is. See Taxes, subsidies and other wedges.","title":"Taxes and subsidies"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Limits-on-any-quantity","page":"Using the module","text":"A Limit is a linear constraint on the quantities of the model: a resource that several products share, a quota, a minimum, a share of one quantity in another, a fixed sum. @limit writes it as the inequality it stands for:\n\nlimits = [@limit(\"stands of SEF\", supply(region = \"SEF\", product = \"logs\") +\n                                  supply(region = \"SEF\", product = \"firewood\") <= 12.4),\n          @limit(\"milled at home\", activity(region = \"SWF\", process = \"sawmill\") >=\n                                   0.5 * supply(region = \"SWF\", product = \"logs\")),\n          @limit(\"import share\", imports(region = \"OF\", product = \"lumber\") <=\n                                 consumption(region = \"OF\", product = \"lumber\") / 3),\n          @limit(\"export band\", 1.0 <= exports(region = \"RW\", product = \"logs\") <= 3.0),\n          @limit(\"fixed quota\", flow(product = \"logs\", from = \"RW\", to = \"EU\") == 2.0)]\n\nmarket = MarketData(; regions, products, demand, supply, processes, transport_costs, limits)\n\nThe quantities are named by eight term builders, each summing over the regions, products and processes it selects — a name, a vector of names, or the colon : for every one, which is also what a selector left out means:\n\nBuilder Sums\nsupply(; region, product) primary supply\nconsumption(; region, product) final demand\nproduction(; region, product) primary supply plus process outputs\nactivity(; region, process) process activity levels\ninputs(; region, process, product) what processes draw of their inputs\nflow(; product, from, to) shipments between regions\nexports(; region, product) shipments leaving the region, or the group of regions; every flow when no region is named\nimports(; region, product) shipments entering it; every flow when no region is named\n\nQuantities may be added, subtracted, multiplied or divided by numbers, put on either side of the comparison, and mixed with constants, which end up in the bound. lower <= expression <= upper sets both bounds at once, and == fixes the expression. Anything else — the product of two quantities, a quantity in a denominator, a power, sqrt — is refused when the limit is written, because it would no longer be linear: a linear limit keeps the problem convex, so the equilibrium is still the solution of one optimisation and its rent has a clear meaning.\n\nA selector is the same everywhere in the package — in a Tax, in a Process's regions, in an Armington destination — and no name in it is special: a region or a product may be called all and is then selected by that name. Every one is :, as in indexing an array:\n\n@limit \"world softwood\" supply(region = :, product = \"swr\") <= 900\n\nA group of regions or products is a vector you build yourself — france = [\"SEF\", \"SWF\", \"GEF\", \"OF\"] — and supply(region = france, product = \"swr\") sums over its members. For a group, exports and imports count only what crosses its border, while flow counts every flow between the regions selected.","title":"Limits on any quantity"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Families-of-limits","page":"Using the module","text":"Where GAMS has indexed equations and JuMP families of constraints, @limits writes one limit per combination of its indices, each named after them:\n\n@limits \"cut\" [r in france] supply(region = r, product = \"swr\") <= cap[r]\n## cut[SEF], cut[SWF], cut[GEF], cut[OF]\n\n@limits \"stands\" [r in regions, p in primary; haskey(stock, (r, p))] supply(region = r, product = p) <= stock[(r, p)]\n## stands[SEF, swr], stands[SEF, hwr], …, only for the pairs with a stock\n\nAn index is written i in I, i ∈ I or i = I; a condition after a semicolon keeps the combinations that meet it, and the last index varies fastest. The result is a plain vector of limits, so it is concatenated with others like any vector. A comprehension does the same with names of your choosing:\n\n[@limit(\"cut $r\", supply(region = r, product = \"swr\") <= cap[r]) for r in france]\n\nTo read a family back from the results, select its members by name: res.limits[startswith.(res.limits.limit, \"cut[\"), :].","title":"Families of limits"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Soft-limits","page":"Using the module","text":"A limit is hard: no price buys past it. Followed by penalty = p, it becomes soft, and can be crossed at a cost of p per unit beyond the bound — the way a sector model holds trade near its recent levels without forbidding a larger move when the market really calls for it:\n\n@limit \"pulp imports DE\" imports(region = \"DE\", product = \"pulp\") <= 1.1 * last_year penalty = 50\n@limits \"trade\" [p in products, r in regions] imports(region = r, product = p) <= 1.1 * last[(r, p)] penalty = 50\n\nBelow the bound a soft limit changes nothing. Past it, its rent is the penalty, which is the most a soft limit's rent can be, and Results.limits says by how much it was crossed in excess. A lower bound works the same way, on the shortfall. The problem stays convex: the crossing is a variable with a linear cost.\n\nThe penalty is a device of the model, not a cost anyone pays. The results report its total in penalties, and welfare leaves it out. A cost that is really paid past a threshold — overtime past a shift, an emergency import past a contract — belongs in the economy instead, as a second process or route at the higher cost. A soft limit on one variable is not a capacity, since it can be exceeded, so the capacity columns ignore it.","title":"Soft limits"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Writing-limits-by-hand","page":"Using the module","text":"Inside @limit the builders are those of Markets.jl, so a variable of your own called supply is not in the way; outside it, write them Markets.supply(…) and so on, and give the expression to Limit yourself:\n\nLimit(name = \"stands of SEF\", upper = 12.4,\n      expression = Markets.supply(region = \"SEF\", product = [\"logs\", \"firewood\"]))\n\nTwo cautions. A macro reads everything up to the end of the line, so several in one call need parentheses, as above. And consumption and inputs of an Armington product are measured, as the model measures them, in units of the region's composite, while flow, exports and imports are physical; a share that mixes the two is linear and valid, but slightly off physically (see Physical accounts).\n\nLimits are generated like any other data, in loops. A resource that several products share — stands that give both logs and firewood, say — is kept from being counted twice by bounding every combination of the products by the stock of everything at least one of them can come from, which is exactly what makes the harvests fit into the stock (Hall's theorem):\n\ncombinations(xs) = [xs[[isodd(i >> (j - 1)) for j in eachindex(xs)]] for i in 1:(2^length(xs) - 1)]\nlimits = [@limit(\"$r: \" * join(C, \" + \"),\n                 sum(supply(region = r, product = p) for p in C) <= available(r, C))\n          for r in regions for C in combinations(primary)]\n\nResults.limits reports each limit with its bounds, the value its expression takes, its rent — the gain in the objective from relaxing the bound that binds by one unit, positive for an upper bound and negative for a lower one, zero for a limit that does not bind — and the regions and model variables it applies to, which Markets.scope(limit, economy) also gives before anything is solved. The rent reaches the quantities too: the supply, activity and trade tables give each row the rent the limits it is part of charge per unit of it — the whole rent for a ceiling on that quantity alone, the rent times its weight for a share of a wider one — and the capacity of the tightest limit on that quantity alone. And for_region slices Results.limits too, keeping the limits that apply to the region, alone or with others.\n\nA limit that bounds one variable from above — a single supply, activity or flow — is set as a bound on that variable rather than as a constraint, which costs the solver nothing; any other limit is a constraint of the model. The forest example uses it for its stands.","title":"Writing limits by hand"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Looking-at-an-economy","page":"Using the module","text":"Typing the name of a MarketData in the REPL gives one line, such as MarketData(3 regions, 7 products, 4 processes), as do repr and a vector or dictionary holding it. Printing it, with print(market) or by interpolating it into a string, gives a summary: what is supplied, consumed, made and used of each product, the processes in the order they chain into one another (stage 1 uses only primary products, stage 2 also the outputs of stage 1, and so on), the trade routes and their costs, the Armington specifications, the trade limits and the taxes and subsidies. For a steel chain that ends in cars:\n\nTransformations, by stage\n  1  blast_furnace  1.6 iron_ore + 0.8 coal ───────→ pig_iron + 0.3 slag  cost 60\n  2  steel_mill     1.1 CES(scrap, pig_iron; σ 2) ─→ steel                cost 150\n  2  dispose_slag   slag ──────────────────────────→ (disposed of)        cost 5\n  3  car_plant      0.9 steel ─────────────────────→ cars                 cost 20000 · cap EU 14, US 13, CN 30\n\nThe mathematical program the economy becomes is returned, built but not solved, by jump_model:\n\nm = jump_model(market)      # the JuMP model solve_market would solve\nprint(m)                    # objective, balances, bounds, in full\nlatex_formulation(m)        # the same, in LaTeX\n\nlatex_formulation is JuMP's, re-exported so that it needs no using JuMP. In a notebook or on a documentation page it is typeset; in a terminal it prints the LaTeX source.\n\nwarning: Check the signs against `print`\nJuMP 1.32 drops the minus sign of a negative coefficient written in scientific notation when it typesets a model (JuMP.jl#4268). The benefit term of a demand with eta  1 has a negative coefficient, usually a large one, so in most models some objective terms are shown with the wrong sign. print(m) is not affected; read the signs from it until the bug is fixed.\n\nIts variables and constraints are named after what they stand for — D[EU,steel], S[US,crude], z[EU,steel_mill], balance[EU,steel], limit[container port] — and it takes the same solver, solver_options, silent and qfloor keywords as solve_market, so it can be modified and solved with JuMP directly. An ad valorem tax on trade is the one thing missing from it: solve_market finds its money equivalent by solving repeatedly, and the model returned is the first of those solves, with the tax at zero.","title":"Looking at an economy"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Solving","page":"Using the module","text":"res = solve_market(market)                       # Ipopt, silent\nres = solve_market(market; silent = false)       # show the Ipopt log\nres = solve_market(market; solver = MyOpt)       # any JuMP nonlinear solver\nres = solve_market(market; solver_options = (max_iter = 500, tol = 1e-9))\n\nsolver_options takes a NamedTuple, a Dict or a vector of pairs. A name is the solver's own name for the option, written as a symbol or a string, and an MOI attribute such as MOI.TimeLimitSec() is passed as it is. The options are set after the ones Markets chooses for Ipopt (see Numerical details), so they override them.\n\ntip: Large economies: a faster barrier strategy\nsolver_options = (mu_strategy = \"adaptive\",) lets Ipopt update its barrier parameter adaptively instead of monotonically. On the forest examples it roughly halves the iterations and the solve time (1693 → 783 iterations for forest_market), and the Egypt port solves in a third of the iterations, with the same equilibrium. It is not the default because it is less robust where the goods of a CES group are nearly perfect complements: with an elasticity of substitution of 0.01 between origins, Ipopt stops on an invalid number where the monotone strategy converges. If an adaptive solve fails, solve again without it.\n\nBefore building anything, solve_market calls validate on the economy. That check is not a formality: the model has one balance per declared region and product, so a name outside those lists would get variables and no balance — a mistyped process input would become a free input and the solver would report success. Every name, duplicate and selector is therefore checked first, and so is everything else that cannot be built as written: a tax or a limit that selects nothing, two Armington specifications for one destination. A problem is an ArgumentError saying which entry is at fault, and validate(economy) on its own gives the whole verdict.\n\nIf the solver then returns anything but an optimal or locally optimal solution, solve_market throws. An infeasible or unbounded economy comes back with tables that look exactly like those of a solved one, so returning them quietly is the more dangerous default. Pass on_failure = \"warn\" or \"ignore\" to get them anyway, and read res.status to see what happened.","title":"Solving"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Reading-the-results","page":"Using the module","text":"A Results carries the solver's verdict in status and its own message in raw_status, the solve_time in seconds and the iterations it took (summed over all solves when an ad valorem tariff is found by iteration, and missing for a solver that does not count them), the value of the maximised objective, the welfare — the same surplus with taxes counted as the transfers they are and without the penalties of soft limits — and twelve DataFrames:\n\nField Columns Content\nproduction region, product, quantity primary supply + manufactured output\nsupply region, product, quantity, capacity, rent primary supply alone, with the ceiling a limit puts on it and the rent the limits charge on it\nconsumption region, product, quantity, physical final demand, in the units of the demand curves and in physical units\ncomposites region, product, received, composite, ratio the composite each region uses of an Armington product, against the physical sum of the varieties it received\ninputs region, process, product, composite, physical what each process draws of each input\nrecipes region, process, inputs, recipe, physical, gap each input nest's physical inputs against its recipe\ntrade product, from, to, quantity, capacity, rent bilateral flows (positive only), with their ceiling and rent as for supply\nnet_trade region, product, exports, imports, net net = exports − imports\nprices region, product, price, producer_price what local users pay, and what local producers get\nactivity region, process, level, capacity, rent process activity, with its capacity and the quasi-rent it earns, as for supply\nlimits limit, lower, upper, penalty, value, excess, rent, regions, variables every Limit, how far a soft one was crossed, the rent of the bound that binds, and what it applies to\ntaxes tax, on, revenue what each instrument raises\n\nEither price column is missing where it has no meaning: a product that a region neither makes, uses nor carries has no price there, a region that cannot produce an Armington product has no producer_price for it, and a region that uses none of one has no price. Prices are signed, so a by-product that must be disposed of at a cost shows a negative one.\n\nprice and producer_price differ only for an Armington product, where local users buy a composite of all origins' varieties while local producers sell their own; for every other product the two columns are equal.\n\nquantity and physical differ only for an Armington product: its users draw on a CES composite of the varieties, which equals their physical sum only at the mix it was calibrated at. The last three tables keep the physical account of the economy, and gap is the quantity the CES aggregation used up or made up; see Physical accounts.\n\nfor_region slices all tables for one region at once. The input data and the underlying JuMP model are kept in res.data and res.model.","title":"Reading the results"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Tracing-a-material","page":"Using the module","text":"What the goods are made of — carbon, dry matter, a metal — is traced through a solved economy by material_accounts, given a Material: its content per unit of each primary product, the share each process loses, and, for a process with several outputs, how it divides the rest among them.\n\ncarbon = Material(name = \"carbon\",\n                  content = Dict(\"logs\" => 0.25),                  # per m³ harvested\n                  losses  = Dict(\"sawmill\" => 0.15, \"pulpmill\" => 0.5),\n                  allocation = Dict(\"sawmill\" => Dict(\"lumber\" => 1.0, \"chips\" => 1.0)))\nacc = material_accounts(res, carbon)\nacc.intensities   # content per unit of each region's production, and of what it uses\nacc.balances      # extracted, made, imported, exported, consumed, processed\nacc.processes     # what enters each process, what it loses, what it passes on\n\nThe content of a manufactured good is not given but follows from the solution, so the accounts close exactly, region by region and for the world: what was extracted was consumed or lost. The consumed column is each region's footprint. The accounts describe the equilibrium and do not change it; see Material accounts.","title":"Tracing a material"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Worked-examples","page":"Using the module","text":"Two regions and a dairy is the smallest complete model — two products, one process, one route, and milk that is both drunk and turned into cheese — and the place to start.\n\nThe forest example builds a complete economy with this API, over three pages. The first writes the model — eight regions (four of them French), the roundwood-to-paper-and-pellets chain, a product that is both burnt directly and milled, joint products, CES input bundles, a process restricted to the regions that have the capacity, and a three-level Armington structure in which the French regions are near-perfect substitutes for each other, close substitutes for the rest of the EU and more distant ones for the other blocs — and runs three scenarios on it, with uncalibrated numbers: a storm that floods one region with salvaged timber, a dearer gas, and a third of the French forests set aside. The second builds the same model from partial, inconsistent statistics read from CSV files, calibrating every parameter it does not observe or choose; the third from complete, consistent data, and compares the scenarios of the three.\n\nThe pages are generated from the scripts in examples/forest/, which are ordinary scripts: run them, in order, to get the same tables in a terminal.\n\njulia --project=docs examples/forest/forest_market.jl\n\nTwo more pages port models published with their code, from the GAMS model library, and check each port against the original. Takayama and Judge is the textbook spatial equilibrium with straight-line supply and demand, and Egyptian agriculture a sector model of 1980: crops as processes, land, water and labour by month as limits, livestock fed from fodder, and a small country trading at world prices. Its port is compared line by line with a JuMP transcription of the original.","title":"Worked examples"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#How-to-amend-an-economy","page":"Using the module","text":"Add a product: add its name to products; give it a DemandCurve and/or SupplyCurve where it is consumed or produced; wire it into processes; add transport_costs entries if it can be shipped.\nAdd or change a process: add a Process. Chains of any length are built by making the output of one process an input of another (e.g. iron ore → steel → cars). A product can be primary-supplied, manufactured, consumed as final demand and used as an input all at once — its balance simply sums every source against every use.\nAdd a region: add it to regions and give it demand, supply and transport data.\nChange substitutability between inputs: change the sigma of a CES nest. Values near 1 give a near Cobb–Douglas mix; large values make the inputs close to perfect substitutes.\nChange substitutability between origins: add or amend an Armington entry. Leave a product out of armington (or give it sigma = Inf) for the homogeneous, spatial-equilibrium treatment.\n\nNo change to the package code is needed for any of these.","title":"How to amend an economy"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Index","page":"API reference","text":"Pages = [\"api.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Public-API","page":"API reference","text":"The types and functions exported by the module: everything needed to describe an economy, solve it and read the results.","title":"Public API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Re-exported-from-JuMP","page":"API reference","text":"latex_formulation(model) typesets a JuMP model, such as the one jump_model returns, as LaTeX. It is JuMP's own function, re-exported unchanged, and is documented in JuMP's API reference. JuMP 1.32 drops the minus sign of some negative coefficients when it does this (JuMP.jl#4268), so read the signs from print(model).","title":"Re-exported from JuMP"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Internals","page":"API reference","text":"Not exported, and subject to change without notice.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#Markets","page":"API reference","text":"Markets\n\nSolve a spatial partial-equilibrium model of an economic sector made of an arbitrary set of products, regions and transformation processes.\n\nThe package is the engine only: it knows nothing about specific products. You describe an economy with the data types MarketData, DemandCurve, SupplyCurve and Process (built from leontief and ces input nests), hand it to solve_market, and get back production, consumption, trade and prices per region in a Results object.\n\nThe engine implements a spatial price equilibrium (Samuelson 1952 / Takayama & Judge 1971) solved as a net-social-surplus maximisation.  Under perfect competition this single optimisation is the market equilibrium:\n\nthe quantities (production, consumption, trade) are the primal solution;\nthe prices are the dual variables (shadow prices) of the per-region, per-product material-balance constraints.\n\nSmooth (CES) input substitution, multi-stage transformation with joint products and residues, and trade between non-identical regional markets (transport costs) all fall out of the same formulation.\n\nA complete example (the forest-products sector) is in examples/forest/.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.Armington","page":"API reference","text":"struct Armington\n\nImperfect substitution between the regional varieties of one product (Armington, 1969).\n\nBy default a product is homogeneous: it is the same good wherever it comes from, regional prices differ by at most the transport cost, and two regions never ship it to each other both ways (a region on the cheapest path between two others may carry it through). Listing a product here instead makes the varieties from the different origins imperfect substitutes: each region uses a CES composite of them, so a region can import and export the same product at once and its price responds to supply and demand everywhere, not only to the cost of the cheapest source.\n\nEach destination r draws on the varieties of the origins it can buy from: its own, plus every origin o with a transport route (product, o, r). The composite is\n\nA ≤ ( Σₒ δₒ^(1/σ) · Xₒ^ρ )^(1/ρ),     ρ = (σ-1)/σ\n\nwith Xₒ the quantity bought from origin o and δₒ its value share. The price a region pays for the composite is then the CES price index of the delivered prices,\n\nPᶜ = ( Σₒ δₒ · (Pₒ + τₒ)^(1-σ) )^(1/(1-σ))\n\nA scale φ multiplies the aggregate, and divides this price, by the same factor.\n\nwhich tends to the cheapest delivered price as σ → ∞: sigma = Inf is exactly the homogeneous (Samuelson spatial price equilibrium) case, and a large finite sigma approaches it. At the other end, sigma = 1 is the Cobb–Douglas composite Πₒ (Xₒ/δₒ)^δₒ, priced at Πₒ (Pₒ + τₒ)^δₒ, and sigma = 0 the Leontief one minₒ Xₒ/δₒ, priced at Σₒ δₒ (Pₒ + τₒ).\n\nA single sigma makes every origin substitute equally well for every other, which is the defining restriction of a flat CES. Two ways out, both available here:\n\nGroups of origins (OriginNest): origins whose products are mutually interchangeable are nested together with a higher sigma, and substitute with the rest at the lower sigma of this specification.\nOne specification per destination (destination): the structure may differ from one importing market to the next, which is what technical standards and non-tariff measures actually do, since the importer sets them.\n\nFields\n\nproduct::String: Product whose regional varieties are imperfect substitutes\nsigma::Float64: Elasticity of substitution sigma between the varieties of the different origins. It must be ≥ 0. Below 1 the varieties are poor substitutes, or complements; 0 makes each destination buy them in fixed proportions (Leontief) and 1 is Cobb–Douglas. Inf (the default for every product not listed) means perfect substitutes, i.e. a homogeneous product\n\nshares::Dict{Tuple{String, String}, Float64}: Value shares delta of each origin in each destination's composite, keyed (origin, destination) — the shares that would be observed if the delivered prices of all origins were equal. They are normalised per destination. An origin with no share given (or a share of 0) is left out of that destination's composite. When this is empty, every origin available to a destination gets an equal share\n\ndestination::Union{Colon, String, Vector{String}}: Importing regions this specification applies to, or : (the default) for every region without one of their own. A product may have one specification per destination plus a : fallback, which is how the structure, the elasticity and the shares can differ between importing markets\n\nnests::Vector{OriginNest}: Groups of origins that substitute for each other more closely than for the rest, as a vector of OriginNests. Every available origin not placed in a group stays a direct member of the composite. Empty (the default) gives the flat CES in which all origins substitute equally\n\nscale::Union{Float64, Dict{String, Float64}}: Scale phi of the composite, one number for every destination or a destination => scale dictionary (a destination absent from it gets 1). With shares that sum to 1 the CES aggregate equals the plain sum of the varieties bought only where their delivered prices are equal, and falls short of it where they differ. A scale of the base-year sum over the base-year aggregate makes the composite count physical units again at the base year, at a price equal to the average delivered unit value, which is what lets base-year data in m³ or tonnes balance exactly; armington_calibration sets it. The default, 1, leaves the aggregate as it is\n\nExample\n\n# moderate substitution, equal shares, same in every market\nArmington(product = \"wine\", sigma = 4)\n\n# calibrated shares: 70% domestic, the rest split between the two other regions\nArmington(product = \"wine\", sigma = 4,\n          shares = merge(Dict((r, r) => 0.7 for r in regions),\n                         Dict((o, r) => 0.15 for o in regions, r in regions if o != r)))\n\n# in the EU market, EU and US cars are near-interchangeable (shared safety\n# standards) while CN cars are a distant substitute; elsewhere everything\n# substitutes freely\nArmington(product = \"cars\", destination = \"EU\", sigma = 2.5,\n          nests = [OriginNest(sigma = 12, origins = [\"EU\", \"US\"])])\nArmington(product = \"cars\", destination = [\"US\", \"CN\"], sigma = 8)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.Armington"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.DemandCurve","page":"API reference","text":"DemandCurve(; product, region, p0, q0, elasticity, form = :isoelastic, shift = 1)\nDemandCurve(; product, region, alpha, beta, shift = 1)\nDemandCurve(c::DemandCurve; shift)\n\nFinal demand for one product — or for a DemandNest of substitutes — in one region: a Curve with side = :demand, given by a reference point and its elasticity or by the coefficients of a straight line. The third form is the curve c with the shifter shift in place of its own: the same calibration, whatever its form, moved by a new multiplier, which is how a scenario moves a curve.\n\nExample\n\nDemandCurve(product = \"gasoline\", region = \"EU\", p0 = 900, q0 = 80, elasticity = 0.3)\nDemandCurve(product = \"gasoline\", region = \"EU\", p0 = 900, q0 = 80, elasticity = 0.3,\n            form = :linear)                       # the tangent line at the same point\nDemandCurve(product = \"wheat\", region = \"A\", alpha = 200, beta = -9)\nthirsty = DemandCurve(milk; shift = 1.2)          # a fifth more milk at every price\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.DemandCurve"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.DemandNest","page":"API reference","text":"struct DemandNest\n\nA group of products that substitute for each other in final demand.\n\nDemand in this model is integrable by construction: it is the gradient of the consumer-benefit term of the objective, which is what makes the surplus-maximisation equivalence hold. An arbitrary matrix of cross-price elasticities is not integrable — it is the same obstruction that rules out an arbitrary matrix of Armington elasticities — so substitution between products is expressed the same way, as a tree. Products that substitute closely go in a nest with a high sigma; the nest as a whole then substitutes against the rest at the lower sigma of the level above, and the whole bundle faces one DemandCurve curve.\n\nThe implied cross-price effects are the usual CES ones: within a nest the compensated cross-price elasticity of product i with respect to the price of j is sigma s_j, with s_j the value share of j, so a large supplier transmits its price more strongly than a small one at the same sigma.\n\nSubstitution against goods outside the model — public transport against cars, say — needs none of this. An exogenous price is a constant, so its effect is a shift of the whole curve: use shift with exogenous_shift and the cross-price elasticity as the driver elasticity.\n\nFields\n\nsigma::Float64: Elasticity of substitution sigma between the members of the group. It must be ≥ 0, and at least the sigma of the nest enclosing it. Below 1 the members are poor substitutes, or complements; 0 makes them be consumed in fixed proportions (Leontief), 1 is Cobb–Douglas, and Inf makes them perfect substitutes — one good to the consumer, bought for whichever of them is cheapest\n\nproducts::Vector{String}: Products belonging directly to the group\nshares::Vector{Float64}: Value shares of those products at equal prices, in the same order. They are normalised, together with the share of each sub-group, over the members of this node. Equal shares when omitted\n\nnests::Vector{DemandNest}: Sub-groups of the group, for a deeper tree\nshare::Float64: Weight of this group among the members of the group enclosing it\n\nExample\n\n## petrol and diesel cars are close substitutes; an electric car is a more\n## distant one for both\nDemandNest(sigma = 1.6, products = [\"electric_car\"], shares = [0.25],\n           nests = [DemandNest(sigma = 5.0, share = 0.75,\n                               products = [\"petrol_car\", \"diesel_car\"],\n                               shares   = [0.65, 0.35])])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.DemandNest"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.Limit","page":"API reference","text":"struct Limit\n\nA linear constraint on the quantities of the model:\n\nlower ≤ expression ≤ upper\n\nwhere expression is a LimitExpression — supply, consumption, production, activity, process inputs and trade flows, weighted by any numbers and summed. It can cap what several products draw from a shared resource, set a quota or a minimum, tie one quantity to a share of another, or fix a sum. With lower == upper it is an equality.\n\nThe limit is part of the economy: it is checked by validate, shown in its summary, and reported in Results.limits with its value and its rent — the gain in the objective from relaxing the bound that binds by one unit, positive for an upper bound, negative for a lower one: what a permit to one more unit would be worth, or the subsidy that would get agents to meet the minimum on their own.\n\nWrite it with @limit, or build the expression with the term builders, qualified as Markets.supply and so on outside the macro.\n\nFields\n\nname::String: Name of the limit, used to report it\nexpression::LimitExpression: What is bounded: a LimitExpression, without its constant, which has been moved into the bounds\n\nlower::Float64: Lower bound, -Inf (the default) for none\nupper::Float64: Upper bound, Inf (the default) for none\npenalty::Float64: Cost per unit of crossing a bound: Inf (the default) for a hard limit, which cannot be crossed, or a positive number for a soft one\n\nEvery ceiling of the model is one: the capacity of a plant, an allowable cut, what a port can ship. A limit that only caps one variable — one supply, one activity, one flow — is set as a bound on it, which costs the solver nothing; any other is a constraint of the model.\n\nA limit is hard by default: no price can buy past it. With a finite penalty it is soft: it can be crossed, at that cost per unit beyond the bound, the way a sector model holds trade near its recent levels without forbidding a larger move. Below the bound nothing changes; past it the rent is exactly the penalty, which is also the most it can ever be. The penalty is a device of the model, not a cost anyone pays, so the results report it apart, in Results.penalties, and leave it out of welfare; a cost that is really paid past a threshold — overtime, an emergency import — belongs in the economy instead, as a second process or route at the higher cost.\n\nExample\n\n## an allowable cut, and the capacity of the mills, region by region\n@limit \"cut SEF\" supply(region = \"SEF\", product = \"logs\") <= 3.5\n[@limit(\"mill $r\", activity(region = r, process = \"sawmill\") <= cap[r]) for r in keys(cap)]\n\n## a port that ships at most 6 thousand containers, counted by product\n@limit \"port CN\" sum(w * exports(region = \"CN\", product = p) for (p, w) in containers) <= 6.0\n\n## two products harvested from the same stands cannot take more than they hold\nLimit(name = \"SEF stands\",\n      expression = Markets.supply(region = \"SEF\", product = [\"swr\", \"smallwood\"]),\n      upper = 12.4)\n\n## no more than a third of the sawnwood used in OF may come from abroad\n@limit \"OF imports\" imports(region = \"OF\", product = \"sawn_sw\") <=\n                    consumption(region = \"OF\", product = \"sawn_sw\") / 3\n\n## imports held near last year's, unless it is worth 50 a unit to go further\n@limit \"pulp imports DE\" imports(region = \"DE\", product = \"pulp\") <= 1.1 * last_year penalty = 50\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.Limit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.LimitExpression","page":"API reference","text":"struct LimitExpression\n\nA linear expression in the quantities of the model: a weighted sum of QuantityRefs plus a constant. It is what a Limit bounds, and it is built by adding, subtracting and scaling the term builders — Markets.supply, Markets.consumption, Markets.production, Markets.activity, Markets.inputs, Markets.flow, Markets.exports and Markets.imports — by numbers. Nothing else is defined on it, so whatever can be built is linear: the product of two quantities, a quantity in a denominator, a power or any other function of one is refused.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.LimitExpression"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.MarketData","page":"API reference","text":"struct MarketData\n\nThe complete description of an economy, to be passed to solve_market.\n\nFields\n\nregions::Vector{String}: Regions of the model\nproducts::Vector{String}: Products of the model\ndemand::Vector{DemandCurve}: Final demand curves, one DemandCurve per consumed product and region\nsupply::Vector{SupplyCurve}: Primary supply curves, one SupplyCurve per supplied product and region\nprocesses::Vector{Process}: Transformation activities, a vector of Processes\narmington::Vector{Armington}: Products whose regional varieties are imperfect substitutes, as a vector of Armington specifications. A product that is not listed here (the default) is homogeneous: the same good wherever it comes from\n\nlimits::Vector{Limit}: Linear constraints on any of the quantities of the model, as a vector of Limits: the capacity of a plant, an allowable cut, a ceiling on what a port can ship, a resource several products share, a quota, a minimum share, a fixed sum\n\ntaxes::Vector{Tax}: Taxes and subsidies, as a vector of Taxes, on production, on consumption, on transformation or on trade\n\ntransport_costs::Dict{Tuple{String, String, String}, Float64}: Unit transport costs, mapping (product, from_region, to_region) to the cost per unit of quantity shipped. This is also what declares trade: a route that is missing from this dictionary cannot be used, and a product with no route at all is not traded. Routes are directional, so a product that moves both ways needs an entry each way\n\nExample\n\nMarketData(regions   = [\"EU\", \"US\"],\n           products  = [\"crude\", \"gasoline\"],\n           demand    = demand,      # vector of DemandCurve\n           supply    = supply,      # vector of SupplyCurve\n           processes = processes,   # vector of Process\n           transport_costs = Dict((\"crude\", \"EU\", \"US\") => 25.0,\n                                  (\"crude\", \"US\", \"EU\") => 25.0))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.MarketData"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.Material","page":"API reference","text":"struct Material\n\nA material carried by the goods of an economy — carbon, dry matter, a metal, water — whose flows material_accounts traces through a solved economy, from the primary supply it enters with to the goods it ends up in.\n\nThe material enters with primary supply, at a given content per unit. It travels with the goods: a region's own production carries the content of what made it, and what a region uses carries the average content of what it received. In a process, part of what enters may be lost — burnt, emitted, discarded — and the rest leaves in the outputs, divided among them by allocation. Nothing else creates or destroys it, so what was extracted is what was consumed plus what was lost.\n\nHow much of the material a unit of a manufactured good carries is not given but follows from the solution: a mill that changes its mix of inputs changes the content of its output. This is the material-intensity accounting of Lorang & Gerlagh (2026), applied after the solve: the equilibrium does not depend on it.\n\nFields\n\nname::String: Name of the material, used to report it\ncontent::Dict{Union{String, Tuple{String, String}}, Float64}: Content per unit of primary supply, keyed by product — the same in every region — or by (region, product) where it differs, the more specific key taking precedence. A supplied product not listed carries none\n\nlosses::Dict{String, Float64}: Share of the material entering each process that leaves it other than in its outputs, keyed by process name; 0 for a process not listed. A process with no outputs, such as a disposal, loses all of it\n\nallocation::Dict{String, Dict{String, Float64}}: How a process with several outputs divides the material leaving it, keyed by process name: a weight per unit of each output, as product => weight. By default every unit of every output gets the same content\n\nExample\n\ncarbon = Material(name = \"carbon\",\n                  content = Dict(\"logs\" => 0.25),            # t C per m³ harvested\n                  losses  = Dict(\"pulpmill\" => 0.5),         # half burnt for energy\n                  allocation = Dict(\"sawmill\" => Dict(\"lumber\" => 1.0, \"chips\" => 1.0)))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.Material"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.Nest","page":"API reference","text":"struct Nest\n\nOne input requirement of a Process. Inputs in the same nest substitute for each other; different nests of the same process are needed in fixed proportion to the process activity.\n\nA Leontief (fixed-coefficient) requirement is a nest with a single product: composite units of it are needed per unit of process activity. Build it with leontief.\nA CES (smoothly substitutable) requirement bundles several products into a composite via a CES aggregator with value shares (δ, Σδ = 1), elasticity of substitution sigma (σ) and scale phi (φ):\ncomposite · z  ≤  φ · ( Σᵢ δᵢ^(1/σ) · qᵢ^ρ )^(1/ρ),     ρ = (σ-1)/σ\nAs an input's price rises the optimiser smoothly shifts the mix toward cheaper inputs. Build it with ces. sigma may be any value ≥ 0: the formula is replaced by its limit at σ = 0 (fixed proportions, minᵢ qᵢ/δᵢ), at σ = 1 (Cobb–Douglas, Πᵢ (qᵢ/δᵢ)^δᵢ) and at σ = ∞ (perfect substitutes, Σᵢ qᵢ).\n\nUse the leontief and ces builders rather than this constructor: they normalise the shares and check the arguments.\n\nFields\n\ncomposite::Float64: Units of the (composite) input needed per unit of process activity\nproducts::Vector{String}: Products of the nest. A single product makes the nest a Leontief one\nshares::Vector{Float64}: Value shares delta of each product, summing to 1 (equal shares by default). They set the input mix when all input prices are equal. Ignored for a single-product (Leontief) nest\n\nsigma::Float64: Elasticity of substitution sigma between the products of the nest. It must be ≥ 0: 0 for inputs needed in fixed proportions, 1 for Cobb–Douglas, Inf for perfect substitutes. It is ignored (and conventionally set to 0) for a single-product (Leontief) nest\n\nphi::Float64: Scale parameter phi of the CES aggregator\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.Nest"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.OriginNest","page":"API reference","text":"struct OriginNest\n\nA group of origins that substitute for each other more closely than they do for the rest, inside an Armington composite.\n\nRegions whose products are technically interchangeable — because they share grades, standards or certification, or because the same non-tariff measures let them through — belong in one group. Within it buyers substitute with the group's own sigma; against anything outside it they substitute with the sigma of the enclosing nest, which is lower.\n\nGroups may be nested further through their own nests field, so the structure is a tree whose leaves are origins. This is the general shape an elasticity specification may take: the substitution between any two origins is the elasticity of the smallest group containing both, and a group must be at least as substitutable inside as it is with the outside.\n\nFields\n\nsigma::Float64: Elasticity of substitution sigma between the members of the group. It must be ≥ 0, and at least the sigma of the nest that encloses it. Inf makes the members perfect substitutes: the group is pooled into a single good, as in the homogeneous case, while still being a distinct variety to the outside\n\norigins::Vector{String}: Origins belonging to the group\nnests::Vector{OriginNest}: Sub-groups of the group, for a deeper tree\n\nExample\n\n# in this market, EU and US products are near-interchangeable\nOriginNest(sigma = 12, origins = [\"EU\", \"US\"])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.OriginNest"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.Process","page":"API reference","text":"struct Process\n\nA transformation activity, operated at a level of activity chosen by the model.\n\nFields\n\nname::String: Name of the process\ninputs::Vector{Nest}: Input requirements, as a vector of Nests built with leontief or ces. Each nest is needed in fixed proportion to the process activity\n\noutputs::Vector{Pair{String, Float64}}: Outputs, as product => yield pairs per unit of activity. Several pairs make a joint-production process (e.g. petrol and diesel from one refinery)\n\nregions::Union{Colon, String, Vector{String}}: Regions where the process may operate, or : (the default) for every region\nvacost::Union{Float64, Dict{String, Float64}}: Value-added cost per unit of activity: the cost of everything not modelled as an explicit input (labour, energy, capital, ...). A single number applies to every region the process operates in; a region => cost dictionary sets them one by one, and must then cover every such region. Wages, energy and capital do not cost the same everywhere, and a cost calibrated on base-year margins differs from region to region. The installed capacity of the plants, which bounds the activity, is a Limit on it\n\nExample\n\n## a refinery splitting each tonne of crude into three fuels\nProcess(name    = \"refinery\",\n        inputs  = [leontief(product = \"crude\", coeff = 1.0)],\n        outputs = [\"gasoline\" => 0.45, \"diesel\" => 0.35, \"fuel_oil\" => 0.15],\n        vacost  = 40)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.Process"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.Results","page":"API reference","text":"struct Results\n\nThe outcome of solve_market: the solved model and the equilibrium quantities and prices, as tidy DataFrames.\n\nUse for_region to slice every table for one region at once.\n\nFields\n\ndata::MarketData: The economy that was solved\nmodel::JuMP.Model: The underlying JuMP model, solved\nstatus::MathOptInterface.TerminationStatusCode: What the solver made of it. Anything but OPTIMAL or LOCALLY_SOLVED means the tables below do not describe an equilibrium, whatever they look like; solve_market throws on those unless told otherwise\n\nraw_status::String: The solver's own account of how it stopped, such as Ipopt's Solve_Succeeded\nsolve_time::Float64: Seconds spent solving, as the solver reports them, or as measured around the solve for a solver that does not. Summed over all solves\n\niterations::Union{Missing, Int64}: The iterations the solver took, summed over all solves: interior-point iterations for Ipopt, simplex ones for a simplex solver. missing for a solver that reports neither\n\nsolves::Int64: How many times the model was solved: 1, unless an ad valorem tax on trade had to be found by iteration\n\nobjective::Float64: The value of the maximised objective: net social surplus, with taxes counted as costs rather than transfers and the penalties of soft limits taken out, so not welfare as soon as either is present\n\nwelfare::Float64: Net social surplus at the solution, taxes counted as the transfers they are and no penalty: the objective's terms evaluated without any tax. The figure to compare across policy scenarios. With a demand of elasticity eta le 1 its level is defined only up to a constant, so only differences between runs on the same demand curves are meaningful\n\npenalties::Float64: What crossing the soft Limits cost in the objective: each one's penalty times its excess. A device of the model rather than a cost anyone pays, so it is not part of welfare; zero without soft limits\n\nproduction::DataFrames.DataFrame: Primary supply + manufactured output: region, product, quantity\nsupply::DataFrames.DataFrame: Primary supply alone: region, product, quantity, the capacity — the tightest Limit on this supply alone, Inf if there is none — and the rent, what the limits it is part of charge per unit of it: the scarcity rent of a binding capacity, or its share of a shared limit's\n\nconsumption::DataFrames.DataFrame: Final demand: region, product, quantity, in the units the demand curves and prices use, and physical, the same in the physical units of the flows. The two differ only for an Armington product, whose users buy a composite: see composites\n\ncomposites::DataFrames.DataFrame: The composite of each Armington product in each region that uses it, against the physical quantity behind it: region, product, received (the sum of the varieties delivered there, its own included), composite (what its users draw on, final demand and processes together) and ratio = received / composite. A CES composite equals the sum of what it is made of only at the mix it was calibrated at; elsewhere ratio is the physical quantity a unit of composite stands for, and every use of the composite in the region is credited with it in physical columns. Value is exact on the composite side: composite times the price users pay is what was spent on the varieties, delivered\n\ninputs::DataFrames.DataFrame: What each process draws of each input: region, process, product, composite (in the units the balances and prices use) and physical (the same in the physical units of the flows)\n\nrecipes::DataFrames.DataFrame: Each input nest of each process against its recipe: region, process, inputs (the products of the nest), recipe (the nest's requirement per unit of activity times the activity), physical (the physical quantity of its inputs, summed) and gap = physical − recipe. A recipe is met in composite units; the gap is the physical quantity the aggregation of imperfect substitutes uses up (positive) or makes up (negative) on the way, whether in the region's Armington composites or in a CES nest itself. It closes a physical account of the economy. The sum means something only where the products of a nest share a physical unit\n\nsolution::NamedTuple: Every solved quantity, unrounded and unfiltered, as dictionaries keyed as in the model: supply, production (supply plus process output) and consumption by (region, product), activity by (region, process), flows by (product, from, to) — a region's own deliveries of an Armington product included — drawn by (region, process, nest, product), and composite and ratio by (region, product): each region's composite of an Armington product, and the physical content of a unit of it. The tables above are built from these with small quantities left out; accounts that must close exactly, such as material_accounts, start from them instead\n\ntrade::DataFrames.DataFrame: Bilateral trade flows, positive ones only: product, from, to, quantity, and the capacity and rent the limits put on each, as for supply: a ceiling on a port or a corridor charges its congestion rent on every flow it covers\n\nnet_trade::DataFrames.DataFrame: Trade by region: region, product, exports, imports, net (= exports − imports)\nprices::DataFrames.DataFrame: Equilibrium prices, the duals of the balances: region, product, price (what local users pay) and producer_price (what local producers get). The two differ only for an Armington product, where users buy a composite of the regional varieties\n\nactivity::DataFrames.DataFrame: Process activity levels: region, process, level, and the capacity and rent the limits put on it, as for supply: the installed capacity of the plants, and the quasi-rent it earns per unit of activity when it binds\n\nlimits::DataFrames.DataFrame: Every Limit: limit, its lower and upper bounds (-Inf and Inf where it has none), its penalty (Inf for a hard limit), the value its expression takes, its excess — how far past its bound a soft limit was taken, zero for a hard one — its rent — the gain in the objective from relaxing the bound that binds by one unit, positive for an upper bound, like a congestion rent or a quota price, negative for a lower one, the subsidy that would get agents to meet it unaided, and zero for a limit that does not bind — and the regions and model variables it applies to, as Markets.scope gives them. Empty when there are none\n\ntaxes::DataFrames.DataFrame: What each Tax raises: tax, what it falls on, and its revenue (negative for a subsidy). For per-unit rates the objective falls short of welfare by exactly this total; a relative rate on supply or demand scales a whole curve integral, and the gap is then not the revenue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.Results"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.SupplyCurve","page":"API reference","text":"SupplyCurve(; product, region, p0, q0, elasticity, form = :isoelastic, shift = 1)\nSupplyCurve(; product, region, alpha, beta, shift = 1)\nSupplyCurve(c::SupplyCurve; shift)\n\nPrimary supply (e.g. crude oil from wells, ore from a mine, fish from the sea, crops from land) of one product in one region: a Curve with side = :supply, given by a reference point and its elasticity or by the coefficients of a straight line. The third form is the curve c with the shifter shift in place of its own: the same calibration, whatever its form, moved by a new multiplier, which is how a scenario moves a curve.\n\nExample\n\nSupplyCurve(product = \"crude\", region = \"US\", p0 = 550, q0 = 600, elasticity = 0.4)\nSupplyCurve(product = \"crude\", region = \"US\", p0 = 550, q0 = 600, elasticity = 0.4,\n            form = :linear)                       # the tangent line at the same point\nSupplyCurve(product = \"wheat\", region = \"A\", alpha = -50, beta = 10.5)\nsalvage = SupplyCurve(logs; shift = 3.0)          # three times the logs at every price\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.SupplyCurve"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.Tax","page":"API reference","text":"struct Tax\n\nA tax or a subsidy — a subsidy being a negative rate — driving a wedge between what one side of a transaction pays and what the other receives.\n\non says what the wedge falls on:\n\non the wedge sits between selected by\n\"supply\" the market price and what primary producers receive product, region\n\"demand\" what consumers pay and the market price product, region\n\"process\" a transformation and its value-added cost process, region\n\"trade\" the price at the origin and the cost of landing a good product, from, to\n\nrate is per unit of the base when relative is false — per tonne produced, per unit of activity, per tonne shipped — and a fraction of it when relative is true: of the price received for \"supply\", of the market price for \"demand\" — so a rate of 0.25 puts what consumers pay a quarter above the market price — of the value-added cost for \"process\", of the price at the origin for \"trade\". Selectors left at : match everything, and several taxes may fall on the same base, in which case the absolute rates add and so do the relative ones, whose sum must stay above -1 as each rate does.\n\nwarning: A taxed equilibrium is not a welfare maximum\nThe quantities and prices a taxed model returns are the right ones: the wedges enter the program exactly as a cost would, and its first-order conditions are the distorted market's. The objective value is not welfare any more, because it treats tax revenue as a cost consumed rather than as a transfer received. Results.welfare is welfare: the same surplus evaluated at the solution without any tax.\n\nnote: Ad valorem rates on trade are solved by iteration\nAn ad valorem tariff is a fraction of the price at the origin, which is a dual of the problem and so cannot be written into its objective. For those, and only those, solve_market solves repeatedly, each time holding the tariff at the money equivalent implied by the previous solution's prices, until the two agree. Every other combination is exact in one solve.\n\nFields\n\nname::String: Name of the tax, used to report its revenue\non::String: What the wedge falls on: \"supply\", \"demand\", \"process\" or \"trade\"\nrate::Float64: Size of the wedge, negative for a subsidy\nrelative::Bool: Whether rate is a fraction of the base rather than an amount per unit\nproduct::Union{Colon, String, Vector{String}}: Products it falls on, or : for every one\nregion::Union{Colon, String, Vector{String}}: Regions it falls on, or : for every one — the producing or consuming region\nprocess::Union{Colon, String, Vector{String}}: Processes it falls on, or : for every one\nfrom::Union{Colon, String, Vector{String}}: Origins, for a tax on trade\nto::Union{Colon, String, Vector{String}}: Destinations, for a tax on trade\n\nExample\n\nTax(name = \"oil_royalty\",   on = \"supply\",  product = \"crude\", region = \"US\", rate = 4.0)\nTax(name = \"fuel_vat\",      on = \"demand\",  product = \"gasoline\", rate = 0.2, relative = true)\nTax(name = \"green_steel\",   on = \"process\", process = \"steel_mill\", region = \"EU\", rate = -15.0)\nTax(name = \"steel_tariff\",  on = \"trade\",   product = \"steel\", to = \"US\", rate = 0.25,\n    relative = true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.Tax"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.armington_calibration-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"armington_calibration(\n;\n    product,\n    flows,\n    prices,\n    sigma,\n    nests,\n    rtol\n)\n\n\nThe Armington specification of product that reproduces a base-year trade matrix exactly: its shares, through every level of the origin groups nests, and the scale of each destination's composite.\n\nflows maps (origin, destination) pairs to the base-year quantity shipped, the diagonal included — what each region keeps of its own production, which trade statistics do not report and production minus exports gives. prices maps the same pairs to the delivered price: the producer price at the origin plus the cost of carrying the product to the destination, plus any duty charged on the way. sigma and nests are the structure the specification will have.\n\nEach destination's tree is calibrated from the bottom up, by ces_calibration at every node: a group's price index and composite quantity make it one member of the group above, and an origin's share is the product of the shares on its path to the root. At the root, the scale is the base-year sum of the flows over the composite quantity, so that the composite counts the physical units the flows are measured in and its price is the average delivered unit value,\n\nP^c_r = fracsum_o X_or q_orsum_o X_or\n\nwhich is the consumer price the base year implies. Calibrated on that price, the destination's demand curves and the input bundles of its processes buy exactly the base-year quantities.\n\nA group of perfect substitutes (sigma = Inf) is a pool, bought only at its lowest delivered price: every member shipping to a destination must have that price there, to within rtol, and the members' shares do not matter, only their sum. An origin of a pool that ships nothing to a destination is kept in its composite if prices gives its delivered price there, so that it can start shipping when it becomes the cheapest, and the calibration checks that it is not the cheapest already. Any other origin that ships nothing is left out of that destination for good, as armington_shares does.\n\nEvery origin given a share must be able to deliver to its destination in the economy the specification goes into — produce the product, and have a route there — or the engine drops it and the shares no longer add up to what was calibrated.\n\nExample\n\n## France pooled, then with the rest of the EU, then against the rest\nflows  = Dict((\"FR\", \"EU\") => 4.0,  (\"EU\", \"EU\") => 70.0, (\"US\", \"EU\") => 6.0)\nprices = Dict((\"FR\", \"EU\") => 214.0, (\"EU\", \"EU\") => 205.0, (\"US\", \"EU\") => 238.0)\narmington_calibration(product = \"sawnwood\", flows = flows, prices = prices,\n                      sigma = 3, nests = [OriginNest(sigma = 9, origins = [\"FR\", \"EU\"])])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.armington_calibration"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.armington_shares-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"armington_shares(; flows, prices, sigma)\n\n\nCalibrate the Armington shares of one product from a base-year trade matrix, so that the model reproduces the observed sourcing at the observed prices.\n\nflows maps (origin, destination) to the quantity the destination bought from that origin in the base year, its own output included as (r, r). prices maps the same pairs to the delivered price of that variety there, i.e. the producer price of the origin plus the cost of carrying it to the destination. sigma is the elasticity the shares are being calibrated for, any finite value ≥ 0.\n\nThe shares are not the observed quantity or value shares. Inverting the CES demand condition X_o = Adelta_o (P^cq_o)^sigma gives\n\ndelta_or propto X_or q_or^sigma\n\nwhich is what this returns, normalised per destination: it is ces_calibration applied to the origins of each destination. Feeding observed shares in directly would only be right if all delivered prices were equal, since delta is by definition the share at equal delivered prices. From observed value shares s rather than quantities the equivalent form is delta propto s q^sigma-1.\n\nPairs with a non-positive flow are left out, and an origin left out of a destination's shares is left out of its composite: a route unused in the base year stays unused, which is the well-known small-shares limitation of Armington calibration.\n\nExample\n\nshares = armington_shares(\n    flows  = Dict((\"EU\", \"EU\") => 80.0, (\"US\", \"EU\") => 15.0, (\"CN\", \"EU\") => 5.0),\n    prices = Dict((\"EU\", \"EU\") => 210.0, (\"US\", \"EU\") => 235.0, (\"CN\", \"EU\") => 250.0),\n    sigma  = 4)\nArmington(product = \"wine\", sigma = 4, shares = shares)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.armington_shares"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.ces-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"ces(; composite, products, shares, sigma, phi)\n\n\nBuild a smoothly substitutable (CES) input requirement of composite units of a bundle of products per unit of process activity.\n\nshares are the value shares of each input when all input prices are equal (equal shares by default); they need not sum to 1, as they are normalised internally. sigma is the elasticity of substitution and must be ≥ 0: below 1 the inputs are poor substitutes, 0 needs them in fixed proportions, 1 is Cobb–Douglas and Inf makes them perfect substitutes.\n\nExample\n\n## 1.1 t of a mix of scrap and pig iron per tonne of steel\nces(composite = 1.10, products = [\"scrap\", \"pig_iron\"],\n    shares = [0.4, 0.6], sigma = 2.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.ces"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.ces_calibration-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"ces_calibration(; quantities, prices, sigma, rtol)\n\n\nCalibrate a CES group from the base-year quantities and prices of its members, so that at those prices the model buys exactly those quantities. The group may be any CES of the package: substitutes in final demand (DemandNest), inputs of a process (ces) or the origins of a product (Armington, for which armington_shares does this for a whole trade matrix at once).\n\nquantities and prices give the base-year quantity and price of each member, in the same order, and sigma is the elasticity of substitution the group is calibrated for. The result is a named tuple of three things the model needs and nobody observes:\n\nshares: the value shares delta of the members, i.e. the shares they would have if all their prices were equal. Inverting the CES demand condition q_i = Adelta_i (Pp_i)^sigma gives delta_i propto q_i p_i^sigma, normalised to sum to 1. They are not the observed shares: a member bought little because it is dear gets a large share, since it would be bought much more at an equal price.\nprice: the CES price index P of the group, the price of one unit of its composite at the base-year prices.\nquantity: the quantity A of the composite, the base-year expenditure sum_i p_i q_i divided by price. It is an index number, not a physical quantity: m³ of firewood and tonnes of pellets do not add up, while their values at a common price do.\nscale: sum_i q_i divided by quantity. Where the members are measured in one unit — m³ of wood of different kinds in a mill's input bundle — this is the scale phi that makes the composite count that unit again at the base year, at the average unit price sum_i p_i q_i  sum_i q_i: pass it as the phi of ces, with the base-year sum_i q_i per unit of activity as composite. Where the members do not add up it means nothing, and is ignored.\n\nprice and quantity are the reference point of whatever the group feeds: the p0 and q0 of the DemandCurve over a DemandNest, or, for a group nested inside another, the price and quantity of the group as one member of the level above.\n\nWhere the CES takes its limiting form, so does the calibration:\n\nsigma shares price quantity\n0 quantity shares q_i  sum q sum_i delta_i p_i sum_i q_i\n1 value shares p_i q_i  sum p q prod_i p_i^delta_i expenditure / price\nInf quantity shares, which do not enter the model the common price sum_i q_i\n\nPerfect substitutes are bought only at the lowest price, so with sigma = Inf every member with a positive quantity must have that price, to within the relative tolerance rtol. A member with a zero quantity gets a zero share, which DemandNest and ces do not accept: leave it out of the group.\n\nExample\n\n## the base year in one region: 7 units of firewood at 55, 1.4 of pellets at 250\nfuel = ces_calibration(quantities = [7.0, 1.4], prices = [55.0, 250.0], sigma = 3)\n## (shares = [0.0505, 0.9495], price = 177.05, quantity = 4.151, scale = 2.023)\nDemandCurve(region = \"OF\", p0 = fuel.price, q0 = fuel.quantity, elasticity = 0.35,\n           product = DemandNest(sigma = 3, products = [\"firewood\", \"pellets\"],\n                                shares = fuel.shares))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.ces_calibration"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.disposal-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"disposal(; product, cost, regions, name)\n\n\nA process that consumes a product and yields nothing: the way to give a by-product somewhere to go.\n\nMaterial balances here are equalities, so every unit produced must be used, traded or consumed. A by-product nobody wants is then a bad, and its equilibrium price is negative — the amount somebody must be paid to take it. That is the right answer to the question as posed, but it is rarely the question meant, and under a large supply shock it can drag a local market somewhere surprising.\n\nAdding a disposal route restores the usual assumption. With cost = 0 the price of the product settles at zero rather than going negative, which is free disposal; with a positive cost it settles at minus that cost, which is disposal that has to be paid for — a landfill charge, an incinerator, a tailings dam.\n\nExample\n\nprocesses = [refinery, steel_mill,\n             disposal(product = \"fuel_oil\"),           # free disposal\n             disposal(product = \"slag\", cost = 12)]    # paid disposal\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.disposal"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.exogenous_shift-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"exogenous_shift(; drivers...)\n\n\nA log-linear shifter of a supply or demand curve, built from the exogenous drivers that move it and the elasticity of the curve with respect to each:\n\ntextshift = prod_j left(fracZ_jZ_j^0right)^gamma_j\n\nEach driver is given as a NamedTuple with its current level, its reference level — the one the curve was calibrated at, which gives a shift of 1 — and the elasticity of the curve with respect to it. Pass them as keyword arguments, named for readability only.\n\nThis is the form that comes out of the usual estimation. Regressing log S on log P and on the log Z_j gives the price elasticity and the gamma_j in one go, so the shifters cost nothing extra to obtain once the curve itself has been estimated.\n\nThe shifter is the same on a straight line: it multiplies the quantity at every price, so gamma_j is the elasticity of the quantity to driver j along the whole line, as along a constant-elasticity curve. A line estimated in levels, Q = alpha + beta P + gamma Z, has its drivers in the intercept instead: there the driver moves alpha, by gamma (Z - Z^0), and is given as a new alpha rather than as a shifter.\n\nExample\n\n# a cod stock 18% larger than at calibration, and a slightly larger fleet\nshift = exogenous_shift(\n    stock = (level = 118.0, reference = 100.0, elasticity = 0.6),\n    fleet = (level = 1.05,  reference = 1.0,   elasticity = 0.25))\n\nSupplyCurve(product = \"cod\", region = \"EU\", p0 = 3000, q0 = 300,\n           elasticity = 0.40, shift = shift)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.exogenous_shift"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.for_region-Tuple{Results, Union{AbstractString, Symbol}}","page":"API reference","text":"for_region(\n    res::Results,\n    region::Union{AbstractString, Symbol}\n) -> NamedTuple{(:production, :supply, :consumption, :composites, :inputs, :recipes, :prices, :net_trade, :activity, :limits, :trade), <:NTuple{11, Any}}\n\n\nSlice every table of res for a single region (trade keeps both incoming and outgoing flows).\n\nReturns a NamedTuple (; production, supply, consumption, composites, inputs, recipes, prices, net_trade, activity, limits, trade), limits holding those that apply to the region, alone or with others. The taxes table is not among them: a tax may span regions, and does not belong to one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.for_region"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.impute_flows-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"impute_flows(\n;\n    production,\n    use,\n    distance,\n    decay,\n    home_bias,\n    totals,\n    rtol,\n    maxiter\n)\n\n\nA trade matrix for one product where the statistics do not give one: the flows that follow a gravity pattern as closely as possible while adding up to everything that is known.\n\nproduction and use give, by region, how much was made and how much was used there — consumed plus taken in by its mills. Every region ships what it makes somewhere, itself included, and receives what it uses from somewhere, so these are the row and column totals of the matrix. They seldom agree exactly; the uses are scaled to the total production so that they do. The known totals below are met as given, so one that fixes the whole use of a region has to agree with that use once scaled.\n\nThe pattern is the gravity one: a flow between two regions falls off as e^-textdecay cdot d with their distance d, a function (origin, destination) -> d or a dictionary keyed (origin, destination) in either order, and a region's deliveries to itself are weighted by home_bias.\n\ntotals adds whatever else is known, as (origins, destinations) => quantity, each side one region or a vector of them: a single observed flow, (\"NA\", \"EU\") => 9.6, or a total over a group, such as national exports that customs report without saying which region of the country they left from, ([\"SEF\", \"SWF\", \"GEF\", \"OF\"], \"EU\") => 12.5.\n\nThe flows are found by iterative proportional fitting — scaling them in turn to meet each total, until all are met to within rtol — which gives the matrix closest to the gravity pattern, in the entropy sense, that meets every total. A set of totals that contradict each other, such as exports larger than production, cannot all be met, and is an error after maxiter rounds.\n\nThe result, diagonal included, is what armington_calibration takes as flows, alone or merged with the flows that were observed.\n\nExample\n\n## logs among the regions of France, from what each harvested and sawed and\n## from national exports to the rest of the EU\nfrance = [\"SEF\", \"SWF\", \"GEF\", \"OF\"]\nimpute_flows(production = Dict(\"SEF\" => 3.6, \"SWF\" => 8.9, \"GEF\" => 3.0, \"OF\" => 3.7),\n             use        = Dict(\"SEF\" => 2.4, \"SWF\" => 5.1, \"GEF\" => 1.6, \"OF\" => 1.9,\n                               \"EU\" => 8.2),\n             distance   = (o, r) -> o == r ? 0.0 : 0.1,\n             decay = 1.4, home_bias = 3.0,\n             totals = Dict((france, \"EU\") => 8.2))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.impute_flows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.jump_model-Tuple{MarketData}","page":"API reference","text":"jump_model(d::MarketData; kwargs...) -> JuMP.Model\n\n\nThe JuMP model that solve_market would solve for the economy d, built but not solved: print(jump_model(d)) shows its variables, objective and constraints in full, and latex_formulation(jump_model(d)) the same in LaTeX.\n\nwarning: Signs in the LaTeX\nJuMP 1.32 drops the minus sign of a negative coefficient written in scientific notation when it typesets a model (JuMP.jl#4268), and the benefit terms of an inelastic demand are such coefficients. Read the signs from print until that is fixed.\n\nd is validated first, as solve_market does, and the keywords are those of solve_market that shape the model — solver, solver_options, silent and qfloor — so the model comes back with its solver attached and can be modified and solved with JuMP directly.\n\nAn ad valorem tax on trade is not in the objective: it is a fraction of a price, which is a dual of the problem, and solve_market finds its money equivalent by solving repeatedly. The model here is the first of those solves, with those tariffs at zero. Every other tax is in it as it will be solved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.jump_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.leontief-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"leontief(; product, coeff)\n\n\nBuild a fixed-coefficient (Leontief) input requirement: coeff units of product per unit of process activity.\n\nExample\n\nleontief(product = \"flour\", coeff = 0.75)    # 0.75 t of flour per t of bread\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.leontief"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.material_accounts-Tuple{Results, Material}","page":"API reference","text":"material_accounts(\n    res::Results,\n    material::Material;\n    tol,\n    maxiter\n) -> @NamedTuple{intensities::DataFrames.DataFrame, balances::DataFrames.DataFrame, processes::DataFrames.DataFrame}\n\n\nTrace material through the solved economy res, and return three tables:\n\nintensities: region, product, the content of a unit of what the region produced, and of a unit of what its users used — the average of what it received, for an Armington product, or of its own production and imports, for a homogeneous one. missing where there is none.\nbalances: by region and product, the material extracted with primary supply, made into the product by processes, imported, exported, consumed in final demand and processed as an input. For every row, extracted + made + imported − exported = consumed + processed.\nprocesses: by region and process, the material input, lost and output.\n\nThe consumed column is the material embodied in each region's final consumption: its footprint. Summed over the world, what was extracted is what was consumed plus what was lost.\n\nThe content of a manufactured good follows from its inputs: a process's output carries what entered it, less its losses, divided among its outputs by the allocation weights. These intensities form a linear system, solved by iteration from zero, which converges as long as the material cannot circulate for ever without loss; tol is the relative precision asked of them.\n\nThe accounts are built from res.solution, so they close exactly, and do not change the equilibrium: they describe it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.material_accounts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.solve_market-Tuple{MarketData}","page":"API reference","text":"solve_market(\n    d::MarketData;\n    max_tax_iterations,\n    tax_tolerance,\n    on_failure,\n    kwargs...\n) -> Results\n\n\nBuild and solve the equilibrium for the economy d and return a Results.\n\nIf the solver does not report an optimal (or locally optimal) solution, on_failure decides what happens: \"error\" (the default) throws, \"warn\" warns and \"ignore\" says nothing. The status, the solver's own message, the time spent and the iterations taken are on the Results.\n\nsolver is any JuMP solver able to handle nonlinear constraints and to return duals (Ipopt by default), and silent suppresses its output.\nsolver_options are passed on to it, as a NamedTuple, a Dict or a vector of pairs: (max_iter = 500, tol = 1e-9), or Dict(\"max_iter\" => 500, MOI.TimeLimitSec() => 60.0). A name is the solver's own name for the option, and an MOI attribute is passed as it is. They are set after Markets' own settings, and so override them.\nqfloor is the lower bound given to the quantities that are raised to a power below 1, which an interior-point solver cannot differentiate at zero. By default each such quantity is floored at a millionth of its own reference magnitude; a number here sets one absolute floor for all of them instead.\ntol is the quantity below which a flow, an output or an activity is treated as zero and left out of the result tables. It defaults to a thousandth of the smallest reference quantity in d, so that it scales with the units the economy is written in.\n\nAn ad valorem tax on trade is a fraction of the price at the origin, which is a dual of the problem and so cannot be written into its objective. When d has one, the equilibrium is found by repeated solves: each holds the tariff at the money equivalent implied by the previous solution's producer prices, and the loop stops when the two agree to tax_tolerance (relative), or warns after max_tax_iterations. Everything else is solved exactly, once.\n\nThat iteration is successive substitution, which converges only where the map contracts — quickly in every case tried here, including rates of several hundred per cent, but without a guarantee. The step is therefore halved whenever an iteration increases the error rather than reducing it, which rescues an oscillating case and leaves a well-behaved one at full speed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.solve_market"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.validate-Tuple{MarketData}","page":"API reference","text":"validate(d::MarketData)\n\n\nCheck that an economy refers only to names it declares, that nothing in it is declared twice, and that every part of it can be built as written.  Throws an ArgumentError naming the offending entry on the first problem found; returns nothing when the economy is well formed.\n\nsolve_market and jump_model call it before building anything, so it rarely has to be called directly.  The checks are:\n\nregions and products are non-empty and free of duplicates;\nevery product and region named anywhere — in a curve, a process, a transport route, an Armington specification, a Limit or a Tax — is one of those declared;\nno two SupplyCurves and no two DemandCurves describe the same product in the same region, and no two Processes share a name;\na transport route goes between two different regions;\nthe Armington specifications are consistent: at most one per product and destination, and a product imperfectly substitutable everywhere or nowhere;\na selector of a Tax or a Limit names something the economy declares and selects at least one quantity of it, no two limits share a name, and the members of one demand group carry one ad valorem rate;\nno process produces out of nothing at no cost, which would make the economy unbounded.\n\nIt also warns, without failing, about an Armington share given to an origin that cannot produce the product, or to a pair with no route: the engine drops such a share, so the pattern of trade differs from the one written down.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.validate"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#Markets.@limit-Tuple{Any, Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"API reference","text":"@limit name lhs <= rhs\n@limit name lhs >= rhs\n@limit name lhs == rhs\n@limit name lower <= expression <= upper\n@limit name lhs <= rhs penalty = p\n\nA Limit written as the inequality it stands for. Inside the macro the term builders — supply, consumption, production, activity, inputs, flow, exports and imports — are those of Markets.jl, whatever the names mean outside it, so they need no qualification; quantities may appear on both sides, and any constant is moved into the bound. name is any expression giving a string. A trailing penalty = p makes the limit soft: it can be crossed at a cost of p per unit (see Limit).\n\nExample\n\n@limit \"SEF stands\" supply(region = \"SEF\", product = \"swr\") +\n                    supply(region = \"SEF\", product = \"smallwood\") <= 12.4\n\n## the same, for every subset of the products a region's stands can supply\n[@limit \"$r: $(join(C, '+'))\" sum(supply(region = r, product = p) for p in C) <= stock(r, C)\n for r in regions for C in subsets]\n\n@limit \"milled at home\" activity(region = \"SWF\", process = \"sawmill\") >=\n                        0.5 * supply(region = \"SWF\", product = \"logs\")\n@limit \"export band\" 1.0 <= exports(region = \"RW\", product = \"logs\") <= 3.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.@limit"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#Markets.@limits-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"API reference","text":"@limits name [i in I, j in J, ...] lhs <= rhs\n@limits name [i in I, j in J; condition] lhs <= rhs\n@limits name [i in I, j in J] lhs <= rhs penalty = p\n\nA family of Limits, one for every combination of the indices, as a constraint family of JuMP or an indexed equation of GAMS: the same inequality, written as for @limit, with the indices free in it. Each member is named after the family and its indices — cut[SEF], stands[SEF, swr] — and the result is a plain vector of limits. An index may be written i in I, i ∈ I or i = I, and a condition after a semicolon keeps only the combinations that meet it. A trailing penalty = p makes every member soft.\n\nTo read a family back from the results, select its members by name: res.limits[startswith.(res.limits.limit, \"cut[\"), :].\n\nExample\n\n@limits \"cut\" [r in france] supply(region = r, product = \"swr\") <= cap[r]\n\n## one limit per region and product, leaving out the products a region lacks\n@limits \"stands\" [r in regions, p in primary; haskey(stock, (r, p))] supply(region = r, product = p) <= stock[(r, p)]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.@limits"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.Name","page":"API reference","text":"What the API accepts wherever it wants a name: an AbstractString or a Symbol.  :EU and \"EU\" mean the same thing everywhere, and both are stored, compared and reported as the string \"EU\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.Name"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.Selector","page":"API reference","text":"What a selector field can hold: every one, one name, or several.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.Selector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.CESNode","page":"API reference","text":"struct CESNode\n\nA node of a CES tree as the model builds it: children are leaves (Strings — origins, or products) or deeper nodes, shares their value shares among themselves, summing to 1, and sigma the elasticity they substitute at. It is the normalised form an OriginNest, a DemandNest and a Nest of process inputs are read into, so that one builder, ces_tree!, serves all three.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.CESNode"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.Curve","page":"API reference","text":"struct Curve{side}\n\nA demand or a supply curve for one product in one region: side is :demand or :supply, and the two aliases DemandCurve and SupplyCurve are how a curve is written and read. The curve is given in one of two ways:\n\nby a reference point (q0, p0) and the own-price elasticity there. The curve has constant elasticity by default, D = q0 · (p/p0)^(-η) or S = q0 · (p/p0)^ε, or is the straight line through the point with that slope, with form = :linear;\nby the coefficients of a straight line, Q = alpha + beta · p, with beta < 0 for demand and beta > 0 for supply. A demand alpha is the quantity demanded at a zero price and must be positive; a supply alpha may have either sign: negative, nothing is supplied below the price -alpha/beta; positive, something is supplied even at a zero price.\n\nEither way, shift multiplies the quantity at every price.\n\nFields\n\nproduct::Union{String, DemandNest}: The product, or, for a demand curve, a DemandNest of products that substitute for each other, in which case the curve describes the composite of the group\n\nregion::String: Region where the product is consumed or supplied\nform::Symbol: Shape of the curve: :isoelastic, the same elasticity at every price, or :linear, the same slope. Giving alpha and beta makes it :linear\n\np0::Float64: Reference price, i.e. the price at which the quantity is q0; NaN for a line given by its coefficients\nq0::Float64: Reference quantity, i.e. the quantity at price p0; NaN for a line given by its coefficients\nelasticity::Float64: Own-price elasticity at the reference point — eta of demand, varepsilon of supply — which must be positive. Any value is allowed: a demand with eta le 1 makes the benefit term of the objective an antiderivative of the inverse demand rather than the integral from zero, which leaves the equilibrium and the prices unchanged but makes the reported objective value a welfare difference rather than a level. Inf makes the curve flat, in either form: any quantity is bought or sold at p0, as an export market or imports at a given world price are (q0, which then plays no role, must still be positive, and shift has no effect). A flat supply that can reach a flat demand paying more than it costs, transport and taxes included, leaves the market without a finite answer. NaN for a line given by its coefficients\n\nalpha::Float64: Quantity at a zero price, alpha in Q = alpha + beta p, for a linear curve; given, or derived from the reference point. NaN for a constant-elasticity curve\n\nbeta::Float64: Slope beta = dQdp of a linear curve, negative for demand and positive for supply; given, or derived from the reference point. NaN for a constant-elasticity curve\n\nshift::Float64: Multiplicative shifter of the whole curve: the quantity at any given price is shift times what the curve otherwise implies. On a line, it scales alpha and beta together, so the price at which the quantity is zero does not move; a flat curve has no quantity to scale and refuses any shifter but 1. It is how everything other than the price enters — income, population and the price of a substitute outside the model for demand; the size of the resource, the roads and ports, the labour and machinery available for supply — leaving the reference point or the coefficients to hold the calibration. exogenous_shift builds one from drivers and their elasticities. The default, 1, is the calibrated curve itself. A hard ceiling on a quantity — an allowable cut, a quota, an exhausted resource — is a Limit instead\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.Curve"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Markets.QuantityRef","page":"API reference","text":"struct QuantityRef\n\nQuantities of the model of one kind — supply, consumption, a trade flow, … — summed over the regions, products and processes their selection selects. Built by the term builders, such as Markets.supply; the building block of a LimitExpression.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.QuantityRef"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.print-Tuple{IO, MarketData}","page":"API reference","text":"print(io::IO, d::MarketData)\n\n\nA summary of the economy d: its products and what happens to each, the processes in the order they chain, the routes and what they cost, and the taxes, subsidies and limits. Shown by print(d) and println(d), hence by string(d) and \"$d\", while typing d in the REPL, repr(d) and a container holding it give only its one-line count; printed to a terminal, lines wider than it are cut, and a route list too long for one line is summarised.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.print"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.activity-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"Markets.activity(; region = :, process = :)\n\nActivity level of the selected processes in the selected regions, summed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.activity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.armington_of-Tuple{MarketData}","page":"API reference","text":"armington_of(\n    d::MarketData\n) -> Dict{Tuple{String, String}, Armington}\n\n\nResolve the Armington specifications of d into a (product, destination) =>Armington dictionary, covering every destination of every product whose varieties are imperfect substitutes. Products absent from it are homogeneous.\n\nA product is Armington in every destination or in none, so a specification with a finite elasticity for some destinations must be matched either by one for each of the others or by a : fallback. validate runs this, so a specification that breaks the rule is caught before anything is built.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.armington_of"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Markets.armington_origins","page":"API reference","text":"armington_origins(\n    d::MarketData,\n    a::Armington,\n    r::String\n) -> Tuple{Vector{String}, Vector{Float64}}\narmington_origins(\n    d::MarketData,\n    a::Armington,\n    r::String,\n    can::Set{Tuple{String, String}}\n) -> Tuple{Vector{String}, Vector{Float64}}\n\n\nThe origins that destination r can buy product a.product from — itself, plus every origin with a transport route into r, keeping only those that can produce it — together with their normalised value shares.  Origins without a positive share are left out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.armington_origins"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Markets.armington_tree","page":"API reference","text":"armington_tree(\n    d::MarketData,\n    a::Armington,\n    r::String\n) -> Tuple{Union{AbstractString, Markets.CESNode}, Vector{String}, Vector{Float64}}\narmington_tree(\n    d::MarketData,\n    a::Armington,\n    r::String,\n    can::Set{Tuple{String, String}}\n) -> Tuple{Union{AbstractString, Markets.CESNode}, Vector{String}, Vector{Float64}}\n\n\nLay out the origins available to destination r as the CESNode tree described by the groups of a: origins placed in a group are aggregated by it first, and every other origin is a direct member of the root.  Each node's shares are those of the origins beneath it, normalised among its siblings. Groups that no available origin belongs to are dropped, and a group left with a single member collapses into it. Returns the tree with the origins and their shares.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.armington_tree"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.build_model-Tuple{MarketData, Dict{Tuple{String, String, String}, Float64}}","page":"API reference","text":"build_model(\n    d::MarketData,\n    tariff::Dict{Tuple{String, String, String}, Float64};\n    solver,\n    solver_options,\n    silent,\n    qfloor\n) -> Tuple{JuMP.Model, NamedTuple{(:D, :S, :z, :T, :X, :A, :drawn, :balance, :origin_balance, :limits, :surplus), <:Tuple{Dict{Tuple{String, String}, JuMP.VariableRef}, Dict{Tuple{String, String}, JuMP.VariableRef}, Dict{Tuple{String, String}, JuMP.VariableRef}, Dict{Tuple{String, String, String}, JuMP.VariableRef}, Dict{Tuple{String, String, String}, JuMP.VariableRef}, Dict{Tuple{String, String}, JuMP.VariableRef}, Dict{Tuple{String, String, Int64, String}, Any}, Dict{Tuple{String, String}, JuMP.ConstraintRef}, Dict{Tuple{String, String}, JuMP.ConstraintRef}, Vector{NamedTuple}, Any}}}\n\n\nBuild the JuMP model for d, with the ad valorem trade tariffs held at the money equivalents given in tariff, and return it with the variables and constraints the result tables are read from.\n\nInternal: jump_model is what a user calls to see the model, and solve_once what solves it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.build_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.ces_constraint!-NTuple{6, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"ces_constraint!(\n    m,\n    qs,\n    δ,\n    σ,\n    rhs,\n    name;\n    scale\n) -> Union{Nothing, JuMP.ConstraintRef{M, C} where {M<:JuMP.GenericModel, C<:MathOptInterface.ConstraintIndex}}\n\n\nRequire the CES aggregate of the quantities qs, with value shares δ and elasticity of substitution σ, times scale, to be at least rhs. The aggregate is\n\n( Σᵢ δᵢ^(1/σ) · qᵢ^ρ )^(1/ρ),     ρ = (σ-1)/σ\n\nand, at the three values of σ where that formula cannot be evaluated, its limit there:\n\nσ = 0, Leontief: minᵢ qᵢ/δᵢ, written as one linear constraint per member;\nσ = 1, Cobb–Douglas: Πᵢ (qᵢ/δᵢ)^δᵢ;\nσ = ∞, perfect substitutes: Σᵢ qᵢ.\n\nEvery one of them is concave in qs, so the set where it is above rhs is convex. The price of the composite is the CES index (Σᵢ δᵢ pᵢ^(1-σ))^(1/(1-σ)) of the members' prices, and its limits: Σᵢ δᵢ pᵢ at 0, Πᵢ pᵢ^δᵢ at 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.ces_constraint!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Markets.ces_tree!","page":"API reference","text":"ces_tree!(\n    m,\n    node,\n    leaf,\n    floor_at,\n    start,\n    base::String;\n    ...\n) -> Any\nces_tree!(\n    m,\n    node,\n    leaf,\n    floor_at,\n    start,\n    base::String,\n    in_power::Bool;\n    ...\n) -> Any\nces_tree!(\n    m,\n    node,\n    leaf,\n    floor_at,\n    start,\n    base::String,\n    in_power::Bool,\n    counter;\n    rhs,\n    scale\n) -> Any\n\n\nThe quantity of the composite that node stands for, as a variable of m: leaf(name, share, in_power) for a leaf — the variable of that origin or product, which the caller creates or looks up, told its share of the start value and whether it is raised to a power — and otherwise a fresh variable tied to its children by the CES aggregator of the node. start is the expected quantity of the node, divided among its children by their shares, and base names the constraints, numbered in the order the nodes are built. A composite needs a positive floor only where it is itself raised to a power, i.e. where the node it belongs to has a finite, positive elasticity.\n\nGiven rhs, the root gets no variable of its own: its aggregate, times scale, is required to cover rhs directly, under the name base. That is a nest of process inputs, whose composite is its coefficient times the activity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.ces_tree!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.check_nesting-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"check_nesting(sigma, nests, what)\n\n\nCheck that every sub-group of a group of elasticity sigma substitutes at least as easily inside as with the outside: the substitution between two leaves is the elasticity of the smallest group containing both, which only means something if the elasticities fall from the leaves to the root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.check_nesting"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.collapse-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"collapse(leaf::AbstractString) -> AbstractString\n\n\nnode with every group of one member replaced by that member, at every level: a group of one is no group, and dropping it keeps the aggregator, the floors and the printed model free of trivial nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.collapse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.consumed-Tuple{MarketData}","page":"API reference","text":"consumed(d::MarketData) -> Set{Tuple{String, String}}\n\n\nThe (product, region) pairs something in the region uses: final demand, or an input of a process operating there.  A region that uses none of a product needs no composite of it, though it may still produce it for others.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.consumed"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.consumption-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"Markets.consumption(; region = :, product = :)\n\nFinal demand of the selected products in the selected regions, summed. For an Armington product it is measured, as the demand curves measure it, in units of the region's composite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.consumption"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.curve_integral-Tuple{Markets.Curve, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"curve_integral(c::Markets.Curve, q) -> Any\n\n\nThe integral of the inverse curve c up to the quantity q, as a function of q that may be a model variable: the gross consumer benefit of a demand curve, the resource cost of a supply curve. For a constant-elasticity curve it is an antiderivative, not the integral from zero, which diverges for a demand with eta le 1: the constant changes neither the optimum nor the prices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.curve_integral"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.curve_price-Tuple{Markets.Curve, Real}","page":"API reference","text":"curve_price(c::Markets.Curve, q::Real) -> Any\n\n\nThe price along the curve c at quantity q, before any tax: the willingness to pay for the q-th unit on a demand curve, the marginal cost of it on a supply curve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.curve_price"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.demand_relative_tax-Tuple{MarketData, DemandCurve}","page":"API reference","text":"demand_relative_tax(\n    d::MarketData,\n    s::DemandCurve\n) -> Float64\n\n\nThe relative rate of tax on a final-demand curve.  An ad valorem rate scales the price of every member of a group alike, and so scales the group's price index; a rate that differed between members would not, and there is no single curve left to put it on.  Per-unit rates have no such problem — they are charged on each product's own quantity — so only this one has to be shared.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.demand_relative_tax"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Markets.denoise","page":"API reference","text":"denoise(x) -> Any\ndenoise(x, atol) -> Any\n\n\nZero when x is within solver noise of it, and x otherwise.  Used on rents so that numerical dust reads as no rent while a genuinely wrong sign survives to be seen.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.denoise"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.duplicates-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"The entries of xs that occur more than once, in order of first occurrence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.duplicates"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.each-Tuple{Colon, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"each(_::Colon, universe) -> Any\n\n\nThe names a selector field holds, as a vector: the one name, the several, or, for the colon, every name of universe, which must then be given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.each"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.exports-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"Markets.exports(; region = :, product = :)\n\nShipments of the selected products leaving the selected regions, summed. For a group of regions, only what leaves the group counts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.exports"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Markets.floored_leaves","page":"API reference","text":"floored_leaves(node) -> Set{String}\nfloored_leaves(node, acc) -> Any\n\n\nThe leaves that sit directly under a node of node that raises them to a power. Only those need a positive lower bound; a leaf under a Leontief node or pooled by an infinitely substitutable group enters linearly and may go to zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.floored_leaves"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.flow-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"Markets.flow(; product = :, from = :, to = :)\n\nShipments of the selected products between the selected regions, summed. A region's deliveries to itself are not flows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.flow"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.imports-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"Markets.imports(; region = :, product = :)\n\nShipments of the selected products entering the selected regions, summed. For a group of regions, only what enters the group counts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.imports"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.inputs-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"Markets.inputs(; region = :, process = :, product = :)\n\nWhat the selected processes draw of the selected products in the selected regions, summed — for an Armington product, in units of the region's composite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.inputs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Markets.leaves","page":"API reference","text":"leaves(n) -> Vector{String}\nleaves(n, acc) -> Any\n\n\nEvery leaf — product or origin — appearing anywhere in the group n, in a vector of groups, or a bare name, which is its own leaf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.leaves"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Markets.limit_terms","page":"API reference","text":"limit_terms(\n    limit::Limit,\n    d::MarketData\n) -> Dict{Tuple, Float64}\nlimit_terms(\n    limit::Limit,\n    d::MarketData,\n    cat\n) -> Dict{Tuple, Float64}\n\n\nThe model variables of limit, each with its coefficient in the limit's expression, the terms that select the same variable added up. A term that selects nothing in the economy is an error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.limit_terms"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.matches-Tuple{Colon, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"Whether a selector field matches a single name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.matches"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.maximum_vacost-Tuple{Process}","page":"API reference","text":"The largest value-added cost a process pays anywhere.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.maximum_vacost"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.members-Tuple{DemandNest}","page":"API reference","text":"The direct members of a group: its products, or its origins.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.members"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.model_catalogue-Tuple{MarketData}","page":"API reference","text":"model_catalogue(\n    d::MarketData\n) -> NamedTuple{(:supply, :consumption, :processes, :flows, :supply_in, :consumption_in, :processes_in, :flows_from, :flows_to, :order), <:Tuple{Vector{T} where T<:Tuple{String, Any}, Vector{Tuple{String, String}}, Vector{Tuple{String, Process}}, Vector{Tuple{String, String, String}}, Dict{String, Vector{T}} where T<:Tuple{String, Any}, Dict{String, Vector{Tuple{String, String}}}, Dict{String, Vector{Tuple{String, Process}}}, Dict{String, Vector{Tuple{String, String, String}}}, Dict{String, Vector{Tuple{String, String, String}}}, Dict{String, Int64}}}\n\n\nThe quantities of the economy d that a Limit can name, as the model will have them: the (region, product) of every supply curve and of every product consumed, the (region, process) of every activity, the (region, process, nest, product) of every input, and the (product, from, to) of every flow between two regions — the routes of a homogeneous product, and the origins of an Armington one that a destination buys from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.model_catalogue"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.namebound-Tuple{Real}","page":"API reference","text":"A bound that is either one number for every region or a region => bound dictionary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.namebound"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.namekeys-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"API reference","text":"A name => number dictionary, keyed by strings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.namekeys"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.namekeys2-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"API reference","text":"A dictionary keyed by a pair of names, e.g. (origin, destination).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.namekeys2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.namekeys3-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"API reference","text":"A dictionary keyed by a triple of names, e.g. (product, from, to).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.namekeys3"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.namepairs-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"A vector of name => number pairs, keyed by strings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.namepairs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.nm-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"nm(x::AbstractString) -> Any\n\n\nThe name x as the string the package stores.  Throws an ArgumentError on anything that is not a name, which is where a misplaced number or a forgotten => is caught.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.nm"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.nms-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"The names xs as strings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.nms"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.producible-Tuple{MarketData}","page":"API reference","text":"producible(d::MarketData) -> Set{Tuple{String, String}}\n\n\nThe (product, region) pairs the economy can produce at all: those with a primary supply curve, and those a process operating in the region yields.  A region that cannot produce a product has no variety of it, so it is nobody's origin for it — not even its own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.producible"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.production-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"Markets.production(; region = :, product = :)\n\nPrimary supply plus the output of every process, of the selected products in the selected regions, summed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.production"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.quantity_keys-Tuple{Markets.QuantityRef, MarketData, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"quantity_keys(\n    q::Markets.QuantityRef,\n    d::MarketData,\n    cat\n) -> Vector{Pair{Tuple, Float64}}\n\n\nThe model variables that the quantity reference q selects, each with its coefficient, keyed as (:supply, region, product), (:consumption, region, product), (:activity, region, process), (:input, region, process, nest, product) or (:flow, product, from, to). Production is supply plus each process's activity times its yield; what a fixed-coefficient (Leontief) input draws is its coefficient times the activity, while a CES input has a variable of its own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.quantity_keys"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.quantity_scale-Tuple{MarketData}","page":"API reference","text":"quantity_scale(d::MarketData) -> Float64\n\n\nThe scale of the economy: the smallest reference quantity in its demand and supply curves, or 1 if it has none.  The quantity floor and the reporting threshold are taken relative to it, so that they mean the same thing whether the model is written in tonnes or in million tonnes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.quantity_scale"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.regions_of-Tuple{Process, MarketData}","page":"API reference","text":"The regions process p operates in, in the economy d.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.regions_of"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.report-Tuple{Results, AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"report(res::Results, on_failure::AbstractString) -> Results\n\n\nAct on the solver's verdict: a solution that is neither optimal nor locally optimal is an error by default, since the tables that come with it look exactly like those of a solved model.  on_failure chooses between throwing, warning and saying nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.scale_of-Tuple{Armington, String}","page":"API reference","text":"Scale of the composite of Armington specification a in destination r.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.scale_of"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.scope-Tuple{Limit, MarketData}","page":"API reference","text":"Markets.scope(limit, d)\n\nWhat limit applies to in the economy d: the variables of the model its expression involves — supply[region, product], consumption[region, product], activity[region, process], inputs[region, process, product] and flow[product, from, to], production and fixed-coefficient inputs being expressed through the activities — and the regions those variables belong to, in the order the economy declares them. It is what Results.limits reports for each limit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.scope"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.selector-Tuple{Colon}","page":"API reference","text":"selector(_::Colon) -> Colon\n\n\nA selector: one name, several names in a vector, or the colon : — the default of every selector field — for every one, as it means every index of an array.\n\nNo name has a special meaning, so a region or product may be called all, every or anything else and still be singled out by naming it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.selector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.solve_once-Tuple{MarketData, Dict{Tuple{String, String, String}, Float64}}","page":"API reference","text":"solve_once(\n    d::MarketData,\n    tariff::Dict{Tuple{String, String, String}, Float64};\n    tol,\n    model_options...\n) -> Results\n\n\nBuild the JuMP model for d and solve it once, with the ad valorem trade tariffs held at the money equivalents given in tariff.\n\nInternal: solve_market is the entry point, and is what validates the economy, acts on the solver's verdict and iterates this function when an ad valorem rate on trade has to be found by fixed point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.solve_once"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.solver_attribute-Tuple{JuMP.Model, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"solver_attribute(m::JuMP.Model, attr, fallback) -> Any\n\n\nWhat the solver reports of attr, or fallback where it reports nothing: not every solver supports every attribute, and asking one that does not throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.solver_attribute"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.supply-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"Markets.supply(; region = :, product = :)\n\nPrimary supply of the selected products in the selected regions, summed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.supply"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.tally-Tuple{Results, Results}","page":"API reference","text":"tally(res::Results, earlier::Results) -> Results\n\n\nres, with the solver's time and iterations summed over it and the solves in earlier: when the ad valorem tariffs are found by iteration, the caller waited for all of them, not just the last.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.tally"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.tax_rates-Tuple{Any, AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"tax_rates(\n    taxes,\n    kind::AbstractString;\n    product,\n    region,\n    process,\n    from,\n    to\n) -> NamedTuple{(:absolute, :relative), <:Tuple{Any, Any}}\n\n\nThe absolute and relative rates of the taxes of taxes that fall on a base, summed. kind is the on field to match and the keywords are the selectors to check, each against the field of the same name.\n\nEach relative rate is above -1 on its own, but several can add up past it, and the model multiplies the base by 1 + relative: at or below zero a cost turns into a gain, or the benefit of consumers into a loss, and the solver may still report success. That is an ArgumentError here, where every summed rate the model uses is computed, and validate asks for the rates of every base so that it is reported before anything is built.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.tax_rates"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.tax_selectors-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"tax_selectors(\n    on::AbstractString\n) -> Union{Tuple{Tuple{Symbol, Symbol}, Tuple{Symbol, Symbol, Symbol}}, Tuple{Tuple{Symbol, Symbol, Symbol}, Tuple{Symbol, Symbol}}}\n\n\nWhich selector fields of a Tax the base on uses, and which it ignores.  A tax that sets a selector its base ignores would apply more widely than it appears to, so validate rejects it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.tax_selectors"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.tree-Tuple{DemandNest}","page":"API reference","text":"tree(\n    n::DemandNest\n) -> Union{AbstractString, Markets.CESNode}\n\n\nThe CESNode tree of a DemandNest: its products and sub-groups, weighted by their shares and share normalised over the node, with groups of one collapsed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.tree"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Markets.vacost_of-Tuple{Process, String}","page":"API reference","text":"Value-added cost per unit of activity of process p in region r.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markets.vacost_of"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/equilibrium/#Equilibrium,-curves-and-welfare","page":"Equilibrium, curves and welfare","text":"","title":"Equilibrium, curves and welfare"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/equilibrium/#Partial,-not-general,-equilibrium","page":"Equilibrium, curves and welfare","text":"The model represents one sector in detail (its products, processes and trade) and treats the rest of the economy as exogenous: income, population or construction activity enter only through the position of the demand curves, and the prices of non-modelled inputs (labour, energy, ...) enter only through the value-added cost of each process. A general-equilibrium model would add the accounting of the whole economy without adding much detail to the markets of the sector.","title":"Partial, not general, equilibrium"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/equilibrium/#The-equilibrium-as-surplus-maximisation","page":"Equilibrium, curves and welfare","text":"Under perfect competition, the competitive spatial equilibrium is the solution of a single optimisation that maximises net social surplus. The problem was posed by Enke (1951) — who solved it by building an electrical circuit — turned into an optimisation by Samuelson (1952), and given the programming formulation everything since has used, by Takayama & Judge (1964). The same formulation, applied to the global forest sector, is what [C3] solves:\n\nmax  underbracesum int_0^D P^D(x)dx_textgross consumer benefit\n     - underbracesum int_0^S P^S(x)dx_textprimary resource cost\n     - underbracesum c_k z_k_textvalue-added cost\n     - underbracesum tau T_texttransport cost\n\nsubject to a material balance for every product in every region. This is equivalent to \"clearing the markets\": the first-order conditions of the optimisation are the market-clearing conditions. One solve gives everything:\n\nthe quantities (production, consumption, trade, process activity) are the primal solution;\nthe prices are the dual variables of the material balances: the value of one more unit of a product available in a region.\n\nThere is no need to iterate on prices or quantities to clear markets.","title":"The equilibrium as surplus maximisation"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/equilibrium/#Where-is-the-producer-surplus?","page":"Equilibrium, curves and welfare","text":"The objective contains the producer surplus even though it is not written as a separate term. Let pi be the equilibrium prices. Adding and subtracting the values pi D and pi S splits net social surplus into:\n\nconsumer surplus, int_0^D P^D - pi D, the area between the demand curve and the price;\nprimary producer surplus, pi S - int_0^S P^S, the area between the price and the supply curve (the rent of the resource owners, e.g. mine or land owners);\nprocessors' profit, value of outputs − value of inputs − value-added cost;\ntraders' profit, (pi_to - pi_from - tau)T on each route.\n\nThe material balances make all the pi terms cancel, so the sum of the four is exactly the objective. Processes have constant returns to scale and trade is competitive, so at the equilibrium the last two are zero: an active process earns exactly its costs, and goods are shipped only where the price gap equals the transport cost. All producer surplus is therefore the rent of primary supply, which is in the objective as the area above the supply curves.","title":"Where is the producer surplus?"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/equilibrium/#Demand-and-supply-curves","page":"Equilibrium, curves and welfare","text":"Final demand and primary supply have constant elasticity, calibrated to pass through a reference point (q_0 p_0):\n\nP^D(D) = p_0 left(fracDq_0right)^-1eta qquad\nP^S(S) = p_0 left(fracSq_0right)^1varepsilon\n\nBoth elasticities need only be positive. Demand may be inelastic, which matters because that is how demand for most fuels, foods and basic materials is estimated — for forest products, for example, see Buongiorno (2015) for the orders of magnitude: earlier versions of this package required eta  1, on the grounds that for eta le 1 the area under the demand curve from zero is infinite. That argument does not hold. The objective needs an antiderivative of the inverse demand, not the integral from zero, and the additive constant — infinite though it is — changes neither the optimum nor the first-order conditions, hence neither quantities nor prices:\n\nint^D P^D = fraca1-1eta D^1-1eta quad (eta ne 1)\nqquad int^D P^D = a log D quad (eta = 1)\n\nWith eta  1 the coefficient and the exponent are both negative, so the term is still increasing and concave and the program is still convex. What is lost is the level of welfare, and with it some of the comparisons between scenarios: see Welfare when demand is inelastic.\n\nwarning: Warning\nInelastic demand changes results substantially, and in the direction people often forget: with eta well below 1 a supply shock is absorbed by prices rather than by quantities. Using eta  1 for a product whose demand is really inelastic makes a model systematically under-react in price and over-react in volume.\n\nA demand curve on its own depends on its own price alone. Products that compete for the same use are instead grouped under one curve, which is what gives cross-price effects between them — see Substitution in final demand. Either way the demand system is the gradient of the objective's benefit term, and that integrability is what makes the surplus-maximisation equivalence legitimate in the first place; it is also why the cross-price structure has to be a tree rather than a free matrix.","title":"Demand and supply curves"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/equilibrium/#Straight-lines","page":"Equilibrium, curves and welfare","text":"A curve can instead be a straight line, D = alpha + beta p with beta  0 or S = alpha + beta p with beta  0: the form of the classic spatial-equilibrium models from Samuelson (1952) and Takayama and Judge onwards, and of most mathematical-programming sector models. Its integral is quadratic, so the objective stays concave and the program convex.\n\nThe two forms agree at the reference point and in the slope there, so small changes are the same in both. They part on large ones. A line has a finite choke price, -alphabeta, above which nothing is demanded; a constant-elasticity demand never reaches zero. And the elasticity of a line grows as the price rises: a demand that is inelastic at the reference point becomes elastic further up. A large supply shock therefore raises the price less along a line than along a constant-elasticity curve calibrated to the same point. Neither is more correct; the line is what a linear regression estimates, and what a model built on one should be ported with.\n\nA line through a reference point is the tangent there to the constant-elasticity curve, form = :linear in DemandCurve and SupplyCurve. The flat curve, elasticity = Inf, is the same in both forms: a price given from outside, as world prices are for a small country.","title":"Straight lines"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/equilibrium/#Everything-other-than-the-price","page":"Equilibrium, curves and welfare","text":"A curve says how much is offered or wanted at a given price. Its position depends on everything else: how much ore is in the ground or how many fish are in the sea, how good the roads and ports are, how many workers and machines there are, how many people live in the region and what they earn. Those are exogenous to a partial-equilibrium model and have to enter it somehow. There are three standard devices, and they are not interchangeable.","title":"Everything other than the price"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/equilibrium/#A-multiplicative-shifter-—-the-workhorse","page":"Equilibrium, curves and welfare","text":"Scale the whole curve by a factor built from the drivers and the elasticity of the curve with respect to each:\n\nS(P) = underbraceprod_j left(fracZ_jZ_j^0right)^gamma_j_textshift\n        q_0 left(fracPp_0right)^varepsilon\n\nThis is what SupplyCurve's and DemandCurve's shift field holds, and what exogenous_shift builds. It is the device most sector partial-equilibrium models use to link a resource to what is taken from it — the forest to the harvest, the fish stock to the catch — and there are three good reasons for that.\n\nIt comes free with the estimation. The regression that gives the price elasticity — log S on log P — gives the gamma_j in the same step if the drivers are in it. No separate exercise is needed.\n\nIt is safe: a positive factor times a positive curve stays positive, at any level of any driver. An additive shifter, S = q_0 (Pp_0)^varepsilon + A, can drive supply negative and destroys the constant-elasticity reading of varepsilon. Use addition only where something genuinely arrives in a fixed quantity regardless of price — a stockpile released by a government, scrap collected under a recycling mandate, a quota of imports from outside the model. In this package that is a separate SupplyCurve held to that quantity by a Limit, not a shifter.\n\nIt separates calibration from scenario. p0 and q0 keep holding the base-year point the curve was fitted to; the scenario lives in shift. Rewrite q0 instead and the calibration is gone, which matters most in a recursive-dynamic run, where the time loop updates the shifter from the state of the resource each period and must not touch the fitted curve.\n\nnote: Quantity shifters and cost shifters are the same thing here\nA new road to a mine does not create ore, it lowers the cost of getting it out — a cost shifter, not a quantity one. With an isoelastic curve the distinction collapses: multiplying the quantity by m is exactly multiplying the marginal cost by m^-1varepsilon, since b = p_0 (m q_0)^-1varepsilon. With varepsilon = 05, offering 10% more ore at every price is the same as mining it 17% more cheaply at every volume. What matters is only that the elasticity you apply is the one your gamma was estimated against.On a straight line the two part. Multiplying the quantity rotates the line about the price at which supply starts, -alphabeta, which stays where it was; making every unit cheaper would lower that price too. A driver that works through cost belongs in alpha on a line, which is also where a regression in levels puts it.","title":"A multiplicative shifter — the workhorse"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/equilibrium/#A-ceiling-—-for-a-limit-that-binds","page":"Equilibrium, curves and welfare","text":"A shifter scales a curve; it never stops it. When the constraint is a genuine limit — a fishing quota, a mining licence, the capacity of the rigs, boats and crews that exist this year — the right device is a bound:\n\nS le bar S(Z)\n\nwhich is a Limit on the supply:\n\n@limit \"cod quota\" supply(region = \"EU\", product = \"cod\") <= 350\n\nThe difference is not cosmetic. At a binding ceiling the price parts company with the marginal cost on the curve, and the gap is the scarcity rent of the resource — the economically interesting quantity, and the one a pure shifter cannot produce. Doubling a shifter doubles supply at every price; a ceiling says that beyond a point no price will buy more.\n\nThe rent is the price producers get minus what the curve says the last unit cost, taxes on production included:\n\nrent = producer_price - ((1 + t_rel) * Markets.curve_price(s, S) + t_abs)\n\nwhich is the multiplier of the ceiling, and is how Results.supply and Results.limits report it: zero whenever the ceiling does not bind.\n\nA ceiling set above what would have been produced anyway changes nothing, so it is safe to carry one on every curve that has a known physical limit.\n\nA ceiling can also be soft: crossed at a given cost per unit, which caps its rent at that cost. That is a device for holding a quantity near a reference — trade near its recent levels, in the tradition of the recursive forest sector models — rather than a physical limit, and its cost is not part of welfare. See Soft limits.","title":"A ceiling — for a limit that binds"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/equilibrium/#A-structural-supply-—-when-the-factors-are-the-point","page":"Equilibrium, curves and welfare","text":"The two devices above are reduced-form: the curve is estimated, and the drivers move it. The alternative is to derive supply from the technology instead, by making the resource a product and its extraction a process. In a fishery model, for example:\n\nthe fish stock becomes a product with its own curve or endowment, bounded by a Limit;\nfishing becomes a Process that consumes the stock, fuel and whatever else the model prices, and yields landed fish;\nlabour, boats and access then enter as priced inputs or as bounds, and the shape of the resulting supply curve is derived rather than assumed.\n\nThis is how a computable general equilibrium model treats factors of production, and it is the honest route when the question is about the factors themselves — the effect of a labour shortage, of a fuel subsidy, of a fleet buy-back. The price to pay is the data: factor shares and factor prices by region, which a reduced-form shifter does not need.\n\nThe forest example takes the reduced-form route instead: its roundwood comes from supply curves, and its storm scenario moves one of them through its shift rather than modelling the forest itself.","title":"A structural supply — when the factors are the point"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/equilibrium/#Choosing","page":"Equilibrium, curves and welfare","text":"Driver Device\nResource stock: reserves, fish stock, farmland shifter, gamma typically well below 1\nQuota, licence, legal cap ceiling\nRoads, ports, accessibility shifter (a cost shifter in disguise)\nWorkers, rigs and machines ceiling, or structural if that is the question\nIncome, population shifter on demand\nStockpile release, recycled material a separate inelastic curve with a ceiling\n\nThe sensible default for a sector model is the second column of devices, with ceilings wherever a physical limit is known, and the structural route reserved for the questions that are really about factors.","title":"Choosing"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/equilibrium/#Welfare-when-demand-is-inelastic","page":"Equilibrium, curves and welfare","text":"Results.welfare is net social surplus at the solution: the terms of the objective evaluated with every tax left out, a tax being a transfer, and without the penalties of soft limits. The objective itself is not welfare as soon as there is a tax or a soft limit. Whether the level of welfare means anything, and which differences between scenarios do, depends on the demand curves.\n\nWhen every demand curve has eta  1 the antiderivative in the objective is zero at zero, so it is the area under the curve from zero. Welfare is then a true level, and two scenarios can be compared whatever differs between them. A straight line or a flat curve counts as such a curve: the area under it from zero is finite.\n\nWhen at least one demand curve has eta le 1 the area under it from zero is infinite: as the quantity falls towards zero the price rises fast enough that the area never stops growing. The model keeps the antiderivative of Demand and supply curves and leaves out the infinite remainder, so\n\nW_texttrue = K + W_textreported\n\nwhere K is infinite and depends on the curve: its elasticity, its reference point and its shifter. Three things follow.","title":"Welfare when demand is inelastic"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/equilibrium/#The-level-means-nothing","page":"Equilibrium, curves and welfare","text":"Neither the size nor the sign of welfare or objective says anything on its own. The forest example, whose demand elasticities lie between 0.3 and 0.45, reports a negative objective, and that is not a market destroying value.","title":"The level means nothing"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/equilibrium/#Differences-are-exact-when-the-inelastic-curves-are-the-same","page":"Equilibrium, curves and welfare","text":"Between two runs whose inelastic demand curves are identical, K is the same in both and cancels:\n\n(K + W_2) - (K + W_1) = W_2 - W_1\n\nThe difference is then the exact change in consumer surplus, producer surplus and tax revenue together, in money: its size as well as its sign. When an inelastic curve itself differs, the two runs leave out different infinite amounts, the difference between their welfare is meaningless, and a different but equally valid antiderivative could even reverse its sign:\n\nWhat differs between the two runs Difference in welfare Prices, quantities, trade, activity, rents\nsupply curves, their shifters included; value-added and transport costs; limits; taxes; demand curves with eta  1 exact, in size and sign exact\non a demand curve with eta le 1: its elasticity, its shifter, its reference point (p0, q0) or its form meaningless, in size and in sign exact\n\nThe last column holds in every case. Prices and quantities come from the first-order conditions of the program, which K does not enter, so a projection that moves the demand curves with their shifters is exact in everything it reports except welfare.","title":"Differences are exact when the inelastic curves are the same"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/equilibrium/#Only-differences,-not-ratios","page":"Equilibrium, curves and welfare","text":"A difference removes K; a ratio keeps it. The relative change (W_2 - W_1)  (K + W_1) has a numerator that is right and a denominator that depends on where the zero was put. A fall in reported welfare from −1000 to −1020 is a loss of 20 whatever K is, but as a percentage:\n\nWhere the zero is put Before After Change\nas reported, K = 0 −1000 −1020 +2%, a \"rise\", the base being negative\nK = 5000 4000 3980 −0.5%\nK = 1000000 999,000 998,980 −0.002%\n\nEvery line describes the same loss, and none is more correct than another: with the true, infinite K every change is 0%. It is the difference between Celsius and Kelvin, the same degree with the zero elsewhere: warming from 10 °C to 20 °C is +10 degrees on both scales, but +100% in Celsius and +3.5% in Kelvin. To express a welfare change in relative terms, divide it by something that has a true zero: the value of the market, pi D, producer surplus, or the revenue a tax raises.","title":"Only differences, not ratios"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/equilibrium/#When-the-inelastic-curves-differ","page":"Equilibrium, curves and welfare","text":"When the scenarios move an inelastic demand curve, the gap in consumer surplus between the two curves is itself infinite — a demand shifted outwards lies above the old one at every quantity — so no figure the model could report would compare them. What remains comparable is:\n\nproducer surplus, pi S - int_0^S P^S, since the area under a supply curve is finite at every elasticity;\nconsumer surplus over a common range of prices: the area under the demand curve between the price p consumers pay and a ceiling bar p chosen once for all the scenarios. For a curve D = textshift cdot q_0 (pp_0)^-eta on one product it is\nint_p^bar p D = textshift cdot q_0 p_0^eta\n    fracbar p^1-eta - p^1-eta1-eta quad (eta ne 1)\nqquad textshift cdot q_0 p_0 logfracbar pp quad (eta = 1)\nfinite for every eta. The ceiling is a convention, such as the price of a backstop substitute; the measure depends on it, so it has to be reported with the results.","title":"When the inelastic curves differ"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_calibration/#The-forest-sector:-calibrating-with-partial-data","page":"Calibrating with partial data","text":"The first page wrote the model with the numbers at hand before any calibration effort, and its reference run landed some way from them. This page builds the same model — the same regions, products, mills and elasticities — from the statistics a real application would actually have: incomplete, rounded, and not quite consistent with each other. The aim is a reference run that sits on the data, as near as the data allow.\n\nThe general ideas are in Calibration and Calibrating with partial data; this page is the worked case.\n\nwarning: For illustrative purposes only\nThe data are plausible, but they are not statistics. They are rounded the way published figures are, small flows are missing as they are from trade statistics, and they do not balance exactly — like real data.\n\nusing Markets, DataFrames, DelimitedFiles\n\nfrance  = [\"SEF\", \"SWF\", \"GEF\", \"OF\"]\nblocs   = [\"EU\", \"NA\", \"AS\", \"RW\"]\nregions = [france; blocs]\nproducts = [\"swr\", \"hwr\", \"smallwood\", \"chips\", \"pulp\",\n            \"sawn_sw\", \"sawn_hw\", \"panel\", \"plywood\", \"paper\", \"pellets\"]","title":"The forest sector: calibrating with partial data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_calibration/#Three-kinds-of-numbers","page":"Calibrating with partial data","text":"Everything the model needs falls into one of three groups, and keeping them apart is most of the discipline of calibration.\n\nObserved: what happened in the base year — what each region produced, used and consumed, at what prices, what crossed borders, what plant there was. It comes from statistics, and is read from files below.\nChosen: how the economy responds, which a single year cannot show — elasticities of supply and demand, and how easily one product or one origin replaces another. They come from the literature or from your own estimation, and are written in this script, where they can be argued about.\nCalibrated: everything else — the reference points of the curves, the shares of every CES, the costs of the mills, and here also the flows the statistics do not report. These are never guessed: they are whatever makes the observed year an equilibrium of the chosen behaviour.","title":"Three kinds of numbers"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_calibration/#The-statistics-at-hand","page":"Calibrating with partial data","text":"The data are long-format CSV files, one observation per row, the shape statistics come in. DelimitedFiles, from the standard library, is enough to read them; in a real application you would more likely reach for CSV.jl.\n\ndata = pkgdir(Markets, \"examples\", \"forest\", \"data\")\n\nfunction read_table(file)\n    cells, header = readdlm(joinpath(data, file), ',', header = true)\n    text(c) = c isa AbstractString ? String(c) : c\n    return DataFrame([String(h) => identity.(text.(cells[:, j]))\n                      for (j, h) in enumerate(vec(header))])\nend\n\n# a column of a table as a dictionary, keyed by the columns named\nlookup(table, value, keys...) =\n    Dict(Tuple(row[k] for k in keys) => row[value] for row in eachrow(table))\n\nProduction, from forest and industry statistics: the harvest of each assortment and the output of each mill, by region.\n\nproduction = read_table(\"partial/production.csv\")\nunstack(production, :region, :product, :quantity)\n\u001b[1m8×12 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m swr    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m hwr    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m smallwood \u001b[0m\u001b[1m chips   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m sawn_sw \u001b[0m\u001b[1m sawn_hw \u001b[0m\u001b[1m panel \u001b[0m\u001b[1m pl\u001b[0m ⋯\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Re\u001b[0m ⋯\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ SEF       3.58    1.58       8.31    0.985      1.2    0.135    0.6     ⋯\n   2 │ SWF       8.79    1.38       5.35    2.1        2.5    0.09     1.2\n   3 │ GEF       2.99    3.78       9.17    0.915      0.8    0.405    1\n   4 │ OF        3.86    5.16      12.5     1.1        0.9    0.45     1.6\n   5 │ EU      335     105        244      76.6       79.9   12.2     46.4     ⋯\n   6 │ NA      488      94.7      137     117        127     14.3     44.4\n   7 │ AS      200     240        317     127         40.7   45.5     98.1\n   8 │ RW      260     166        439      80.5       56.5   23       42.7\n\u001b[36m                                                               4 columns omitted\u001b[0m\n\nProducer prices: roadside for logs, mill gate for the rest, by region. This is what each region's own product fetched, so there is none where a region makes none.\n\nproducer_prices = read_table(\"partial/producer_prices.csv\")\nunstack(producer_prices, :region, :product, :price)\n\u001b[1m8×12 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m swr    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m hwr    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m smallwood \u001b[0m\u001b[1m chips  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m sawn_sw \u001b[0m\u001b[1m sawn_hw \u001b[0m\u001b[1m panel  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m pl\u001b[0m ⋯\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64?    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64?  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64?  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m In\u001b[0m ⋯\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ SEF         95     111         61     106      188      261     219     ⋯\n   2 │ SWF         93     112         63     104      183      265     213\n   3 │ GEF         96     108         62     107      192      252     217\n   4 │ OF          96     108         62     108      196      255     217\n   5 │ EU          98     117         68     111      200      277     241     ⋯\n   6 │ NA          94     132         80     100      196      311     238\n   7 │ AS         135     131         79     118      240      287     241\n   8 │ RW         103     107         64     115      189      230     214\n\u001b[36m                                                               4 columns omitted\u001b[0m\n\nUse: what each region took in of each product, for its households and its mills together, from consumption and industry surveys.\n\nuse = read_table(\"partial/use.csv\")\nunstack(use, :region, :product, :quantity)\n\u001b[1m8×12 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m swr    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m hwr     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m smallwood \u001b[0m\u001b[1m chips   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m pulp   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m sawn_sw \u001b[0m\u001b[1m sawn_hw \u001b[0m\u001b[1m \u001b[0m ⋯\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m \u001b[0m ⋯\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ SEF       2.65    0.419       3.46    0.32     0.55    1.95     0.228   ⋯\n   2 │ SWF       7.86    1.61        5.25    1.71     1.54    0.931    0.108\n   3 │ GEF       1.94    1.05        3.3     0.429    1.1     0.815    0.101\n   4 │ OF        3.73    1.93        8.96    1.42     3.3     5.66     0.658\n   5 │ EU      288      89.6       243      76       73.4    80.4     10.7     ⋯\n   6 │ NA      424      97.3       155     102       86.1   134       15.4\n   7 │ AS      301     269         366     142      129      45.5     53.3\n   8 │ RW      274     157         388      82.3     73.9    40.3     15.4\n\u001b[36m                                                               4 columns omitted\u001b[0m\n\nFinal consumption, the households' part of it. Firewood needs its own source — household energy surveys — because most of it never passes through a market that statistics see.\n\nconsumption = read_table(\"partial/consumption.csv\")\nunstack(consumption, :region, :product, :quantity)\n\u001b[1m8×8 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m smallwood \u001b[0m\u001b[1m sawn_sw \u001b[0m\u001b[1m sawn_hw \u001b[0m\u001b[1m panel   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m plywood \u001b[0m\u001b[1m paper   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m pellets \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real?   \u001b[0m\n─────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ SEF          2.36    1.95     0.228    1.03    0.196     1.83     0.637\n   2 │ SWF          1.07    0.931    0.108    0.461   0.0952    0.814    0.322\n   3 │ GEF          1.99    0.815    0.101    0.465   0.0811    0.763    0.395\n   4 │ OF           5.05    5.66     0.658    3.29    0.562     5.71     1.73\n   5 │ EU          59.3    80.4     10.7     48.9    10.2      71.1     27.5\n   6 │ NA          17.1   134       15.4     43      19.8      81.4     12.3\n   7 │ AS          97.6    45.5     53.3    110      93.2     132       20.3\n   8 │ RW         166      40.3     15.4     29.1    19.4      41.8      7.26\n\nTrade, from customs. Customs see borders, so they report trade between the blocs and France's trade as one country; they say nothing of which French region a log came from or went to, nor of anything moving between French regions. Flows under a thousand m³ or tonnes are not reported.\n\ncustoms = read_table(\"partial/customs.csv\")\nfirst(customs, 8)\n\u001b[1m8×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m product \u001b[0m\u001b[1m from   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m to     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m quantity \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Real     \u001b[0m\n─────┼───────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ swr      France  EU          6.64\n   2 │ swr      France  NA          1.77\n   3 │ swr      France  AS          3.76\n   4 │ swr      France  RW          2.42\n   5 │ swr      EU      France      8.37\n   6 │ swr      EU      NA         12.6\n   7 │ swr      EU      AS         34.7\n   8 │ swr      EU      RW         17.2\n\nThe mills: what the EU's industry statistics say each mill that can choose its wood actually used, per unit of output, and the pellet plants in place, which ran with some room to spare.\n\ninput_mix  = read_table(\"partial/input_mix.csv\")\ncapacities = read_table(\"partial/capacity.csv\")\n\nFreight, per unit of quantity and per unit of distance, and the distances, relative to the Europe–North America crossing:\n\nfreight   = read_table(\"freight.csv\")\ndistances = read_table(\"distance.csv\")\n\ndist = lookup(distances, :distance, :from, :to)\ndistance(a, b) = a == b ? 0.0 : get(dist, (a, b), get(dist, (b, a), 0.0))\nrate = Dict(zip(freight.product, freight.cost))\ntransport_costs = Dict((p, a, b) => rate[p] * distance(a, b)\n                       for p in products, a in regions, b in regions if a != b)\n\nPolicies and logistics, as on the first page: an export duty of a quarter of the border price on logs leaving the rest of the world, a ceiling on what the Landes can ship, and the base-year price of gas, which wood fuel competes with.\n\nlog_duty = 0.25\ntaxes = [Tax(name = \"rw_log_duty\", on = \"trade\", product = [\"swr\", \"hwr\"], from = \"RW\",\n             rate = log_duty, relative = true)]\nduty(p, from) = from == \"RW\" && p in (\"swr\", \"hwr\") ? log_duty : 0.0\nevacuation = @limit(\"landes_evacuation\",\n                    exports(region = \"SWF\", product = [\"swr\", \"smallwood\"]) <= 18.0)\ngas_reference = 30.0","title":"The statistics at hand"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_calibration/#Do-the-data-hang-together?","page":"Calibrating with partial data","text":"Not quite, and it is worth seeing how much before relying on them. Every m³ produced anywhere is used somewhere, so world production and world use should be equal, product by product:\n\nmade = lookup(production, :quantity, :region, :product)\nused = lookup(use, :quantity, :region, :product)\nworld(table, p) = sum(q for ((_, q2), q) in table if q2 == p)\nDataFrame((product = p, produced = world(made, p), used = world(used, p),\n           gap_pct = 100 * (world(used, p) / world(made, p) - 1)) for p in products)\n\u001b[1m11×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m product   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m produced \u001b[0m\u001b[1m used     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m gap_pct     \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64     \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ swr         1302.22  1303.18    0.0737203\n   2 │ hwr          617.6    617.909   0.0500324\n   3 │ smallwood   1172.33  1172.97    0.0545921\n   4 │ chips        406.2    406.179  -0.00516987\n   5 │ pulp         369.2    368.89   -0.0839653\n   6 │ sawn_sw      309.5    309.556   0.0180937\n   7 │ sawn_hw       96.08    95.895  -0.192548\n   8 │ panel        236.0    236.246   0.104237\n   9 │ plywood      143.6    143.534  -0.0457521\n  10 │ paper        336.0    335.417  -0.173512\n  11 │ pellets       70.4     70.444   0.0625\n\nAt most two tenths of a per cent apart: rounding, the usual condition of statistics from different sources. Larger gaps would deserve a look before going further.","title":"Do the data hang together?"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_calibration/#Behaviour:-what-one-year-cannot-tell","page":"Calibrating with partial data","text":"The same elasticities as on the first page, where each is discussed: supply, demand and its bundles, the mills' choice of wood, and the substitution between origins, with the French regions near-perfect substitutes for each other.\n\nsupply_elasticity = Dict(\n    \"swr\"       => Dict(\"SEF\" => 0.40, \"SWF\" => 0.45, \"GEF\" => 0.40, \"OF\" => 0.40,\n                        \"EU\"  => 0.60, \"NA\"  => 0.60, \"AS\"  => 0.55, \"RW\" => 0.55),\n    \"hwr\"       => Dict(\"SEF\" => 0.35, \"SWF\" => 0.35, \"GEF\" => 0.35, \"OF\" => 0.35,\n                        \"EU\"  => 0.50, \"NA\"  => 0.50, \"AS\"  => 0.50, \"RW\" => 0.50),\n    \"smallwood\" => Dict(\"SEF\" => 0.50, \"SWF\" => 0.55, \"GEF\" => 0.50, \"OF\" => 0.50,\n                        \"EU\"  => 0.60, \"NA\"  => 0.60, \"AS\"  => 0.55, \"RW\" => 0.55))\n\nη_construction, η_fuel, η_paper = 0.45, 0.35, 0.30\nσ_lumber, σ_construction, σ_fuel = 5.0, 1.6, 3.0\nlumber = [\"sawn_sw\", \"panel\", \"plywood\"]\nfuels  = [\"smallwood\", \"pellets\"]\ngas_cross_elasticity = 0.35\n\nσ_mills = Dict(\"plymill\" => 1.8, \"panelmill\" => 2.5, \"pulpmill\" => 1.8, \"pelletmill\" => 3.0)\n\nσ_blocs = Dict(\"swr\" => 6.0, \"hwr\" => 6.0, \"smallwood\" => 7.0, \"chips\" => 7.0,\n               \"pulp\" => 5.0, \"sawn_sw\" => 3.5, \"sawn_hw\" => 3.0, \"panel\" => 3.0,\n               \"plywood\" => 2.5, \"paper\" => 4.0, \"pellets\" => 6.0)\nσ_europe = Dict(p => 3σ for (p, σ) in σ_blocs)\nσ_france = 30.0\neurope(p) = OriginNest(sigma = σ_europe[p], origins = [\"EU\"],\n                       nests = [OriginNest(sigma = σ_france, origins = france)])","title":"Behaviour: what one year cannot tell"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_calibration/#From-statistics-to-model","page":"Calibrating with partial data","text":"","title":"From statistics to model"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_calibration/#The-flows-nobody-reported","page":"Calibrating with partial data","text":"Calibrating trade needs a full matrix of flows between regions, and the statistics give part of it. What they give is enough to fill in the rest:\n\nbetween the blocs, the flows themselves, from customs;\nwhat each bloc keeps of its own production — production less exports;\nand for the French corner of the matrix, its edges: what each French region produced and used, and what France as a whole bought from and sold to each bloc.\n\nThe corner itself — which French region shipped how much to which, and which of them the trade with each bloc went through — is filled in by impute_flows: the flows closest to a gravity pattern that add up to all those edges. In that small table the French regions stand for themselves, and each bloc stands for its trade with France: what it sent into France, as an origin, and what it took from France, as a destination. Blocs do not trade with each other in it — that trade is already known.\n\ncustoms_flow = lookup(customs, :quantity, :product, :from, :to)\nfrom_customs(p, o, r) = get(customs_flow, (p, o, r), 0.0)   # unreported: under a thousand\n\nfunction french_corner(p)\n    into_france = Dict(b => from_customs(p, b, \"France\") for b in blocs)\n    out_of_france = Dict(b => from_customs(p, \"France\", b) for b in blocs)\n    return impute_flows(\n        production = merge(Dict(r => made[(r, p)] for r in france if haskey(made, (r, p))),\n                           into_france),\n        use        = merge(Dict(r => used[(r, p)] for r in france if haskey(used, (r, p))),\n                           out_of_france),\n        distance = distance, decay = 1.4, home_bias = 3.0,\n        totals = Dict((blocs, blocs) => 0.0))\nend\n\nThe gravity parameters say how fast trade falls off with distance and how much a region leans toward itself. They are guesses, and need not be good ones: what the imputed flows must get right is their totals, which they do by construction. How little the guess matters is measured in Calibrating with partial data.\n\nWith the corner filled in, the whole matrix:\n\nflows = Dict{Tuple{String,String,String},Float64}()\nfor p in products\n    for ((o, r), x) in french_corner(p)\n        x > 0 && (flows[(p, o, r)] = x)\n    end\n    for o in blocs, r in blocs\n        o != r && from_customs(p, o, r) > 0 && (flows[(p, o, r)] = from_customs(p, o, r))\n    end\n    for b in blocs\n        haskey(made, (b, p)) || continue\n        exported = sum(from_customs(p, b, r) for r in [blocs; \"France\"] if r != b)\n        flows[(p, b, b)] = made[(b, p)] - exported\n    end\nend\nminimum(values(flows))\n0.00039824284997105755\n\nNot negative anywhere, which is the first check a base year has to pass: a region cannot export more than it makes. For softwood logs, the French corner looks like this:\n\nunstack(DataFrame((from = o, to = r, quantity = x) for ((p, o, r), x) in flows\n                  if p == \"swr\" && (o in france || r in france)), :from, :to, :quantity)\n\u001b[1m8×9 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m from   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m AS             \u001b[0m\u001b[1m SWF      \u001b[0m\u001b[1m OF        \u001b[0m\u001b[1m EU            \u001b[0m\u001b[1m GEF       \u001b[0m\u001b[1m \u001b[0m ⋯\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64?       \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64?  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64?      \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64?  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m \u001b[0m ⋯\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ GEF           0.632398  0.190195  0.106764         1.1627   0.185083    ⋯\n   2 │ RW     \u001b[90m missing        \u001b[0m 0.31792   0.161801  \u001b[90m missing       \u001b[0m 0.0812833\n   3 │ EU     \u001b[90m missing        \u001b[0m 3.76073   2.02422   \u001b[90m missing       \u001b[0m 1.1216\n   4 │ SEF           0.766124  0.230413  0.12402          1.29507  0.0631818\n   5 │ SWF           1.59279   1.87504   0.284388         2.80798  0.136991    ⋯\n   6 │ OF            0.768567  0.258542  0.441527         1.37403  0.0699094\n   7 │ NA     \u001b[90m missing        \u001b[0m 1.13872   0.540356  \u001b[90m missing       \u001b[0m 0.256672\n   8 │ AS     \u001b[90m missing        \u001b[0m 0.088186  0.0468063 \u001b[90m missing       \u001b[0m 0.0252188\n\u001b[36m                                                               3 columns omitted\u001b[0m","title":"The flows nobody reported"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_calibration/#Two-prices-per-region","page":"Calibrating with partial data","text":"A variety costs its buyer what its producer was paid, plus the duty if there is one, plus the freight to get it there:\n\nπˢ = lookup(producer_prices, :price, :region, :product)   # producer prices\ndelivered(p, o, r) = o == r ? πˢ[(o, p)] :\n                     πˢ[(o, p)] * (1 + duty(p, o)) + transport_costs[(p, o, r)]\n\nEach region buys a mix of varieties at different delivered prices, so two prices coexist in every region: what its producers get, from the statistics, and what its users pay for the mix. The second is the average delivered price of what the region bought,\n\npi^c_r = fracsum_o X_or q_orsum_o X_or\n\nthe value of its purchases divided by their volume — which is how a statistical office computes a consumer unit value, and also the price the calibrated model charges for the mix when it reproduces the base year.\n\nπᶜ = Dict{Tuple{String,String},Float64}()\nfor r in regions, p in products\n    bought = [(o, x) for ((q, o, d), x) in flows if q == p && d == r && x > 0]\n    isempty(bought) && continue\n    πᶜ[(r, p)] = sum(x * delivered(p, o, r) for (o, x) in bought) / sum(last, bought)\nend\n\nDataFrame((region = r, producer = πˢ[(r, \"sawn_sw\")], consumer = πᶜ[(r, \"sawn_sw\")])\n          for r in regions)\n\u001b[1m8×3 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m producer \u001b[0m\u001b[1m consumer \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────────────\n   1 │ SEF          188   209.65\n   2 │ SWF          183   207.303\n   3 │ GEF          192   210.053\n   4 │ OF           196   210.505\n   5 │ EU           200   203.443\n   6 │ NA           196   200.261\n   7 │ AS           240   236.667\n   8 │ RW           189   193.754\n\nSoftwood lumber, for instance. French mills get about 190 for it while French builders pay 210, because a good part of what they buy comes from the rest of the EU, dearer once delivered. In Asia-Pacific it is the other way round: imports are cheaper than the local product.","title":"Two prices per region"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_calibration/#Trade-shares","page":"Calibrating with partial data","text":"A destination's composite is a CES over its origins, and its shares are not the observed shares: they are the shares each origin would have if all delivered prices were equal. armington_calibration inverts the CES demand condition through every level of the origin tree, from the flows and the delivered prices. It also sets the scale of each composite, so that the composite counts the m³ the flows bring in: the m³ the flows bring in are then the m³ the mills and the households take out.\n\narmington = [armington_calibration(\n                 product = p, sigma = σ_blocs[p], nests = [europe(p)],\n                 flows   = Dict((o, r) => x for ((q, o, r), x) in flows if q == p),\n                 prices  = Dict((o, r) => delivered(p, o, r)\n                                for o in regions, r in regions if haskey(πˢ, (o, p))))\n             for p in products]\n\nA route that carried nothing in the base year, or too little to be reported, gets no share, and stays closed whatever prices do: the well-known limit of Armington calibration.","title":"Trade shares"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_calibration/#Supply","page":"Calibrating with partial data","text":"Each forest supply curve runs through the observed harvest at the observed producer price:\n\nsupply = [SupplyCurve(product = p, region = r, p0 = πˢ[(r, p)], q0 = made[(r, p)],\n                     elasticity = supply_elasticity[p][r])\n          for p in keys(supply_elasticity) for r in regions]","title":"Supply"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_calibration/#Demand","page":"Calibrating with partial data","text":"Each bundle is calibrated as on the first page, with ces_calibration, but on each region's consumer prices and consumption rather than on one price for all: the household in OF buys the mix of French and imported pellets, at what that mix costs in OF.\n\nconsumed = lookup(consumption, :quantity, :region, :product)\n\nfunction construction_demand(r)\n    inner = ces_calibration(quantities = [consumed[(r, p)] for p in lumber],\n                            prices     = [πᶜ[(r, p)] for p in lumber], sigma = σ_lumber)\n    outer = ces_calibration(quantities = [consumed[(r, \"sawn_hw\")], inner.quantity],\n                            prices     = [πᶜ[(r, \"sawn_hw\")], inner.price],\n                            sigma = σ_construction)\n    return DemandCurve(region = r, p0 = outer.price, q0 = outer.quantity,\n        elasticity = η_construction,\n        product = DemandNest(sigma = σ_construction,\n            products = [\"sawn_hw\"], shares = [outer.shares[1]],\n            nests = [DemandNest(sigma = σ_lumber, share = outer.shares[2],\n                                products = lumber, shares = inner.shares)]))\nend\n\nfunction fuel_demand(r, gas = gas_reference)\n    fuel = ces_calibration(quantities = [consumed[(r, p)] for p in fuels],\n                           prices     = [πᶜ[(r, p)] for p in fuels], sigma = σ_fuel)\n    return DemandCurve(region = r, p0 = fuel.price, q0 = fuel.quantity, elasticity = η_fuel,\n        shift = exogenous_shift(gas = (level = gas, reference = gas_reference,\n                                       elasticity = gas_cross_elasticity)),\n        product = DemandNest(sigma = σ_fuel, products = fuels, shares = fuel.shares))\nend\n\nbuild_demand(gas = gas_reference) =\n    [[construction_demand(r) for r in regions];\n     [fuel_demand(r, gas) for r in regions];\n     [DemandCurve(product = \"paper\", region = r, p0 = πᶜ[(r, \"paper\")],\n                 q0 = consumed[(r, \"paper\")], elasticity = η_paper) for r in regions]]\n\ndemand = build_demand()","title":"Demand"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_calibration/#The-mills","page":"Calibrating with partial data","text":"A mill that can choose its wood is calibrated like a demand bundle, from the observed mix at the consumer prices of the region it was observed in. Its quantities are all m³ of wood, so here they do add up, and the scale of the calibration lets the bundle keep counting m³: the composite is then the m³ of wood a unit of output takes, as the conversion factor says it.\n\nfunction bundle(process)\n    mix = input_mix[input_mix.process .== process, :]\n    c = ces_calibration(quantities = mix.quantity,\n                        prices = [πᶜ[(r, p)] for (r, p) in zip(mix.region, mix.product)],\n                        sigma = σ_mills[process])\n    return ces(composite = sum(mix.quantity), products = mix.product, shares = c.shares,\n               sigma = σ_mills[process], phi = c.scale)\nend\n\ntechnology = [\n    (\"sawmill_sw\", [leontief(product = \"swr\", coeff = 1.0)], [\"sawn_sw\" => 0.50, \"chips\" => 0.35]),\n    (\"sawmill_hw\", [leontief(product = \"hwr\", coeff = 1.0)], [\"sawn_hw\" => 0.45, \"chips\" => 0.35]),\n    (\"plymill\",    [bundle(\"plymill\")],                      [\"plywood\" => 1.0,  \"chips\" => 0.8]),\n    (\"panelmill\",  [bundle(\"panelmill\")],                    [\"panel\"   => 1.0]),\n    (\"pulpmill\",   [bundle(\"pulpmill\")],                     [\"pulp\"    => 1.0]),\n    (\"papermill\",  [leontief(product = \"pulp\", coeff = 1.10)], [\"paper\" => 1.0]),\n    (\"pelletmill\", [bundle(\"pelletmill\")],                   [\"pellets\" => 1.0]),\n]\n\nThe shares are the same wherever the process runs, so a pellet mill in SWF, facing other prices, uses another mix — the one those prices make cheapest. That is why one region's mix was enough.\n\nThe vacost of a mill — labour, energy, capital — is never observed in the units the model wants, but the base year implies it. A mill that ran and did not expand earned no more than its costs, so its cost is its margin: what its outputs fetched at the producer prices of its region, less what its inputs cost at the consumer prices there. For a bundle, the cost of the inputs is its price index at the region's prices, per m³ of the bundle.\n\nprice_index(δ, p, σ) = sum(δ .* p .^ (1 - σ))^(1 / (1 - σ))\ninput_cost(n::Nest, r) = length(n.products) == 1 ?\n    n.composite * πᶜ[(r, only(n.products))] :\n    n.composite * price_index(n.shares, [πᶜ[(r, p)] for p in n.products], n.sigma) / n.phi\nmargin(inputs, outputs, r) = sum(y * πˢ[(r, k)] for (k, y) in outputs) -\n                             sum(input_cost(n, r) for n in inputs)\noperating(outputs) = [r for r in regions if get(made, (r, first(first(outputs))), 0.0) > 0]\n\nDataFrame((; region = r,\n             (Symbol(name) => r in operating(outputs) ? margin(inputs, outputs, r) : missing\n              for (name, inputs, outputs) in technology)...)\n          for r in regions)\n\u001b[1m8×8 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m sawmill_sw \u001b[0m\u001b[1m sawmill_hw \u001b[0m\u001b[1m plymill \u001b[0m\u001b[1m panelmill \u001b[0m\u001b[1m pulpmill    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m paperm\u001b[0m ⋯\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64?    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float6\u001b[0m ⋯\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ SEF        28.4631     33.1359  158.855    96.1549 \u001b[90m missing     \u001b[0m   168. ⋯\n   2 │ SWF        26.2753     33.6311  136.764    89.8748      261.834    139.\n   3 │ GEF        31.0632     31.4612  162.853    94.3946 \u001b[90m missing     \u001b[0m   150.\n   4 │ OF         33.3277     33.5248  156.914    94.6011      285.92     131.\n   5 │ EU         39.6901     44.8836  150.199   118.897       298.399    199. ⋯\n   6 │ NA         37.8565     42.3484  142.735   113.204       284.676    189.\n   7 │ AS         33.911      38.4     128.165   101.513       252.874    170.\n   8 │ RW         30.9165     35.1091  117.633    93.474       233.387    156.\n\u001b[36m                                                               2 columns omitted\u001b[0m\n\nThe margins differ from region to region, as wages and energy do, which is why a process takes a region => cost dictionary. They are positive everywhere, the second check a base year has to pass: a negative one would say a mill ran at a loss, and the model would close it. A mill running flat out could have earned a rent on top of its costs, which one year cannot tell apart from them; that is why the capacities in the data are those of plants with room to spare. Each becomes a limit on the activity of its plants:\n\nplant_limits = [@limit(\"$(row.process) plant $(row.region)\",\n                       activity(region = row.region, process = row.process) <= row.capacity)\n                for row in eachrow(capacities)]\n\nprocesses = [Process(name = name, inputs = inputs, outputs = outputs,\n                     regions  = operating(outputs),\n                     vacost   = Dict(r => margin(inputs, outputs, r) for r in operating(outputs)))\n             for (name, inputs, outputs) in technology]","title":"The mills"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_calibration/#The-calibrated-reference-run","page":"Calibrating with partial data","text":"calibrated = MarketData(; regions, products, demand, supply, processes,\n                          transport_costs, armington, taxes,\n                          limits = [plant_limits; evacuation])\nres = solve_market(calibrated)","title":"The calibrated reference run"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_calibration/#How-close-does-it-land-to-the-data?","page":"Calibrating with partial data","text":"Every curve, share and cost was fitted to one observation, and each passes through it exactly. But the observations do not quite agree with each other, and an equilibrium has to satisfy every balance at once, so it settles near every observation and on none. How near is the first thing to look at, every time. Model trade is compared at the level customs report it, with the French regions added up:\n\nmodelled(table, value, keys...) = lookup(table, value, keys...)\nrelative_gaps(model, observed) = sort([abs(get(model, k, 0.0) / v - 1) for (k, v) in observed])\nat_customs_level(trade) = combine(groupby(transform(trade,\n        :from => ByRow(r -> r in france ? \"France\" : r) => :from,\n        :to   => ByRow(r -> r in france ? \"France\" : r) => :to), [:product, :from, :to]),\n    :quantity => sum => :quantity)\nreported(trade) = (t = at_customs_level(trade); t[t.from .!= t.to, :])\n\ncomparison = [\n    \"producer prices\" => (modelled(res.prices, :producer_price, :region, :product), πˢ),\n    \"production\"      => (modelled(res.production, :quantity, :region, :product), made),\n    \"consumption\"     => (modelled(res.consumption, :quantity, :region, :product), consumed),\n    \"customs trade\"   => (lookup(reported(res.trade), :quantity, :product, :from, :to),\n                          lookup(customs, :quantity, :product, :from, :to))]\n\nDataFrame((observed = name, entries = length(observed),\n           median_gap = gaps[cld(end, 2)], largest_gap = last(gaps))\n          for (name, (model, observed)) in comparison\n          for gaps in (relative_gaps(model, observed),))\n\u001b[1m4×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m observed        \u001b[0m\u001b[1m entries \u001b[0m\u001b[1m median_gap  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m largest_gap \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String          \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64     \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ producer prices       86  0.00106155    0.00386404\n   2 │ production            86  0.00081291    0.00767134\n   3 │ consumption           56  0.000572178   0.00390559\n   4 │ customs trade        220  0.00163231    0.0241094\n\nWithin a fraction of a per cent for prices and quantities, a few per cent at worst for the smallest trade flows: gaps of the size of the rounding. The data are consistent up to their own precision, and the calibration did its job. Gaps of ten or twenty per cent would say the data contradict each other — a balance that does not close, a price from another year — or that a step went wrong, a unit, a sign, a forgotten duty; either is worth chasing before asking the model anything, because every scenario starts from where the model landed.\n\nCompare the first page, whose reference run missed its own inputs by far more.","title":"How close does it land to the data?"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_calibration/#The-same-scenarios","page":"Calibrating with partial data","text":"The storm in the Landes and the dearer gas of the first page, unchanged: the salvage triples what SWF offers at any price, and gas doubles.\n\nreconfigure(d; kwargs...) =\n    MarketData(; (f => get(kwargs, f, getfield(d, f)) for f in fieldnames(MarketData))...)\nsalvage = [s.region == \"SWF\" && s.product == \"swr\" ?\n               SupplyCurve(s; shift = 3.0) : s\n           for s in supply]\nstorm = solve_market(reconfigure(calibrated, supply = salvage))\ngas   = solve_market(reconfigure(calibrated, demand = build_demand(60.0)))\n\nprice_of(r, p, result) = only(result.prices[(result.prices.region .== r) .&\n                                            (result.prices.product .== p), :price])\nquantity_of(table, r, p) = (rows = table[(table.region .== r) .& (table.product .== p),\n                                            :quantity];\n                            isempty(rows) ? 0.0 : only(rows))\nchange(after, before) = 100 * (after / before - 1)\n\nDataFrame((region = r,\n           storm_log_price = change(price_of(r, \"swr\", storm), price_of(r, \"swr\", res)),\n           gas_pellets  = change(quantity_of(gas.consumption, r, \"pellets\"),\n                                 quantity_of(res.consumption, r, \"pellets\")),\n           gas_firewood = change(quantity_of(gas.consumption, r, \"smallwood\"),\n                                 quantity_of(res.consumption, r, \"smallwood\")))\n          for r in regions)\n\u001b[1m8×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m storm_log_price \u001b[0m\u001b[1m gas_pellets \u001b[0m\u001b[1m gas_firewood \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64         \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64      \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ SEF           -1.90096      13.624         32.949\n   2 │ SWF           -5.37079      13.8991        33.5463\n   3 │ GEF           -1.88032      12.4448        31.5242\n   4 │ OF            -1.92407      14.5928        33.9853\n   5 │ EU            -1.46531      15.0494        35.9563\n   6 │ NA            -0.633874     18.1867        35.7753\n   7 │ AS            -0.667131     10.1266        33.1821\n   8 │ RW            -0.690085      4.43862       26.4284\n\nPercentage changes from the calibrated reference run. They are read the same way as on the first page; whether they differ from the first page's, and from what perfect data would give, is the subject of the last page, which reads these results from a file:\n\nresults_dir = joinpath(pkgdir(Markets), \"examples\", \"forest\", \"results\")\n\nfunction outcome(result, scenario)\n    t = outerjoin(select(result.prices, :region, :product, :price, :producer_price),\n                  rename(result.production, :quantity => :production),\n                  select(result.consumption, :region, :product, :quantity => :consumption),\n                  on = [:region, :product])\n    return insertcols!(t, 1, :scenario => scenario)\nend\n\nfunction save_outcomes(file, runs)\n    t = vcat((outcome(r, s) for (s, r) in runs)...)\n    cell(x) = ismissing(x) ? \"\" : x isa Real ? string(round(x; sigdigits = 6)) : string(x)\n    mkpath(results_dir)\n    open(joinpath(results_dir, file), \"w\") do io\n        println(io, join(names(t), \",\"))\n        foreach(row -> println(io, join(cell.(collect(row)), \",\")), eachrow(t))\n    end\nend\n\nsave_outcomes(\"partial.csv\", [\"reference\" => res, \"storm\" => storm, \"gas\" => gas])","title":"The same scenarios"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_calibration/#What-the-wood-is-made-of","page":"Calibrating with partial data","text":"The model counts m³ and tonnes of products. What those products are made of — the carbon in them, say — can be traced through the same solution with material_accounts, which neither needs nor changes anything in the equilibrium; see Material accounts.\n\nA Material needs three things. Its content per unit of primary supply: about a quarter of a tonne of carbon in a m³ of wood, a little more in hardwood, which is denser. What each mill loses of what enters it: the sawdust and bark a sawmill does not turn into lumber or chips, the half of the wood a pulp mill burns for energy. And, for a mill with several outputs, how it divides the rest; by default every unit of output gets the same content, which suits a sawmill whose lumber and chips are both m³ of the same wood. The figures are illustrative.\n\ncarbon = Material(name = \"carbon\",\n                  content = Dict(\"swr\" => 0.23, \"hwr\" => 0.29, \"smallwood\" => 0.25),\n                  losses  = Dict(\"sawmill_sw\" => 0.15, \"sawmill_hw\" => 0.20,\n                                 \"plymill\" => 0.10, \"panelmill\" => 0.05,\n                                 \"pulpmill\" => 0.50, \"papermill\" => 0.05,\n                                 \"pelletmill\" => 0.10))\naccounts = material_accounts(res, carbon)\n\nThe accounts close for every region and product, and for the world: the carbon harvested is the carbon in what was consumed, plus what the mills lost on the way, in million tonnes:\n\n(harvested = sum(accounts.balances.extracted),\n consumed  = sum(accounts.balances.consumed),\n lost      = sum(accounts.processes.lost))\n(harvested = 772.2432248242017, consumed = 521.2368026052873, lost = 251.00642221891434)\n\nThe content of a manufactured product is not given but follows from the solution — from what each mill took in, and where that came from. A tonne of pellets carries a different amount of carbon in each region, because each region's pellet mills use a different mix of chips, smallwood and logs:\n\ncarbon_in(acc, product, column) =\n    DataFrame((region = r, content = only(acc.intensities[(acc.intensities.region .== r) .&\n                                                           (acc.intensities.product .== product),\n                                                           column]))\n              for r in regions)\ncarbon_in(accounts, \"pellets\", :produced)\n\u001b[1m8×2 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m content  \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\n─────┼──────────────────\n   1 │ SEF     0.542802\n   2 │ SWF     0.537954\n   3 │ GEF     0.542741\n   4 │ OF      0.543399\n   5 │ EU      0.534592\n   6 │ NA      0.502886\n   7 │ AS      0.535942\n   8 │ RW      0.551728\n\nAnd the consumed column is each region's footprint, the carbon embodied in its final consumption of wood products:\n\ncombine(groupby(accounts.balances, :region), :consumed => sum => :footprint)\n\u001b[1m8×2 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m footprint \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\n─────┼───────────────────\n   1 │ SEF       2.83156\n   2 │ SWF       1.30413\n   3 │ GEF       1.51493\n   4 │ OF        8.01574\n   5 │ EU      108.323\n   6 │ NA      107.897\n   7 │ AS      194.342\n   8 │ RW       97.0091\n\nRun on a scenario, the same accounts say how the content of the products moves with the economy. After the storm, a tonne of the Landes pellets carries more carbon:\n\nstorm_accounts = material_accounts(storm, carbon)\nleftjoin(rename(carbon_in(accounts, \"pellets\", :produced), :content => :reference),\n         rename(carbon_in(storm_accounts, \"pellets\", :produced), :content => :storm),\n         on = :region)\n\u001b[1m8×3 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m reference \u001b[0m\u001b[1m storm    \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\n─────┼─────────────────────────────\n   1 │ SEF      0.542802  0.542821\n   2 │ SWF      0.537954  0.552373\n   3 │ GEF      0.542741  0.542687\n   4 │ OF       0.543399  0.543346\n   5 │ EU       0.534592  0.533614\n   6 │ NA       0.502886  0.502548\n   7 │ AS       0.535942  0.535571\n   8 │ RW       0.551728  0.551197\n\nThe carbon follows the wood. The Landes pellet mills now take in 2.47 m³ of wood per tonne, against 2.40 before: their CES bundle, and the composites of the region they draw on, stand for more physical wood once the mix moves — the gap of the Physical accounts — and in these accounts that wood and its carbon are carried into the pellets rather than left unexplained.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"What the wood is made of"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#The-forest-sector:-France-in-the-world","page":"Writing the model","text":"This example builds a complete spatial partial-equilibrium model of the forest-products sector with Markets.jl, solves it, and runs three scenarios on it. It is the first of three pages:\n\nthis one, on how to write the model and use it, with the kind of numbers that are at hand before any calibration effort;\nCalibrating with partial data, on turning incomplete, inconsistent statistics into the same model;\nComplete data, and what calibration changes, on what perfect information would take, and how much the scenarios depend on which of the three you have.\n\nwarning: For illustrative purposes only\nThe data in this example are plausible, but they are not statistics, and are not calibrated: what that means, and why it matters, is the subject of the last section. For simplicity, the structure of the model and its data are also written together here; the next pages read the data from files, as an application should.\n\nThe economy has eight regions. France is split into four regions and the rest of the world into four blocs:\n\n \nSEF South-east France — the Alps and the Massif Central, spruce and fir\nSWF South-west France — the Landes, Europe's largest maritime-pine plantation\nGEF Grand Est — the Vosges, and the oak and beech of the north-east\nOF the rest of France\nEU the rest of the European Union\nNA North America\nAS Asia-Pacific\nRW the rest of the world\n\nWhat makes the regional detail work is the way the varieties of a product substitute for each other, which is the subject of the Armington section below: French products are near-perfect substitutes for each other, close substitutes for the rest of the EU, and more distant substitutes for the other blocs.\n\nThe sector modelled here is the one the Global Forest Products Model established as the reference, Buongiorno et al. (2003); for the same economics in working open-source code, with different choices about dynamics and data, see TiMBA, [C3].\n\nQuantities are in million m³ for wood products and million tonnes for pulp, paper and pellets; prices are in USD per m³ or per tonne.\n\nusing Markets, DataFrames","title":"The forest sector: France in the world"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Regions-and-products","page":"Writing the model","text":"france  = [\"SEF\", \"SWF\", \"GEF\", \"OF\"]\nregions = [france..., \"EU\", \"NA\", \"AS\", \"RW\"]\n\nThe product list follows the FAOSTAT / UNECE chain, splitting roundwood by coniferous (softwood, swr) and non-coniferous (hardwood, hwr), because the two lead to different products.\n\nsmallwood is the third assortment the forest yields: small-diameter roundwood from thinnings and tops, too poor for sawing. In French statistics it is the bois d'industrie et bois énergie category, and the name says what matters here — the same log can be burnt in a stove or sent to a mill, and which it is depends on what the two uses are willing to pay.\n\nproducts = [\"swr\", \"hwr\", \"smallwood\", \"chips\", \"pulp\",\n            \"sawn_sw\", \"sawn_hw\", \"panel\", \"plywood\", \"paper\", \"pellets\"]\n\nThey are connected by the transformations set up further down:\n\n  FOREST ── swr ───────┬─ sawmill_sw ─→ sawn_sw + chips\n         ── hwr ───────┼─ sawmill_hw ─→ sawn_hw + chips\n         ── smallwood ─┤\n                       ├─ plymill    : CES(swr, hwr) ───────────────────→ plywood + chips\n                       ├─ panelmill  : CES(chips, smallwood, swr, hwr) ─→ panel\n                       ├─ pulpmill   : CES(swr, hwr, chips, smallwood) ─→ pulp ─→ papermill ─→ paper\n                       └─ pelletmill : CES(chips, smallwood, swr, hwr) ─→ pellets\n\n                          smallwood ─────────────────────────────────→ burnt as firewood\n\nTwo things in that picture are worth more than a passing glance.\n\nchips are a sawmill and plymill by-product and an input to three other mills, so the competition for residues is part of the equilibrium rather than an assumption about it.\n\nsmallwood is both a final product and an intermediate one. It comes out of the forest, and from there it either goes into a household stove as firewood — final demand, like sawnwood or paper — or into a mill as a raw material. The model does not need to be told which: both uses face the same local price, and the split between them is whatever that price makes optimal. A product in this engine may be primary-supplied, manufactured, consumed and used as an input all at once; its balance simply sums the sources and the uses.","title":"Regions and products"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Primary-supply","page":"Writing the model","text":"Roundwood coming out of the forest, as a constant-elasticity curve through a reference point: each region's harvest at its roadside price, which forest statistics give. The French regions harvest some 31 Mm³ of industrial roundwood between them: the Landes (SWF) dominate softwood, while GEF and OF hold most of the hardwood. Their supply elasticities are lower than the blocs' — French forests are fragmented between many small private owners, which makes the harvest slow to respond to price.\n\nsupply = SupplyCurve[\n    # softwood roundwood\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"swr\", region = \"SEF\", p0 =  95, q0 =   3.58, elasticity = 0.40),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"swr\", region = \"SWF\", p0 =  93, q0 =   8.79, elasticity = 0.45),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"swr\", region = \"GEF\", p0 =  96, q0 =   2.99, elasticity = 0.40),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"swr\", region = \"OF\",  p0 =  96, q0 =   3.86, elasticity = 0.40),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"swr\", region = \"EU\",  p0 =  98, q0 = 335.0,  elasticity = 0.60),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"swr\", region = \"NA\",  p0 =  94, q0 = 488.0,  elasticity = 0.60),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"swr\", region = \"AS\",  p0 = 135, q0 = 200.0,  elasticity = 0.55),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"swr\", region = \"RW\",  p0 = 103, q0 = 260.0,  elasticity = 0.55),\n    # hardwood roundwood\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"hwr\", region = \"SEF\", p0 = 111, q0 =   1.58, elasticity = 0.35),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"hwr\", region = \"SWF\", p0 = 112, q0 =   1.38, elasticity = 0.35),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"hwr\", region = \"GEF\", p0 = 108, q0 =   3.78, elasticity = 0.35),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"hwr\", region = \"OF\",  p0 = 108, q0 =   5.16, elasticity = 0.35),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"hwr\", region = \"EU\",  p0 = 117, q0 = 105.0,  elasticity = 0.50),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"hwr\", region = \"NA\",  p0 = 132, q0 =  94.7,  elasticity = 0.50),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"hwr\", region = \"AS\",  p0 = 131, q0 = 240.0,  elasticity = 0.50),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"hwr\", region = \"RW\",  p0 = 107, q0 = 166.0,  elasticity = 0.50),\n    # smallwood: thinnings and tops, worth less than a sawlog\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"smallwood\", region = \"SEF\", p0 = 61, q0 =   8.31, elasticity = 0.50),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"smallwood\", region = \"SWF\", p0 = 63, q0 =   5.35, elasticity = 0.55),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"smallwood\", region = \"GEF\", p0 = 62, q0 =   9.17, elasticity = 0.50),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"smallwood\", region = \"OF\",  p0 = 62, q0 =  12.5,  elasticity = 0.50),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"smallwood\", region = \"EU\",  p0 = 68, q0 = 244.0,  elasticity = 0.60),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"smallwood\", region = \"NA\",  p0 = 80, q0 = 137.0,  elasticity = 0.60),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"smallwood\", region = \"AS\",  p0 = 79, q0 = 317.0,  elasticity = 0.55),\n    SupplyCurve(product = \"smallwood\", region = \"RW\",  p0 = 64, q0 = 439.0,  elasticity = 0.55),\n]","title":"Primary supply"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Final-demand","page":"Writing the model","text":"Only finished products are consumed. Each region's consumption is known, and OF — which holds two thirds of the French population — is much the largest French market. For prices, what is usually at hand is one price per product: a world or national average, the same for every region.\n\nreference = Dict(\n    # product    => (price, quantities by region)\n    \"sawn_sw\"   => (205.0, Dict(\"SEF\" => 1.95,  \"SWF\" => 0.931,  \"GEF\" => 0.815,  \"OF\" => 5.66,\n                               \"EU\"  => 80.4,  \"NA\"  => 134.0,  \"AS\"  => 45.5,   \"RW\" => 40.3)),\n    \"sawn_hw\"   => (280.0, Dict(\"SEF\" => 0.228, \"SWF\" => 0.108,  \"GEF\" => 0.101,  \"OF\" => 0.658,\n                               \"EU\"  => 10.7,  \"NA\"  => 15.4,   \"AS\"  => 53.3,   \"RW\" => 15.4)),\n    \"panel\"     => (240.0, Dict(\"SEF\" => 1.03,  \"SWF\" => 0.461,  \"GEF\" => 0.465,  \"OF\" => 3.29,\n                               \"EU\"  => 48.9,  \"NA\"  => 43.0,   \"AS\"  => 110.0,  \"RW\" => 29.1)),\n    # plywood: veneers glued crosswise, dearer than particleboard and used\n    # where a panel has to carry a load\n    \"plywood\"   => (285.0, Dict(\"SEF\" => 0.196, \"SWF\" => 0.0952, \"GEF\" => 0.0811, \"OF\" => 0.562,\n                               \"EU\"  => 10.2,  \"NA\"  => 19.8,   \"AS\"  => 93.2,   \"RW\" => 19.4)),\n    \"paper\"     => (935.0, Dict(\"SEF\" => 1.83,  \"SWF\" => 0.814,  \"GEF\" => 0.763,  \"OF\" => 5.71,\n                               \"EU\"  => 71.1,  \"NA\"  => 81.4,   \"AS\"  => 132.0,  \"RW\" => 41.8)),\n    # firewood: smallwood bought and burnt as it comes, the oldest use of all\n    \"smallwood\" => (70.0,  Dict(\"SEF\" => 2.36,  \"SWF\" => 1.07,   \"GEF\" => 1.99,   \"OF\" => 5.05,\n                               \"EU\"  => 59.3,  \"NA\"  => 17.1,   \"AS\"  => 97.6,   \"RW\" => 166.0)),\n    # pellets: the same energy, dried, ground and compressed\n    \"pellets\"   => (285.0, Dict(\"SEF\" => 0.637, \"SWF\" => 0.322,  \"GEF\" => 0.395,  \"OF\" => 1.73,\n                               \"EU\"  => 27.5,  \"NA\"  => 12.3,   \"AS\"  => 20.3,   \"RW\" => 7.26)),\n)\n\nThe own-price elasticities are below 1: demand for forest products is usually found to be price-inelastic, construction lumber and paper most of all, since their cost is a small part of the building or the printed product they end up in. An inelastic demand makes prices, rather than quantities, absorb a supply shock — which matters for the storm scenario at the end.","title":"Final demand"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Products-that-compete-for-the-same-job","page":"Writing the model","text":"Those seven products are not seven separate markets. Softwood lumber and panels do the same job in a building and a builder swaps one for the other readily; hardwood lumber is a more distant substitute for either. Pellets and firewood are the same heat in different packaging. Paper competes with nothing else here.\n\nThat is a DemandNest each: a tree of products with one demand curve on the bundle. It has to be a tree rather than a matrix of cross-price elasticities, for the reason set out in Substitution in final demand — a free matrix is not the gradient of any benefit function, and the model would stop being the market it claims to represent.","title":"Products that compete for the same job"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Calibrating-a-bundle","page":"Writing the model","text":"A demand curve over a bundle needs three numbers that no statistic reports: the shares of its members, and the price and quantity of the bundle itself, which are the p0 and q0 of its curve. What the reference table gives instead is a price and a quantity for each product. ces_calibration turns the second into the first, choosing the three so that at the base-year prices the model buys exactly the base-year quantities. For the wood fuel of OF, 5.05 Mm³ of firewood at 70 and 1.73 Mt of pellets at 285:\n\nces_calibration(quantities = [5.05, 1.73], prices = [70.0, 285.0], sigma = 3)\n(shares = [0.041458696774834, 0.958541303225166], price = 222.15629247442956, quantity = 3.8106055451813896, scale = 1.7792447734648071)\n\nThe shares are what each product's share would be if both cost the same. Pellets get 96% of the bundle although households buy three times more firewood: they are bought little because they are four times dearer, and would be bought far more at an equal price.\nThe price is the CES price index of the bundle — the cost of one unit of it at the base-year prices.\nThe quantity is the base-year spending, 847, divided by that price. It is an index number, not a volume: m³ of firewood and tonnes of pellets would mean nothing added up, while their values at a common price do. That is what a CES composite is.\n\nFeed those back into the CES demand condition at the base-year prices and out come exactly 5.05 and 1.73.","title":"Calibrating a bundle"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Wood-in-buildings","page":"Writing the model","text":"Construction is a nest inside a nest — softwood lumber, particleboard and plywood together at σ = 5, hardwood lumber against that group at σ = 1.6. The three in the inner group are what a builder actually chooses between for a floor or a wall; appearance-grade hardwood is a different kind of purchase.\n\nA nested bundle is calibrated from the inside out. The inner group is calibrated on its three products; its price and quantity then make it one member of the outer bundle, next to hardwood lumber; and the outer price and quantity are the reference point of the curve:\n\nσ_lumber, σ_construction, σ_fuel = 5.0, 1.6, 3.0\nlumber = [\"sawn_sw\", \"panel\", \"plywood\"]\n\nfunction construction_demand(r)\n    q(p) = reference[p][2][r]                    # base-year quantity in region r\n    price(p) = reference[p][1]                   # base-year price\n    inner = ces_calibration(quantities = q.(lumber), prices = price.(lumber),\n                            sigma = σ_lumber)\n    outer = ces_calibration(quantities = [q(\"sawn_hw\"), inner.quantity],\n                            prices     = [price(\"sawn_hw\"), inner.price],\n                            sigma = σ_construction)\n    return DemandCurve(region = r, p0 = outer.price, q0 = outer.quantity, elasticity = 0.45,\n        product = DemandNest(sigma = σ_construction,\n            products = [\"sawn_hw\"], shares = [outer.shares[1]],\n            nests = [DemandNest(sigma = σ_lumber, share = outer.shares[2],\n                                products = lumber, shares = inner.shares)]))\nend\nconstruction_demand (generic function with 1 method)\n\nThe elasticity of 0.45 is the own-price elasticity of the whole bundle: how much less wood goes into buildings when all of it gets dearer. How the bundle splits when only one product gets dearer is the job of the two σ.","title":"Wood in buildings"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Wood-for-heat","page":"Writing the model","text":"Wood fuel is a single nest, and the one place where a price from outside the model matters: firewood and pellets both compete with gas and oil, whose prices this sector does not determine. An exogenous price needs no nest at all — being a constant, it shifts the whole curve — so the cross-price elasticity goes into exogenous_shift. Here gas sits at its reference level, which leaves the shifter at exactly 1; the scenario at the end moves it.\n\ngas_price, gas_reference, gas_cross_elasticity = 30.0, 30.0, 0.35\nfuels = [\"smallwood\", \"pellets\"]\n\nfunction fuel_demand(r, gas = gas_price)\n    fuel = ces_calibration(quantities = [reference[p][2][r] for p in fuels],\n                           prices     = [reference[p][1] for p in fuels], sigma = σ_fuel)\n    return DemandCurve(region = r, p0 = fuel.price, q0 = fuel.quantity, elasticity = 0.35,\n        shift = exogenous_shift(gas = (level = gas, reference = gas_reference,\n                                       elasticity = gas_cross_elasticity)),\n        product = DemandNest(sigma = σ_fuel, products = fuels, shares = fuel.shares))\nend\nfuel_demand (generic function with 2 methods)","title":"Wood for heat"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#All-of-final-demand","page":"Writing the model","text":"Paper competes with nothing here, so it keeps a plain curve through its own reference point. The whole demand side is then one curve per bundle and region. It is built by a function of the gas price rather than written out once, so that the gas scenario at the end can rebuild it at another price:\n\nbuild_demand(gas = gas_price) =\n    [[construction_demand(r) for r in regions];\n     [fuel_demand(r, gas) for r in regions];\n     [DemandCurve(product = \"paper\", region = r, p0 = reference[\"paper\"][1],\n                 q0 = reference[\"paper\"][2][r], elasticity = 0.30) for r in regions]]\n\ndemand = build_demand()","title":"All of final demand"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Processes","page":"Writing the model","text":"Six transformations, of three kinds.\n\nFixed proportions with a by-product. Sawmilling turns a log into lumber and chips in set amounts. The plywood mill is the same shape — veneer is peeled from the log, and what is left, nearly half of it, comes out as chips — except that its input is a CES bundle of the two roundwood grades, because a veneer mill can work softwood or hardwood and will lean toward whichever is cheaper.\n\nA CES bundle of wood. Panel, pulp and pellet mills draw on a mix of chips, smallwood and roundwood, so when chips become scarce — because the other mills are bidding for them — each shifts smoothly toward roundwood.\n\nPlywood sits on both sides of that: it sheds chips, which helps the pulp mill, and bids for the same logs, which does not. Which effect wins is not something to guess at; it is the kind of link a sector model exists to settle.\n\nThe recipes below — the shares of each wood in a bundle, the value-added costs — are reasonable guesses, the same in every region. The next page works them out from data instead.\n\nFixed proportions, single input. The paper mill takes pulp and nothing else.\n\nPulp mills are capital-heavy and few: in France only the Landes (SWF) and a handful of sites elsewhere (OF) have the capacity, which the regions argument expresses. SEF and GEF therefore have no pulp of their own and must buy it from somewhere else.\n\npulp_regions = [\"SWF\", \"OF\", \"EU\", \"NA\", \"AS\", \"RW\"]\n6-element Vector{String}:\n \"SWF\"\n \"OF\"\n \"EU\"\n \"NA\"\n \"AS\"\n \"RW\"\n\nPellet mills, by contrast, exist everywhere — but only so many of them. A Process with constant returns would pile into whichever region makes pellets most cheaply; the installed plant is what stops it, region by region. The plants' capacity, in Mt of pellets a year, becomes a limit on the mills' activity further down:\n\npellet_capacity = Dict(\"SEF\" => 0.427, \"SWF\" => 0.50, \"GEF\" => 0.349, \"OF\" => 0.645,\n                       \"EU\"  => 28.3,  \"NA\"  => 15.3, \"AS\"  => 19.9,  \"RW\" => 14.1)\n\nprocesses = [\n    Process(name    = \"sawmill_sw\",\n            inputs  = [leontief(product = \"swr\", coeff = 1.0)],\n            outputs = [\"sawn_sw\" => 0.50, \"chips\" => 0.35],\n            vacost  = 40),\n    Process(name    = \"sawmill_hw\",\n            inputs  = [leontief(product = \"hwr\", coeff = 1.0)],\n            outputs = [\"sawn_hw\" => 0.45, \"chips\" => 0.35],\n            vacost  = 45),\n    # Plywood is peeled from veneer logs, so it takes roundwood and nothing\n    # else — and leaves a great deal of it behind as chips, which the panel,\n    # pulp and pellet mills then bid for.\n    Process(name    = \"plymill\",\n            inputs  = [ces(composite = 2.0,\n                           products  = [\"swr\", \"hwr\"],\n                           shares    = [0.45, 0.55],\n                           sigma     = 1.8)],\n            outputs = [\"plywood\" => 1.0, \"chips\" => 0.8],\n            vacost  = 180),\n    Process(name    = \"panelmill\",\n            inputs  = [ces(composite = 1.30,\n                           products  = [\"chips\", \"smallwood\", \"swr\", \"hwr\"],\n                           shares    = [0.40, 0.25, 0.23, 0.12],\n                           sigma     = 2.5)],\n            outputs = [\"panel\" => 1.0],\n            vacost  = 120),\n    Process(name    = \"pulpmill\",\n            regions = pulp_regions,\n            inputs  = [ces(composite = 4.0,\n                           products  = [\"swr\", \"hwr\", \"chips\", \"smallwood\"],\n                           shares    = [0.35, 0.22, 0.23, 0.20],\n                           sigma     = 1.8)],\n            outputs = [\"pulp\" => 1.0],\n            vacost  = 300),\n    Process(name    = \"papermill\",\n            inputs  = [leontief(product = \"pulp\", coeff = 1.10)],\n            outputs = [\"paper\" => 1.0],\n            vacost  = 200),\n    # the pellet mill bids for the same wood as the panel and pulp mills, and\n    # for the same smallwood that households would otherwise burn as it comes\n    Process(name    = \"pelletmill\",\n            inputs  = [ces(composite = 2.2,            # m³ of wood per tonne\n                           products  = [\"chips\", \"smallwood\", \"swr\", \"hwr\"],\n                           shares    = [0.40, 0.40, 0.12, 0.08],\n                           sigma     = 3.0)],\n            outputs = [\"pellets\" => 1.0],\n            vacost  = 90),\n]","title":"Processes"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Distance-and-transport-costs","page":"Writing the model","text":"Distances are relative, with the Europe–North America crossing as the unit. The French regions are an order of magnitude closer to each other than to anywhere else, which is what will keep most French wood inside France.\n\ndist = Dict(\n    (\"SEF\", \"SWF\") => 0.12, (\"SEF\", \"GEF\") => 0.10, (\"SEF\", \"OF\") => 0.08,\n    (\"SWF\", \"GEF\") => 0.14, (\"SWF\", \"OF\")  => 0.08, (\"GEF\", \"OF\") => 0.07,\n    (\"SEF\", \"EU\")  => 0.22, (\"SWF\", \"EU\")  => 0.26, (\"GEF\", \"EU\") => 0.18, (\"OF\", \"EU\") => 0.22,\n    (\"SEF\", \"NA\")  => 1.10, (\"SWF\", \"NA\")  => 1.00, (\"GEF\", \"NA\") => 1.12, (\"OF\", \"NA\") => 1.05,\n    (\"SEF\", \"AS\")  => 1.28, (\"SWF\", \"AS\")  => 1.35, (\"GEF\", \"AS\") => 1.30, (\"OF\", \"AS\") => 1.32,\n    (\"SEF\", \"RW\")  => 0.82, (\"SWF\", \"RW\")  => 0.85, (\"GEF\", \"RW\") => 0.88, (\"OF\", \"RW\") => 0.85,\n    (\"EU\", \"NA\")   => 1.00, (\"EU\", \"AS\")   => 1.20, (\"EU\", \"RW\")  => 0.80,\n    (\"NA\", \"AS\")   => 1.10, (\"NA\", \"RW\")   => 1.00, (\"AS\", \"RW\")  => 0.95,\n)\ndistance(a, b) = a == b ? 0.0 : get(dist, (a, b), get(dist, (b, a), 0.0))\n\nFreight is charged per unit of quantity, not of value, because shipping a m³ of logs costs much the same whether the logs are cheap or dear. Bulky, low-value products therefore carry a high transport cost relative to their price, which is what keeps roundwood trade regional and lets paper travel.\n\nfreight = Dict(\"swr\" => 12.0, \"hwr\" => 12.0, \"smallwood\" => 13.0, \"chips\" => 14.0,\n               \"pulp\" => 35.0, \"sawn_sw\" => 30.0, \"sawn_hw\" => 32.0, \"panel\" => 35.0,\n               \"plywood\" => 38.0, \"paper\" => 55.0, \"pellets\" => 28.0)\n\ntransport_costs = Dict((p, a, b) => freight[p] * distance(a, b)\n                       for p in products, a in regions, b in regions if a != b)\nlength(transport_costs)\n616","title":"Distance and transport costs"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Who-substitutes-for-whom","page":"Writing the model","text":"This is where the regional detail earns its place. If the varieties of a product were perfect substitutes everywhere, each market would simply buy from the cheapest source and a small cost advantage would take everything. If instead they were all imperfect substitutes with one elasticity, French oak would be no closer to French pine than to Asian hardwood, which is just as wrong.\n\nWhat the data say is a hierarchy:\n\ninside France the product of one region is nearly the same good as that of another — a m³ of Landes pine and a m³ of Vosges pine of the same grade are all but interchangeable, and mostly transport separates them;\nbetween France and the rest of the EU the products are close but not identical: shared standards and certification, short distances, but different species mixes and supply contracts;\nbetween the big blocs they are more distant substitutes: different species, grading rules, phytosanitary requirements and commercial habits.\n\nThat hierarchy is a tree, which is exactly what nested OriginNests express: the French regions together at a very high elasticity, then France and the rest of the EU at a high one, then Europe as a whole against the other blocs at a lower one.\n\nσ_blocs = Dict(\"swr\" => 6.0, \"hwr\" => 6.0, \"smallwood\" => 7.0, \"chips\" => 7.0,\n               \"pulp\" => 5.0, \"sawn_sw\" => 3.5, \"sawn_hw\" => 3.0, \"panel\" => 3.0,\n               \"plywood\" => 2.5, \"paper\" => 4.0, \"pellets\" => 6.0)\nDict{String, Float64} with 11 entries:\n  \"pellets\"   => 6.0\n  \"paper\"     => 4.0\n  \"smallwood\" => 7.0\n  \"sawn_sw\"   => 3.5\n  \"sawn_hw\"   => 3.0\n  \"panel\"     => 3.0\n  \"hwr\"       => 6.0\n  \"swr\"       => 6.0\n  \"chips\"     => 7.0\n  \"pulp\"      => 5.0\n  \"plywood\"   => 2.5\n\nCommodities graded to a standard (chips, roundwood, pulp) substitute readily between blocs; manufactured products, where origin carries a reputation, do so less. Within Europe every product substitutes three times more easily than between blocs, and within France more easily still:\n\nσ_europe = Dict(p => 3σ for (p, σ) in σ_blocs)\nσ_france = 30.0\n\neurope(p) = OriginNest(sigma   = σ_europe[p],\n                       origins = [\"EU\"],\n                       nests   = [OriginNest(sigma = σ_france, origins = france)])\n\nNote the direction of the inequality the package enforces: a group must hold together at least as tightly as it holds to the outside, 30 ≥ 3σ ≥ σ. The reverse would say that members of a group are worse substitutes for each other than for outsiders, which no cost function can represent.\n\nWhy not make the French regions perfect substitutes, sigma = Inf, and France a single market up to transport? Because with mills that have constant returns, that can leave the model unable to say where in France the milling happens — see Perfect substitutes and constant returns for why, and for the remedies. A finite elasticity, however high, gives each region's product a demand of its own, which settles it.\n\nThe shares are the other half of the specification: they say how much of each origin a market would take if all delivered prices were equal, so they carry the home bias and the trade pattern that prices alone do not explain. Calibrating them properly means fitting a base-year trade matrix, which is what the next page does; here they come from a gravity rule — big suppliers and near ones weigh more, and a region leans toward itself.\n\nsize_of = Dict(r => sum(s.q0 for s in supply if s.region == r) for r in regions)\ndecay, home_bias = 1.4, 3.0\nshares = Dict((o, r) => size_of[o] * exp(-decay * distance(o, r)) * (o == r ? home_bias : 1.0)\n              for o in regions, r in regions)\n\nOne Armington specification per product then assembles the three levels. The same structure applies to every destination here, but it need not — a destination argument would let the EU market treat a bloc differently from how that bloc treats the EU, which is what non-tariff measures do. The gravity rule gives every pair a share, but a region that mills no pulp has no pulp variety to offer: the engine would drop such a share, and validate would warn about it, so the pulp shares are kept to the regions that make it.\n\noffers(p) = p == \"pulp\" ? pulp_regions : regions\narmington = [Armington(product = p,\n                       sigma   = σ_blocs[p],\n                       shares  = Dict(k => v for (k, v) in shares if k[1] in offers(p)),\n                       nests   = [europe(p)])\n             for p in products]","title":"Who substitutes for whom"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#What-cannot-move,-and-what-is-taxed","page":"Writing the model","text":"Three final things stand between the economy as described and the equilibrium.","title":"What cannot move, and what is taxed"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#What-the-forest-holds","page":"Writing the model","text":"The supply curves say how much wood owners are willing to sell at each price. They say nothing of how much there is: a year's harvest cannot take more than the stands hold. That limit is not one per product, because the products share their stands. Softwood logs come from conifers, hardwood logs from broadleaves, and smallwood — thinnings and tops — from both, so a m³ of conifer can be sold as a log or as smallwood, but not as both.\n\nHere is what each region can cut in a year, in Mm³, by kind of stand — about a fifth more than it cuts in a normal year:\n\nstock = Dict(\"SEF\" => (conifers =  11.0, broadleaves =   6.0),\n             \"SWF\" => (conifers =  16.0, broadleaves =   4.0),\n             \"GEF\" => (conifers =   7.0, broadleaves =  13.0),\n             \"OF\"  => (conifers =   9.0, broadleaves =  18.0),\n             \"EU\"  => (conifers = 520.0, broadleaves = 340.0),\n             \"NA\"  => (conifers = 700.0, broadleaves = 190.0),\n             \"AS\"  => (conifers = 360.0, broadleaves = 570.0),\n             \"RW\"  => (conifers = 480.0, broadleaves = 610.0))\nstands_of = Dict(\"swr\" => [:conifers], \"hwr\" => [:broadleaves],\n                 \"smallwood\" => [:conifers, :broadleaves])\nprimary = [\"swr\", \"hwr\", \"smallwood\"]\n\nThe rule that keeps every m³ counted once is the one of the Forest Sector Model FFSM: for every combination of the primary products, their total harvest cannot exceed the stock of all the stands at least one of them can come from. Softwood logs alone are bounded by the conifers; softwood logs and smallwood together by conifers and broadleaves, since smallwood can come from either. Checking each product on its own would not do — two products may each fit while together drawing on the same stands beyond what they hold — and checking every combination is exactly enough: by Hall's theorem, the harvests then fit into the stands without any m³ being counted twice.\n\nWith three products that is seven combinations per region. Each is a @limit: a linear constraint on the quantities of the model, here on the sum of the supplies of a combination. Inside @limit, supply(…) names the supply of the model, not the curves of the same name above:\n\ncombinations(xs) = [xs[[isodd(i >> (j - 1)) for j in eachindex(xs)]]\n                    for i in 1:(2^length(xs) - 1)]\navailable(r, C, stock) = sum(getfield(stock[r], s) for s in union((stands_of[p] for p in C)...))\n\nforest_limits(stock) =\n    [@limit(\"$r: \" * join(C, \" + \"),\n            sum(supply(region = r, product = p) for p in C) <= available(r, C, stock))\n     for r in regions for C in combinations(primary)]\n\nforest_limits(stock)[1:7]\n7-element Vector{Limit}:\n Limit(\"SEF: swr\": supply(region = SEF, product = swr) ≤ 11.0)\n Limit(\"SEF: hwr\": supply(region = SEF, product = hwr) ≤ 6.0)\n Limit(\"SEF: swr + hwr\": supply(region = SEF, product = swr) + supply(region = SEF, product = hwr) ≤ 17.0)\n Limit(\"SEF: smallwood\": supply(region = SEF, product = smallwood) ≤ 17.0)\n Limit(\"SEF: swr + smallwood\": supply(region = SEF, product = swr) + supply(region = SEF, product = smallwood) ≤ 17.0)\n Limit(\"SEF: hwr + smallwood\": supply(region = SEF, product = hwr) + supply(region = SEF, product = smallwood) ≤ 17.0)\n Limit(\"SEF: swr + hwr + smallwood\": supply(region = SEF, product = swr) + supply(region = SEF, product = hwr) + supply(region = SEF, product = smallwood) ≤ 17.0)","title":"What the forest holds"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Plants,-logistics-and-policy","page":"Writing the model","text":"The pellet plants are the simplest kind of limit: one per region, on the activity of its pellet mills. A family of limits, one per region, is what @limits writes — pellet_plant[SEF], pellet_plant[SWF], and so on:\n\npellet_limits = @limits \"pellet_plant\" [r in regions] activity(region = r, process = \"pelletmill\") <=\n                                                      pellet_capacity[r]\npellet_limits[1:2]\n2-element Vector{Limit}:\n Limit(\"pellet_plant[SEF]\": activity(region = SEF, process = pelletmill) ≤ 0.427)\n Limit(\"pellet_plant[SWF]\": activity(region = SWF, process = pelletmill) ≤ 0.5)\n\nWood leaving the Landes has to be carried on the roads and through the ports that are there. One limit caps the sum of what SWF exports of the two products that travel by road, wherever it goes. At 18 Mm³ it is slack in a normal year — the region ships about 13 — and the storm scenario at the end is what makes it bite.\n\nevacuation = @limit(\"landes_evacuation\",\n                    exports(region = \"SWF\", product = [\"swr\", \"smallwood\"]) <= 18.0)\n\nAll the limits of the economy, for a given stock of the stands — the scenarios below change the stock and nothing else:\n\nall_limits(stock) = [forest_limits(stock); pellet_limits; evacuation]\n\nAnd the rest of the world taxes its log exports, as several timber exporters do, to keep the wood at home for its own mills. The rate is ad valorem, a quarter of the price at the border, which is the one kind of wedge this model cannot write into its objective — the price is a dual of the problem. It is found by iteration instead, which solve_market does on its own; see Why ad valorem rates on trade are special.\n\ntaxes = [Tax(name = \"rw_log_duty\", on = \"trade\", product = [\"swr\", \"hwr\"], from = \"RW\",\n             rate = 0.25, relative = true)]\n1-element Vector{Tax}:\n Tax(\"rw_log_duty\", \"trade\", 0.25, true, [\"swr\", \"hwr\"], Colon(), Colon(), \"RW\", Colon())","title":"Plants, logistics and policy"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Solving","page":"Writing the model","text":"example_market = MarketData(; regions, products, demand, supply, processes,\n                              transport_costs, armington, taxes,\n                              limits = all_limits(stock))\n\nres = solve_market(example_market)","title":"Solving"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Prices","page":"Writing the model","text":"Because the products are differentiated by origin, two prices coexist in every region: what local users pay for the composite they consume, and what local producers are paid for their own variety.\n\nunstack(res.prices, :region, :product, :price)\n\u001b[1m8×12 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m chips    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m hwr      \u001b[0m\u001b[1m panel    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m paper    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m pellets  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m plywood  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m pul\u001b[0m ⋯\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Flo\u001b[0m ⋯\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ AS       125.313   129.368   264.878  1027.48    309.388   339.127   75 ⋯\n   2 │ EU       116.723   119.751   252.611   990.118   291.127   318.532   70\n   3 │ GEF      120.923   123.595   265.074  1011.03    301.082   332.534   72\n   4 │ NA       106.274   130.144   254.242   994.822   299.933   330.009   71\n   5 │ OF       120.894   123.591   265.664  1011.89    301.649   333.279   72 ⋯\n   6 │ RW       117.98    106.808   247.793   977.491   282.161   306.028   69\n   7 │ SEF      121.343   124.774   265.95   1012.17    301.723   333.309   72\n   8 │ SWF      121.237   125.186   266.855  1013.53    302.722   334.567   72\n\u001b[36m                                                               5 columns omitted\u001b[0m\n\nunstack(res.prices, :region, :product, :producer_price)\n\u001b[1m8×12 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m chips    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m hwr      \u001b[0m\u001b[1m panel    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m paper    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m pellets  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m plywood  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m pul\u001b[0m ⋯\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64? \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Flo\u001b[0m ⋯\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ AS       125.136   127.816   261.472  1026.48    308.482   337.096      ⋯\n   2 │ EU       114.753   116.569   245.855   978.129   286.033   310.715\n   3 │ GEF      114.348   106.648   250.088   993.882   291.649   320.375 \u001b[90m mis\u001b[0m\n   4 │ NA       103.246   128.894   247.899   983.864   296.497   325.04\n   5 │ OF       113.675   106.519   249.992   994.178   291.37    321.038      ⋯\n   6 │ RW       116.558   103.704   241.156   963.787   276.181   296.284\n   7 │ SEF      114.565   109.198   250.603   994.559   292.262   322.03  \u001b[90m mis\u001b[0m\n   8 │ SWF      114.315   110.036   250.822   995.267   293.113   321.863\n\u001b[36m                                                               5 columns omitted\u001b[0m\n\nThe missing entries are not a failure: SEF and GEF have no pulp mill, so they have no pulp of their own to price. They buy it, and the first table gives what they pay.\n\nNotice how close the four French rows are to each other in both tables, and how far they are from NA or AS. That is the nest at work.","title":"Prices"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Quantities","page":"Writing the model","text":"unstack(res.production, :region, :product, :quantity)\n\u001b[1m8×12 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m swr       \u001b[0m\u001b[1m hwr       \u001b[0m\u001b[1m smallwood \u001b[0m\u001b[1m chips      \u001b[0m\u001b[1m sawn_sw    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m sawn_h\u001b[0m ⋯\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64?  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64?  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64?  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64?   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64?   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float6\u001b[0m ⋯\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ SEF       3.57778    1.57098    8.39734    0.372819    0.343713   0.081 ⋯\n   2 │ SWF       8.74602    1.37148    5.36497    0.421258    0.42313    0.073\n   3 │ GEF       2.99303    3.76337    9.22115    0.49321     0.452164   0.115\n   4 │ OF        3.86505    5.13511   12.574      0.787167    0.760791   0.172\n   5 │ EU      337.204    104.806    246.973     80.5284     88.2619    13.266 ⋯\n   6 │ NA      491.355     93.5794   137.915    118.188     137.372     13.876\n   7 │ AS      196.397    237.066    316.322    110.339      33.0813    49.317\n   8 │ RW      256.443    163.424    438.703     77.2525     57.8462    24.963\n\u001b[36m                                                               6 columns omitted\u001b[0m\n\nunstack(res.consumption, :region, :product, :quantity)\n\u001b[1m8×8 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m panel      \u001b[0m\u001b[1m paper      \u001b[0m\u001b[1m pellets   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m plywood    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m sawn_hw   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m sawn_\u001b[0m ⋯\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64?   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64?   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64?  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64?   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64?  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float\u001b[0m ⋯\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ AS      124.229     128.318     20.9052    72.2492     57.1197     34.2 ⋯\n   2 │ EU       45.8852     69.8887    27.0894     7.08959    11.0793     84.9\n   3 │ GEF       0.432823    0.745314   0.376368   0.0573725   0.104825    0.8\n   4 │ NA       40.2118     79.8995    12.8253    11.8665     14.0949    145.4\n   5 │ OF        3.06646     5.57622    1.65128    0.398081    0.682751    5.6 ⋯\n   6 │ RW       28.5803     41.2464     6.47421   15.6596     17.9882     42.0\n   7 │ SEF       0.958898    1.78697    0.608857   0.139355    0.237321    1.9\n   8 │ SWF       0.427767    0.794542   0.309466   0.0673399   0.11179     0.9\n\u001b[36m                                                               2 columns omitted\u001b[0m","title":"Quantities"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#What-binds,-and-what-it-is-worth","page":"Writing the model","text":"Several of the things set up above are limits rather than curves, and the solution says what each is worth at the margin.\n\nThe pellet mills first. Where the installed plant is enough, the capacity shows no rent; where it is not, the bound binds and the rent is what another tonne of annual capacity would earn its owner — the signal that would, in a model with investment, build the next mill. In the reference run every plant has room to spare:\n\nres.activity[res.activity.process .== \"pelletmill\", :]\n\u001b[1m8×5 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m process    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m level     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m capacity \u001b[0m\u001b[1m rent    \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ SEF     pelletmill   0.120275     0.427      0.0\n   2 │ SWF     pelletmill   0.108606     0.5        0.0\n   3 │ GEF     pelletmill   0.14982      0.349      0.0\n   4 │ OF      pelletmill   0.268476     0.645      0.0\n   5 │ EU      pelletmill  26.3239      28.3        0.0\n   6 │ NA      pelletmill  13.0833      15.3        0.0\n   7 │ AS      pelletmill  18.4897      19.9        0.0\n   8 │ RW      pelletmill  11.9493      14.1        0.0\n\nThe limits come next, the evacuation ceiling of the Landes and the stands of every region together. Their rent is what one more unit of the bound would be worth; all are slack in a normal year, so all are zero:\n\nres.limits[res.limits.rent .!= 0, :]\n\u001b[1m0×9 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m limit  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m lower   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m upper   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m penalty \u001b[0m\u001b[1m value   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m excess  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m rent    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m regions \u001b[0m\u001b[1m \u001b[0m ⋯\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Array…  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m \u001b[0m ⋯\n─────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\u001b[36m                                                                1 column omitted\u001b[0m\n\nThe evacuation ceiling, for one: the region ships about 13 Mm³ of roundwood against a ceiling of 18, so it costs nothing and earns nothing.\n\nres.limits[res.limits.limit .== \"landes_evacuation\", :]\n\u001b[1m1×9 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m limit             \u001b[0m\u001b[1m lower   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m upper   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m penalty \u001b[0m\u001b[1m value   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m excess  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m rent   \u001b[0m ⋯\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String            \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64\u001b[0m ⋯\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ landes_evacuation     -Inf     18.0      Inf  12.7817      0.0      0.0 ⋯\n\u001b[36m                                                               2 columns omitted\u001b[0m\n\nAnd the export duty on logs leaving the rest of the world raises this, at a quarter of the border price on every m³ that still crosses:\n\nres.taxes\n\u001b[1m1×3 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m tax         \u001b[0m\u001b[1m on     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m revenue \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String      \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\n─────┼──────────────────────────────\n   1 │ rw_log_duty  trade   1202.48\n\nThat revenue is a transfer, not a cost: the program subtracts it like any other cost, so the objective of this model is welfare minus the revenue rather than welfare. Adding it back is what makes a taxed run comparable with an untaxed one.","title":"What binds, and what it is worth"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Firewood-or-raw-material?","page":"Writing the model","text":"smallwood is the product that has to choose. Every region's harvest of it is split between the stove and the mills by nothing more than the local price, and the split comes out very differently from place to place:\n\nquantity_of(table, r, p) = (rows = table[(table.region .== r) .& (table.product .== p),\n                                            :quantity];\n                            isempty(rows) ? 0.0 : only(rows))\nshipped(direction, r) = sum(res.trade[(res.trade.product .== \"smallwood\") .&\n                                      (res.trade[!, direction] .== r), :quantity]; init = 0.0)\n\nsmallwood_use = DataFrame(\n    (region      = r,\n     harvest     = quantity_of(res.production, r, \"smallwood\"),\n     firewood    = quantity_of(res.consumption, r, \"smallwood\"),\n     net_exports = shipped(:from, r) - shipped(:to, r))\n    for r in regions)\nsmallwood_use.to_the_mills =\n    smallwood_use.harvest .- smallwood_use.firewood .- smallwood_use.net_exports\nsmallwood_use\n\u001b[1m8×5 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m harvest   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m firewood  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m net_exports \u001b[0m\u001b[1m to_the_mills \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64      \u001b[0m\n─────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ SEF       8.39734    2.39363      5.36557      0.638143\n   2 │ SWF       5.36497    1.07723      3.09677      1.19098\n   3 │ GEF       9.22115    2.01149      6.49966      0.710004\n   4 │ OF       12.574      5.17694      4.87559      2.52145\n   5 │ EU      246.973     59.8762     -16.6932     203.79\n   6 │ NA      137.915     13.4082     -22.2119     146.719\n   7 │ AS      316.322     88.8014     -47.8629     275.383\n   8 │ RW      438.703    173.067       66.9303     198.706\n\nThe shares are worth reading off. North America burns a tenth of what it cuts, and imports wood for its mills on top; Asia-Pacific and the rest of the world burn between a quarter and two fifths. The French regions burn between a fifth (SWF, GEF) and two fifths (OF, where most of the population is) — and export most of what is left rather than milling it, because French industry is small next to its neighbours' and the wood is worth more across the border.\n\nNone of that was imposed. The same wood is offered to the stove and to the mill at one price, and the quantities follow from which use outbids the other where.\n\nThe pellet mills are bidding for that same wood, which is what makes the competition interesting — a pellet is wood that has been dried and pressed rather than burnt as it came, so the two uses are close substitutes in energy terms but reach the consumer as different products:\n\nres.activity[res.activity.process .== \"pelletmill\", :]\n\u001b[1m8×5 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m process    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m level     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m capacity \u001b[0m\u001b[1m rent    \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ SEF     pelletmill   0.120275     0.427      0.0\n   2 │ SWF     pelletmill   0.108606     0.5        0.0\n   3 │ GEF     pelletmill   0.14982      0.349      0.0\n   4 │ OF      pelletmill   0.268476     0.645      0.0\n   5 │ EU      pelletmill  26.3239      28.3        0.0\n   6 │ NA      pelletmill  13.0833      15.3        0.0\n   7 │ AS      pelletmill  18.4897      19.9        0.0\n   8 │ RW      pelletmill  11.9493      14.1        0.0","title":"Firewood or raw material?"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Trade","page":"Writing the model","text":"The ten largest flows:\n\nfirst(sort(res.trade, :quantity, rev = true), 10)\n\u001b[1m10×6 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m product   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m from   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m to     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m quantity \u001b[0m\u001b[1m capacity \u001b[0m\u001b[1m rent    \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ swr        NA      AS       39.6317       Inf      0.0\n   2 │ smallwood  RW      AS       38.6518       Inf      0.0\n   3 │ swr        EU      AS       23.9559       Inf      0.0\n   4 │ smallwood  RW      EU       19.551        Inf      0.0\n   5 │ smallwood  RW      NA       17.9877       Inf      0.0\n   6 │ swr        NA      EU       15.6065       Inf      0.0\n   7 │ swr        RW      AS       14.3456       Inf      0.0\n   8 │ pulp       RW      AS       12.8728       Inf      0.0\n   9 │ hwr        RW      AS       12.8246       Inf      0.0\n  10 │ swr        EU      NA       12.3858       Inf      0.0\n\nFrance as a whole, by product — what the four regions together buy from and sell to the rest of the world:\n\nfrench_trade = combine(groupby(\n    res.trade[in.(res.trade.from, Ref(france)) .⊻ in.(res.trade.to, Ref(france)), :],\n    [:product]),\n    :quantity => sum => \"crossing_the_border\")\n\u001b[1m11×2 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m product   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m crossing_the_border \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64             \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ chips                 3.11849\n   2 │ hwr                  12.2194\n   3 │ panel                 5.51584\n   4 │ paper                 9.92315\n   5 │ pellets               3.02749\n   6 │ plywood               0.977673\n   7 │ pulp                  3.69739\n   8 │ sawn_hw               1.39973\n   9 │ sawn_sw               9.93187\n  10 │ smallwood            32.2768\n  11 │ swr                  21.6851","title":"Trade"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#How-close-is-France-to-one-market?","page":"Writing the model","text":"The French regions were declared near-perfect substitutes, not perfect ones, and the difference is visible in two places.\n\nFirst, wood moves between French regions in both directions — the cross-hauling a homogeneous market would never do, because for an identical good it would be a pure waste of freight. Here it is a modest share of what France makes:\n\nintra = res.trade[in.(res.trade.from, Ref(france)) .& in.(res.trade.to, Ref(france)), :]\nmade_in_france = combine(groupby(res.production[in.(res.production.region, Ref(france)), :],\n                                 :product), :quantity => sum => :made_in_france)\nleftjoin(combine(groupby(intra, :product), :quantity => sum => :between_regions),\n         made_in_france, on = :product)\n\u001b[1m11×3 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m product   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m between_regions \u001b[0m\u001b[1m made_in_france \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64         \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64?       \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ swr              0.84044         19.1819\n   2 │ hwr              0.375469        11.8409\n   3 │ smallwood        2.99572         35.5574\n   4 │ chips            0.0929862        2.07445\n   5 │ sawn_sw          0.270323         1.9798\n   6 │ sawn_hw          0.0334468        0.443059\n   7 │ panel            0.175345         1.63859\n   8 │ plywood          0.0211073        0.429994\n   9 │ paper            0.306358         2.56719\n  10 │ pellets          0.107144         0.647178\n  11 │ pulp             0.0504428        1.13122\n\nSecond, producer prices can differ by more than the freight between two French regions, which no-arbitrage would forbid for an identical good:\n\npp(r, p) = only(res.prices[(res.prices.region .== r) .& (res.prices.product .== p),\n                           :producer_price])\nprice_gaps = DataFrame((product = p, from = a, to = b,\n                        price_gap      = abs(pp(a, p) - pp(b, p)),\n                        transport_cost = transport_costs[(p, a, b)])\n                       for p in products for a in france for b in france\n                       if a < b && !ismissing(pp(a, p)) && !ismissing(pp(b, p)))\nprice_gaps.ratio = price_gaps.price_gap ./ price_gaps.transport_cost\nfirst(sort(price_gaps, :ratio, rev = true), 6)\n\u001b[1m6×6 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m product \u001b[0m\u001b[1m from   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m to     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m price_gap \u001b[0m\u001b[1m transport_cost \u001b[0m\u001b[1m ratio   \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64        \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\n─────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ swr      OF      SWF       4.34519            0.96  4.52624\n   2 │ hwr      OF      SWF       3.51772            0.96  3.66429\n   3 │ hwr      OF      SEF       2.67965            0.96  2.79131\n   4 │ swr      GEF     SWF       4.2741             1.68  2.54411\n   5 │ hwr      GEF     SEF       2.5504             1.2   2.12534\n   6 │ hwr      GEF     SWF       3.38847            1.68  2.01695\n\nTen of the sixty-one pairs have a gap above the freight, by a few units of price at most — 4 on a softwood log worth about 108, between OF and SWF.\n\nThat is the price of σ_france being finite, and it is worth paying. With sigma = Inf both would vanish — no cross-hauling, no gap above freight — but where the French mills operate could be left undetermined, as Perfect substitutes and constant returns explains. The higher σ_france, the closer France comes to one market and the closer the model comes to that indeterminacy; 30 keeps it well away while leaving the four regions nearly interchangeable.","title":"How close is France to one market?"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Implementing-scenarios","page":"Writing the model","text":"A scenario is the same economy with something changed: a curve shifted, a stock smaller, a price outside the model higher. The economy is plain data, so a scenario is a copy of it with some fields replaced, solved again, and read against the reference run. One small helper does the copying:\n\nreconfigure(d; kwargs...) =\n    MarketData(; (f => get(kwargs, f, getfield(d, f)) for f in fieldnames(MarketData))...)\n\nThree scenarios follow: a storm that floods the Landes with salvaged pine, a dearer gas that sends households to wood fuel, and a third of the French forests set aside.","title":"Implementing scenarios"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#A-storm-in-the-Landes","page":"Writing the model","text":"In January 2009 the storm Klaus felled some 40 Mm³ of maritime pine in south-west France, several years of harvest at once. The salvage has to be sold, so the region's supply curve shifts far out.\n\nThat is what the shift field of a SupplyCurve is for. Everything about the curve other than its price response lives there — the standing volume, the road network, the labour and machines available — while p0 and q0 go on holding the calibration. Tripling q0 would have worked here too, but it would have destroyed the reference point the curve was fitted to; shifting leaves it intact, which is what lets a scenario be run, reported and reversed. For a driver with a known elasticity, exogenous_shift turns levels into the multiplier:\n\nshift = exogenous_shift(growing_stock = (level = 118.0, reference = 100.0,\n                                         elasticity = 0.6))\n\nHere the salvage simply triples what the Landes offer at any price. The felled trees are also wood that can now be cut, so the storm adds them to the conifer stock of the Landes, as dead timber that can be salvaged — some 30 Mm³ of the 40 Klaus brought down. Everything else is left alone:\n\nsalvage = [s.region == \"SWF\" && s.product == \"swr\" ?\n               SupplyCurve(s; shift = 3.0) : s\n           for s in supply]\n\nfelled = merge(stock, Dict(\"SWF\" => (conifers = stock[\"SWF\"].conifers + 30.0,\n                                     broadleaves = stock[\"SWF\"].broadleaves)))\nstorm = solve_market(reconfigure(example_market, supply = salvage,\n                                 limits = all_limits(felled)))\n\nThe question a spatial model exists to answer is how far the shock travels. Comparing the softwood price before and after, region by region:\n\nprice_of(r, p, result) = only(result.prices[(result.prices.region .== r) .&\n                                            (result.prices.product .== p), :price])\n\nDataFrame((region = r,\n           before = price_of(r, \"swr\", res),\n           after  = price_of(r, \"swr\", storm),\n           change_pct = 100 * (price_of(r, \"swr\", storm) / price_of(r, \"swr\", res) - 1))\n          for r in regions)\n\u001b[1m8×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m before   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m after    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m change_pct \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64    \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ SEF     108.306   106.445    -1.71801\n   2 │ SWF     107.564    94.4563  -12.1859\n   3 │ GEF     107.649   105.832    -1.68772\n   4 │ OF      107.775   105.817    -1.81632\n   5 │ EU      102.255   100.88     -1.3442\n   6 │ NA       98.3157   97.7497   -0.575678\n   7 │ AS      127.677   126.932    -0.58341\n   8 │ RW      102.362   101.724    -0.622773\n\nTwo things are going on at once, and they are worth separating.\n\nThe gradient across the blocs is the Armington structure at work. The salvage lands on France, passes with some loss into the rest of the EU, a close substitute, and barely reaches North America or Asia, which buy a different good. Had every product been homogeneous worldwide, a single world price would have moved everywhere at once; had every variety been equally distant, the shock would have stayed bottled up in SWF. Neither is what the forest sector looks like.\n\nThe gap inside France is the evacuation limit. SWF loses seven times as much as its neighbours, which are near-perfect substitutes for it and should share in the glut. What holds the glut back is that the wood cannot get out:\n\nstorm.limits[storm.limits.limit .== \"landes_evacuation\", :]\n\u001b[1m1×9 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m limit             \u001b[0m\u001b[1m lower   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m upper   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m penalty \u001b[0m\u001b[1m value   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m excess  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m rent   \u001b[0m ⋯\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String            \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64\u001b[0m ⋯\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ landes_evacuation     -Inf     18.0      Inf     18.0      0.0   12.718 ⋯\n\u001b[36m                                                               2 columns omitted\u001b[0m\n\nThe ceiling that cost nothing in a normal year is now full, and its congestion rent is the price of a m³ of capacity to move wood out of the Landes. That rent is what drives a wedge between SWF and the rest of the French market, and it is why a salvage price collapse is a local event: not because the wood is worth less in principle, but because it cannot reach anyone who would pay more for it.","title":"A storm in the Landes"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#When-gas-gets-dearer","page":"Writing the model","text":"The last of the exogenous things is the price of the fuels wood competes with. Doubling the gas price does not touch any curve in the model — gas is not in it — but it shifts the wood-fuel bundle's demand by (60/30)^0.35 ≈ 1.27, which exogenous_shift works out from the cross-price elasticity:\n\ngas = solve_market(reconfigure(example_market, demand = build_demand(60.0)))\n\nDataFrame((region = r,\n           firewood = 100 * (quantity_of(gas.consumption, r, \"smallwood\") /\n                             quantity_of(res.consumption, r, \"smallwood\") - 1),\n           pellets  = 100 * (quantity_of(gas.consumption, r, \"pellets\") /\n                             quantity_of(res.consumption, r, \"pellets\") - 1),\n           roundwood_price = 100 * (price_of(r, \"swr\", gas) / price_of(r, \"swr\", res) - 1))\n          for r in regions)\n\u001b[1m8×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m firewood \u001b[0m\u001b[1m pellets  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m roundwood_price \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64         \u001b[0m\n─────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ SEF      31.3946  14.533            3.10591\n   2 │ SWF      31.569   15.2867           3.11404\n   3 │ GEF      30.5339  12.9997           3.11255\n   4 │ OF       32.1299  15.464            3.10952\n   5 │ EU       37.4309  13.956            3.1939\n   6 │ NA       43.4594  16.3508           2.97329\n   7 │ AS       35.25     9.56828          2.91842\n   8 │ RW       24.9435  10.864            3.21266\n\nHouseholds burn much more wood, and the pull reaches back up the chain to the price of standing timber — which is how an energy policy becomes a forestry question.\n\nBut the two columns do not move together, and the reason is the mills. The shifter raises demand for the bundle; which half of it grows depends on which half can grow. Pellets are made in plants whose capacity was fixed above:\n\ngas.activity[gas.activity.process .== \"pelletmill\", :]\n\u001b[1m8×5 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m process    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m level   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m capacity \u001b[0m\u001b[1m rent     \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\n─────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ SEF     pelletmill    0.427     0.427  11.4008\n   2 │ SWF     pelletmill    0.5       0.5     8.69313\n   3 │ GEF     pelletmill    0.349     0.349  16.3863\n   4 │ OF      pelletmill    0.645     0.645  15.3367\n   5 │ EU      pelletmill   28.3      28.3    33.4347\n   6 │ NA      pelletmill   15.3      15.3    32.6674\n   7 │ AS      pelletmill   19.9      19.9    36.4456\n   8 │ RW      pelletmill   14.1      14.1    25.8638\n\nEvery one of them now runs full, and its capacity earns a rent. Pellets can grow only as far as the room the plants had spare, so most of the extra demand lands on the fuel that needs no plant: a log in a stove.\n\nThree of the features set up in this example meet in that one table — a bundle of substitutes in demand, a capacity bound on transformation, and a price from outside the model — and the result is not what any of them would have given alone.","title":"When gas gets dearer"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Setting-forests-aside","page":"Writing the model","text":"The stands bind once there are fewer of them. Suppose a third of every French forest is set aside — protected, out of reach of the harvest — and nothing else changes:\n\nprotected = Dict(r => r in france ? (conifers = 2 / 3 * s.conifers, broadleaves = 2 / 3 * s.broadleaves) : s\n                 for (r, s) in stock)\naside = solve_market(reconfigure(example_market, limits = all_limits(protected)))\n\nNow the French regions hit their stands, and the limits that bind are those of all three products together: every m³ either kind of stand can give is cut.\n\naside.limits[aside.limits.rent .> 0, :]\n\u001b[1m4×9 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m limit                      \u001b[0m\u001b[1m lower   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m upper   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m penalty \u001b[0m\u001b[1m value   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m excess \u001b[0m ⋯\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String                     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64\u001b[0m ⋯\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ SEF: swr + hwr + smallwood     -Inf  11.3333      Inf  11.3333      0.0 ⋯\n   2 │ SWF: swr + hwr + smallwood     -Inf  13.3333      Inf  13.3333      0.0\n   3 │ GEF: swr + hwr + smallwood     -Inf  13.3333      Inf  13.3333      0.0\n   4 │ OF: swr + hwr + smallwood      -Inf  18.0         Inf  18.0         0.0\n\u001b[36m                                                               3 columns omitted\u001b[0m\n\nThe rent of each is what one more m³ of standing timber would be worth to the economy: the stumpage value the scarcity creates, on top of what the supply curves ask. What it does to the harvest and to the price of logs:\n\nDataFrame((region = r,\n           harvest_before = sum(quantity_of(res.production, r, p) for p in primary),\n           harvest_after  = sum(quantity_of(aside.production, r, p) for p in primary),\n           log_price_change = 100 * (price_of(r, \"swr\", aside) / price_of(r, \"swr\", res) - 1))\n          for r in regions)\n\u001b[1m8×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m harvest_before \u001b[0m\u001b[1m harvest_after \u001b[0m\u001b[1m log_price_change \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64        \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64       \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64          \u001b[0m\n─────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ SEF            13.5461        11.3333          0.558855\n   2 │ SWF            15.4825        13.3333          0.620317\n   3 │ GEF            15.9775        13.3333          0.547215\n   4 │ OF             21.5741        18.0             0.577605\n   5 │ EU            688.983        691.489           0.430724\n   6 │ NA            722.849        724.051           0.268935\n   7 │ AS            749.784        750.85            0.265862\n   8 │ RW            858.57         860.143           0.292905\n\nFrance cuts a sixth less wood, as much as its stands now allow, yet the price of its logs barely moves: French logs compete with those of the rest of Europe, a close substitute, which makes up most of the difference. The scarcity shows up instead in the rent — the gap between what a m³ fetches and what its owner would have sold it for — which is the value the protected stands take out of the harvest. A region whose wood had no close substitute would see it in its prices too.","title":"Setting forests aside"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#The-limits-of-uncalibrated-data","page":"Writing the model","text":"Every curve in this model passes through a reference point taken from the data: each supply curve through a region's harvest at its roadside price, each demand curve through a region's consumption at the price given for the product. Yet the equilibrium does not land on those points. Here are the consumer prices it settles at, against the one price per product it was given:\n\nDataFrame((product = p, given = reference[p][1],\n           lowest  = minimum(price_of(r, p, res) for r in regions),\n           highest = maximum(price_of(r, p, res) for r in regions))\n          for p in sort(collect(keys(reference))))\n\u001b[1m7×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m product   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m given   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m lowest   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m highest   \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\n─────┼─────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ panel        240.0  247.793    266.855\n   2 │ paper        935.0  977.491   1027.48\n   3 │ pellets      285.0  282.161    309.388\n   4 │ plywood      285.0  306.028    339.127\n   5 │ sawn_hw      280.0  256.819    307.115\n   6 │ sawn_sw      205.0  209.078    245.345\n   7 │ smallwood     70.0   65.7857    81.0101\n\nand the harvests, against those the supply curves were drawn through:\n\nharvest_gap = DataFrame((product = s.product, region = s.region, given = s.q0,\n                         model = quantity_of(res.production, s.region, s.product))\n                        for s in supply)\nharvest_gap.gap_pct = 100 .* (harvest_gap.model ./ harvest_gap.given .- 1)\nfirst(sort(harvest_gap, :gap_pct, by = abs, rev = true), 6)\n\u001b[1m6×5 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m product   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m given   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m model    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m gap_pct  \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ swr        AS        200.0  196.397   -1.8017\n   2 │ hwr        RW        166.0  163.424   -1.5521\n   3 │ swr        RW        260.0  256.443   -1.36792\n   4 │ hwr        AS        240.0  237.066   -1.22258\n   5 │ smallwood  EU        244.0  246.973    1.21857\n   6 │ hwr        NA         94.7   93.5794  -1.18336\n\nThe harvests land within 2% of the data: logs trade freely, and the supply curves hold them close to their reference points. The prices do not. They miss the one price given by up to a fifth, and by different amounts in different regions — plywood costs 306 to 339 where 285 was given, softwood lumber 209 to 245 against 205.\n\nThe reason is that the numbers are not consistent with each other. One price per product says every region pays the same, while freight and the preference for local products say they cannot. The trade shares come from a rule, not from what was shipped, so the flows the model needs to feed each region are not those that made the observed harvests worth cutting. The mills' costs are guesses, so the margins they leave do not match the prices the mills paid and got. Each piece of data pulls the equilibrium its own way, and the equilibrium is wherever they balance — which is not the base year.\n\nDoes it matter? A scenario is read as a difference from the model's own reference run, not from the data, and for much of it the answer is no: constant-elasticity curves give the same percentage response wherever they are evaluated. But a difference also depends on what the shock runs into — how close a limit is, how large each market share is, where the mills run — and those the uncalibrated reference run gets wrong in its own way. How much that changes the storm and the gas scenarios is measured on the third page.\n\nGetting the reference run onto the data is calibration: choosing the numbers nobody observes — trade shares, costs, the recipe of each mill, the reference point of each curve — so that the observed year is an equilibrium. The next page, Calibrating with partial data, does it with the incomplete and inconsistent statistics a real application has to work with.\n\nThe results of this page are kept, for that comparison:\n\nresults_dir = joinpath(pkgdir(Markets), \"examples\", \"forest\", \"results\")\n\nfunction outcome(result, scenario)\n    t = outerjoin(select(result.prices, :region, :product, :price, :producer_price),\n                  rename(result.production, :quantity => :production),\n                  select(result.consumption, :region, :product, :quantity => :consumption),\n                  on = [:region, :product])\n    return insertcols!(t, 1, :scenario => scenario)\nend\n\nfunction save_outcomes(file, runs)\n    t = vcat((outcome(r, s) for (s, r) in runs)...)\n    cell(x) = ismissing(x) ? \"\" : x isa Real ? string(round(x; sigdigits = 6)) : string(x)\n    mkpath(results_dir)\n    open(joinpath(results_dir, file), \"w\") do io\n        println(io, join(names(t), \",\"))\n        foreach(row -> println(io, join(cell.(collect(row)), \",\")), eachrow(t))\n    end\nend\n\nsave_outcomes(\"uncalibrated.csv\", [\"reference\" => res, \"storm\" => storm, \"gas\" => gas])","title":"The limits of uncalibrated data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_market/#Where-to-go-from-here","page":"Writing the model","text":"Change the elasticities in σ_blocs and σ_europe and watch the gradient above widen or flatten, or those in σ_lumber, σ_construction and σ_fuel and watch demand move between products instead of between regions.\nRaise or remove the pellet capacities and see how much of the energy shock moves from firewood to pellets.\nPut a tariff on something and compare the objective with and without the revenue added back; the difference is the transfer the program counts as a cost.\nPut real elasticities in. Buongiorno (2015) for the demand side, McDaniel & Balistreri (2003) for the Armington ones.\nAdd limits of your own with @limit: a minimum share of domestic logs in the French mills, a quota on exports, a cap on the smallwood that may be burnt — any linear constraint on the quantities of the model, with its rent reported in Results.limits.\nCalibrate it: the next page replaces the gravity shares, the single prices and the guessed costs with numbers worked out from data.\nGive the EU market its own destination specification to represent a non-tariff measure applying to one bloc only.\nMake transport concave in distance, or compute distances between the centres of gravity of the forest resource and of the population rather than between region centroids.\nDrive a multi-period run from outside: call solve_market once per period and update each region's supply shift from the standing volume left after the harvest, and each mill's capacity limit from what was built. The engine stays static on purpose — the time loop belongs to whatever uses it.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Where to go from here"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Code-implementation","page":"Code implementation","text":"This page describes the mathematical program built by solve_market, how results are extracted, and the layout of the code. The reasons behind the formulation are on the Modelling choices page.","title":"Code implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Source-layout","page":"Code implementation","text":"File Content\nsrc/Markets.jl module definition, exports\nsrc/names.jl names and selectors: strings everywhere, symbols accepted, : for every one\nsrc/limits.jl Limit, LimitExpression, @limit and @limits: linear constraints on the quantities of the model\nsrc/types.jl data schema: Markets.Curve with its two sides DemandCurve and SupplyCurve, DemandNest, Nest, leontief, ces, Process, Armington, OriginNest, Tax, MarketData, and the calibration helpers\nsrc/validate.jl validate: every check on an economy, run before anything is built\nsrc/results.jl Results and for_region: extraction of the result tables\nsrc/model.jl solve_market and jump_model: build and solve the JuMP model, with the one CES tree builder both demand groups and origin groups go through\nsrc/materials.jl Material and material_accounts: material carried by the goods, traced through a solution\nsrc/show.jl the summary print(::MarketData) gives\nexamples/minimal/ the two-region dairy, the opening example page\nexamples/forest/ the full-size example, in three runnable scripts that are also the source of its three pages: forest_market.jl (writing the model), forest_calibration.jl (calibrating it with partial data) and forest_full_data.jl (complete data, and the comparison); data/ holds their base years, results/ what the first two save for the third\nexamples/spatequ/, examples/egypt/ ports of two models of the GAMS library, checked against the originals\ntest/runtests.jl test suite\ndocs/ this documentation (Documenter.jl, with Literate.jl for the example pages)\n\nEvery type of the data schema is declared with Base.@kwdef, so it is built with keyword arguments and its optional fields have defaults; the field documentation on the API reference page is generated from the struct definitions with DocStringExtensions. The two nest builders, leontief and ces, take keyword arguments too.\n\nThe model is written with JuMP ([C1], Lubin et al. 2023) and solved by default with Ipopt ([C2]), the interior-point method of Wächter & Biegler (2006). Variables and constraints are created only for the combinations present in the data, and stored in dictionaries keyed by the names themselves (e.g. D[(region, product)], T[(product, from, to)]), not in dense arrays.","title":"Source layout"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#The-mathematical-program","page":"Code implementation","text":"","title":"The mathematical program"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Sets-and-data","page":"Code implementation","text":"Symbol Meaning\nr r in R regions\np in P products\nk in K processes; R_k subseteq R the regions where k operates\nn in N_k input nests of process k; I_n the products of nest n\nmathcalD mathcalS subseteq R times P (region, product) pairs with a demand / supply curve\nmathcalT trade routes (p r r) present in transport_costs\nP^A subseteq P products with a finite Armington elasticity; P^H = P setminus P^A the homogeneous ones\nO_pr subseteq R origins destination r buys p in P^A from: itself, plus every origin with a route into r and a positive share\np_0 q_0 eta varepsilon reference price, reference quantity, demand and supply elasticities\nm_rp the shift scaling a curve\nunderline L_l overline L_l w_lv the bounds of limit l and the coefficient of variable v in its expression\ny_kp yield of output p per unit of activity of k\nbar a_n delta_ni sigma_n phi_n composite requirement, shares, elasticity of substitution and scale of nest n\ndelta_por value share of origin o in destination r (normalised over O_pr)\nmathcalN_pr the CES tree destination r aggregates the origins of p with: leaves are origins, each node nu carrying an elasticity sigma_nu and shares over its children\nc_kr value-added cost per unit of activity of k in r (vacost, one number or one per region)\ntau_prr unit transport cost on a route","title":"Sets and data"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Variables","page":"Code implementation","text":"Variable Domain Meaning\nD_rp ge q_min (rp) in mathcalD final demand; bounded by 0 instead on a straight or a flat curve, whose marginal benefit at zero is finite\nS_rp ge 0 (rp) in mathcalS primary supply\nz_rk ge 0 k in K r in R_k process activity\nC_s demand curves s over a group the composite of a group of substitutes, whose members are the D of each\nx_rkni ge q_min CES nests n, i in I_n quantity of input i used in nest n; bounded by 0 instead when sigma_n = 0\nT_prr ge 0 (prr) in mathcalT, p in P^H trade flow from r to r\nX_por ge 0 p in P^A, o in O_pr quantity of the variety of origin o used in r; floored only under a node with 0  sigma  infty\nA_pr ge 0 p in P^A, r in R composite of p available to the users of r, built only where r uses it\nC_nu ge 0 internal nodes nu of mathcalN_pr sub-composite of a group of origins; floored only when its parent has 0  sigma  infty\n\nq_min is, for each variable, 10^-6 of that variable's own reference magnitude, or the one absolute floor given as the qfloor keyword of solve_market (see Numerical details).","title":"Variables"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Objective","page":"Code implementation","text":"beginaligned\nmax  sum_(rp) in mathcalD fraca_rp1 - 1eta_rp D_rp^1 - 1eta_rp\n       - sum_(rp) in mathcalS fracb_rp1 + 1varepsilon_rp S_rp^1 + 1varepsilon_rp \n        - sum_k sum_r in R_k c_kr z_rk\n       - sum_(prr) in mathcalT tau_prr T_prr\n       - sum_p in P^A sum_r sum_o in O_pr o ne r tau_por X_por\nendaligned\n\nwith a = p_0(m q_0)^1eta and b = p_0(m q_0)^-1varepsilon: a shifter enters by scaling the reference quantity the curve is anchored at. The first two sums are the closed-form integrals of the inverse demand curve P^D(D) = a D^-1eta and of the inverse supply curve P^S(S) = b S^1varepsilon.","title":"Objective"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#CES-nest-constraints","page":"Code implementation","text":"For each process k, region r in R_k and nest n with more than one product, with rho_n = (sigma_n - 1)sigma_n:\n\nphi_n left( sum_i in I_n delta_ni^1sigma_n x_rkni^rho_n right)^1rho_n ge bar a_n z_rk\n\nThe constraint is an inequality, which keeps the feasible set convex; at the optimum it is binding because inputs are costly.\n\nWhere the formula cannot be evaluated, the aggregator is replaced by its limit: at sigma_n = 1 by the Cobb–Douglas prod_i (x_rknidelta_ni)^delta_ni, at sigma_n = 0 by min_i x_rknidelta_ni, which is written as the linear constraints phi_n x_rkni ge delta_ni bar a_n z_rk, one per input, and at sigma_n = infty by sum_i x_rkni. The same replacement is made in the Armington and the final-demand trees below; it is done in one place, ces_constraint!. A nest with a single product (Leontief) creates no variable and no constraint: the input use bar a_n z_rk enters the material balance directly.","title":"CES nest constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Material-balance-of-a-homogeneous-product","page":"Code implementation","text":"For every region r in R and product p in P^H:\n\nbeginaligned\n S_rp + sum_k y_kp z_rk + sum_r T_prr \n=  D_rp + sum_k sum_substackn in N_k  I_n = p bar a_n z_rk\n      + sum_k sum_substackn in N_k  p in I_n I_n  1 x_rknp\n      + sum_r T_prr\nendaligned\n\nTerms are present only where defined (e.g. S_rp only if (rp) in mathcalS). In the code the constraint is written as sources + imports − uses − exports == 0.","title":"Material balance of a homogeneous product"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Balances-of-an-Armington-product","page":"Code implementation","text":"For p in P^A the single balance splits in two, because what a region produces and what its users consume are no longer the same good. Writing textsrc_rp and textuse_rp for the source and use sides above (without the trade terms), for every r in R:\n\ntextsrc_rp = sum_r  r in O_pr X_prr\nqquadtext(origin balance all local output is shipped somewhere)\n\nA_pr = textuse_rp\nqquadtext(absorption balance the composite covers all local use)\n\nbuilt only where the region uses the product at all — in final demand or as a process input. A region that uses none of it holds no composite and gets neither of these two constraints, though its origin balance remains so that it can produce the product for others. (Building them unconditionally made such a region infeasible, since A carries a positive lower bound while textuse is zero.)\n\nleft( sum_c in mathrmchildren(nu) delta_nuc^1sigma_nu q_c^rho_nu right)^1rho_nu ge q_nu\nqquad rho_nu = fracsigma_nu - 1sigma_nu\nqquadtext(CES at each node)\n\none per internal node nu of the tree, where q_c is X_por for a leaf and C_c for a group, and q_nu is A_pr at the root and C_nu below it. With no groups the tree is one node and this is a single CES over the origins. At the root the left-hand side is multiplied by the destination's scale phi_pr (1 unless given), which divides the composite price by the same factor; armington_calibration sets it so that at the base year the composite equals the plain sum of the flows.\n\nNote that o = r is in O_pr: a region's own variety goes through the composite like any other, and X_prr is its domestic use. A node left with one child contributes no variable and no constraint; in particular a destination with a single origin gets the linear constraint X_por ge A_pr, avoiding the powers.\n\nThe aggregator is the same calibrated CES as the input nests, so the price of each node is the standard CES index over its children,\n\npi_nu = left( sum_c in mathrmchildren(nu) delta_nuc\n          pi_c^1-sigma_nu right)^1(1-sigma_nu)\n\nwith pi_c = pi^s_po + tau_por at a leaf — the delivered price of that origin — and pi^c_pr = pi_textroot the price the users of r pay. Flattened to a single node this is\n\npi^c_pr = left( sum_o in O_pr delta_por\n              bigl(pi^s_po + tau_porbigr)^1-sigma right)^1(1-sigma)\n\nwhich tends to min_o (pi^s_po + tau_por) as sigma to infty, is prod_o (pi^s_po + tau_por)^delta_por at sigma = 1 and sum_o delta_por (pi^s_po + tau_por) at sigma = 0. At sigma = infty the engine does not build these constraints at all: the product is treated as homogeneous, which is the same model.","title":"Balances of an Armington product"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#How-the-tree-is-built","page":"Code implementation","text":"Groups of origins and groups of substitutes in final demand are the same structure, and the model builds both through one internal node type, CESNode — an elasticity, children that are leaves or further nodes, and the children's shares among themselves — and one builder, ces_tree!, which walks the tree, creates a composite variable and one ces_constraint! per node, and asks the caller for the variable of each leaf: an existing variety X_por for an origin, a fresh consumption variable D_rp for a product. A node is floored only when the node above it raises it to a power, and a leaf likewise.\n\narmington_tree turns the groups of a specification into that tree for one destination. The origins available to the destination are collected first, each with its share; every group is then reduced to the available origins it holds, and groups that keep none are dropped. Each node's shares are the summed shares of the origins beneath each child, normalised among siblings — so the data stay a plain bilateral matrix and the nesting only says how they are read. A DemandNest is read into the same form with its own shares. In both, a node left with one child is replaced by that child, at every level, so that the floors, the constraints and the printed model never carry a trivial group.\n\nThe specification applied to a (product, destination) pair is the one whose destination names it, or the one for every destination (:) otherwise, which is what lets the elasticity, the groups and the shares differ between importing markets.","title":"How the tree is built"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Limits","page":"Code implementation","text":"Every ceiling, floor and fixed sum of the economy is a Limit: a linear expression in the model's variables between two bounds,\n\nunderline L_l le sum_v w_lv v le overline L_l\n\nwhere v runs over the supplies S, final demands D, activities z, CES inputs x and cross-border flows — the trade variables T of a homogeneous product and the varieties X_por, o ne r, of an Armington one. Production is S plus yields times z, and what a fixed-coefficient nest draws is its coefficient times z, so both expand into these. A limit that is only an upper bound on one variable with a positive coefficient is set as a bound on that variable, v le overline L_l  w_lv; any other is one constraint per finite bound, or an equality where the two coincide.\n\nThe rent of a limit is partial W  partial L, the change in the objective from moving the bound that binds: minus the dual of its constraint, or of the variable bound divided by w_lv. It is reported only where the expression actually sits on the bound — complementary slackness, which an interior-point solver satisfies only to its tolerance. The rent per unit a variable carries is sum_l w_lvtextrent_l over the limits it appears in, and its capacity the tightest bound a hard limit on it alone implies.\n\nA soft limit, with a finite penalty pi_l, gets a slack for each finite bound, e_l^+ e_l^- ge 0:\n\nsum_v w_lv v - e_l^+ le overline L_l qquad\nsum_v w_lv v + e_l^- ge underline L_l\nqquad W mathrel-= pi_l(e_l^+ + e_l^-)\n\nIt is never set as a variable bound. The slack is linear in the objective, so the program stays convex, and its first-order condition caps the dual at pi_l: below the bound the limit behaves as a hard one, and once crossed its rent is the penalty. A bound counts as reached when the expression is on it or beyond it, and excess is e_l^+ + e_l^-. The penalty is reported apart, as sum_l pi_l (e_l^+ + e_l^-), and added back to the objective in welfare.","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Substitutes-in-final-demand","page":"Code implementation","text":"A DemandCurve whose product is a DemandNest builds the same calibrated CES tree as the Armington one, over products instead of origins: one consumption variable per product of the group, one composite per node, and at each node\n\nleft( sum_c delta_c^1sigma_nu q_c^rho_nu\nright)^1rho_nu ge q_nu\n\nThe curve's benefit term is then taken on the root composite, not on the individual quantities, which is what gives the cross-price effects while keeping the objective a single concave function of the quantities — so demand stays integrable and the program stays convex. A group of one product collapses to the plain case with no composite and no constraint.","title":"Substitutes in final demand"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Tax-wedges","page":"Code implementation","text":"A tax enters the objective exactly as a cost does, so each wedge modifies one coefficient that was already there. Writing t_a for the summed absolute rates and t_r for the summed relative ones on a given base:\n\nOn Effect\nsupply b to (1 + t_r)b, plus a term t_a S: the market must cover the marginal cost and the wedge\ndemand a to a(1 + t_r), less a term t_a C(1+t_r): the curve is what the consumer pays, the gradient is what the market pays\nprocess c_kr to (1 + t_r)c_kr + t_a\ntrade tau to tau + t_a + text(ad valorem equivalent)\n\nThe first three are exact in one solve. The fourth is exact for t_a; the ad valorem part is a fraction of pi^s_po, a dual, so solve_market runs solve_once repeatedly, each pass holding the tariff at the money equivalent implied by the previous pass's producer prices, until successive passes agree to within tax_tolerance. At the fixed point the wedge equals the rate times the origin price by construction, which is what a tariff means.\n\nThat loop is successive substitution, and it converges only where the map contracts. In practice it contracts hard — the price at the origin barely moves with the tariff, being set mostly by the exporter's own market — and rates of several hundred per cent settle in three to seven passes. There is no guarantee of it, though, so the step is halved whenever a pass increases the error rather than reducing it: a well-behaved problem never triggers that and runs at full speed, an oscillating one is damped until it settles. Beyond some rate a tariff becomes prohibitive, the route empties, and the price gap is then bounded by the wedge rather than equal to it — which is the equilibrium, not a failure of the loop.\n\nRevenue is recomputed afterwards from the solution, instrument by instrument, and reported in Results.taxes. Results.welfare is the objective's terms evaluated at the solution with every tax left out. Adding the revenue back to the objective would give the same figure for per-unit rates only: a relative rate on supply or demand scales a whole curve integral, and the objective then differs from welfare by more, or less, than what the tax raises.","title":"Tax wedges"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Convexity","page":"Code implementation","text":"The first term of the objective is concave for every eta  0: for eta  1 the exponent 1 - 1eta is in (01), for eta  1 the coefficient and the exponent are both negative, and at eta = 1 it is a logarithm; on a straight line it is a quadratic with a negative leading coefficient. The second is convex and subtracted — a power above 1, or a quadratic with a positive leading coefficient — and the others are linear, so the objective is concave. The CES aggregator is concave for rho  1, negative rho included, and so are its limits at sigma = 0 (a minimum of linear functions) and sigma = 1 (a weighted geometric mean with weights summing to 1), so the set where it is above a linear function is convex. The material balances are linear. The problem is therefore a convex program, and the local optimum found by Ipopt is the global one.","title":"Convexity"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Validation","page":"Code implementation","text":"validate runs before anything is built, and solve_market calls it. It exists because of how the program is assembled: the balances are created by looping over regions × products, so a name outside those two lists gets variables and objective terms but no balance. A mistyped process input then becomes a free input, a mistyped consumed product becomes an unbounded one, and the solver reports success either way. Rather than guard each site, every name, duplicate and selector is checked once, up front, against the declared sets.\n\nThe same pass checks everything else that would otherwise surface only while the model is built, so that validate alone is the verdict on a specification: the Armington specifications resolve to one per product and destination (armington_of), with a product imperfectly substitutable everywhere or nowhere; every term of every limit selects at least one quantity the model will have (limit_terms over model_catalogue); every tax selects at least one curve, process or route; and the members of one demand group carry one ad valorem rate. It rejects two economies that have no finite answer to give: one with no regions or no products, and one containing a process with no inputs, no value-added cost and no ceiling on its activity, which is a free source of its own output.","title":"Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#From-the-solution-to-prices","page":"Code implementation","text":"The balance faced by the users of a region is stored in balance[(r, p)], and the price those users pay is the dual of that constraint:\n\ndual(balance[(r, p)])             # the `price` column\n\nThe dual is the change in the objective per extra unit of product available to them, i.e. the competitive price. For an Armington product the origin balance is stored as well, in origin_balance[(r, p)], and its dual is what local producers are paid:\n\ndual(origin_balance[(r, p)])      # the `producer_price` column\n\nwarning: Do not take the absolute value, and do not use `shadow_price`\nThe sign is information, not noise. Because the balances are equalities, a by-product that nobody wants has a negative price — the amount somebody must be paid to take it — and an earlier version of this package reported abs(dual(...)), which silently turned such a price into its positive mirror image. shadow_price is no remedy: for an equality constraint it reports the gain from relaxing the constraint in whichever direction helps, so it returns the same positive number. The raw dual is the signed economic price and is what the code now uses.\n\nThe two prices differ by the composition of the CES bundle: local users pay the price index of all the varieties they buy, local producers are paid the value of their own. For a homogeneous product there is no origin balance and producer_price repeats price.\n\nEither column is missing where the corresponding balance does not exist: a region that cannot produce an Armington product has no producer_price for it, and a region that uses none of one has no price, since it holds no composite of it.\n\nnote: Note\nBecause there is no free disposal, the price of a product in excess supply — a by-product with no profitable use — is genuinely negative, and is reported as such. Adding a zero-cost process that consumes it is how to ask for free disposal instead; see Joint products and residues.","title":"From the solution to prices"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Seeing-the-model","page":"Code implementation","text":"build_model (internal) builds the JuMP model and returns it with the variables and constraints the results are read from; solve_once solves what it returns, and jump_model hands it to the user unsolved. Every variable and every constraint is named — the balances balance[r,p] and origin_balance[r,p], the CES aggregators input_nest[r,k,n] for the n-th nest of a process, demand_group[r][i] and origin_group[p,r][i] for the i-th node of a tree, and armington[p,r], and limit[name] for a limit that is a constraint — so print(jump_model(d)) can be read against the formulation above. A balance in which no variable appears — a product the region neither makes, uses nor carries — is not written, since a constraint on nothing would only count against the degrees of freedom Ipopt insists on. A per-unit demand tax of zero is left out of the objective rather than written as a term that is zero, which would only make the printed model harder to read.","title":"Seeing the model"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Results-extraction","page":"Code implementation","text":"build_results (internal) reads the variable values after the solve and fills a Results with the tables:\n\nproduction: S_rp plus sum_k y_kp z_rk, for all region and product pairs with a value above tol, and supply for S_rp alone, with the capacity and the rent the limits put on it;\nconsumption: D_rp above tol, in composite and in physical units;\ncomposites, inputs and recipes: the physical account of the CES aggregates, as described under Physical accounts;\ntrade: T_prr and the cross-border X_por (o ne r) above tol;\nnet_trade: exports and imports summed over routes, for pairs with any trade above tol;\nprices: one row for every region and product, if the solver returned duals, with price and producer_price as above;\nactivity: z_rk above tol;\nlimits and taxes: each limit with its value, excess and rent, and each tax with its revenue.\n\nEvery solved quantity, unrounded and unfiltered — the x variables and the Leontief uses in drawn, the domestic varieties X_prr among the flows, production, and each region's composite — is kept in res.solution, which is what material_accounts and any account that must close exactly start from.","title":"Results extraction"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Numerical-details","page":"Code implementation","text":"The quantity floor, qfloor. A variable that is raised to a power with an exponent below 1 has an infinite derivative at zero, which an interior-point solver cannot evaluate there. Those variables are therefore bounded below by a small positive qfloor rather than by 0, which forces a sliver of quantity that would otherwise be absent. It applies to exactly three kinds of variable:\nfinal demand D, whose marginal benefit is a D^-1eta;\nthe inputs x of a CES process nest, which enter as x^rho, except in a nest with sigma = 0, where they enter linearly;\nthe Armington varieties and sub-composites that sit directly under a node with 0  sigma  infty. A variety under a Leontief node (sigma = 0) or pooled by an infinitely substitutable group enters its aggregator linearly and is bounded below by 0, so it can go cleanly to zero.\nPrimary supply S, process activity z, trade flows T and the composites A need no floor and have none.\nTwo consequences to keep in mind. A process with activity z = 0 still draws qfloor of each of its CES inputs, and its nest constraint is then slack rather than binding. And every origin–destination pair that is part of a CES node trades at least qfloor, whatever the prices.\nThe floor is set per variable. Each floored variable is bounded below by 10^-6 of its own reference magnitude — the reference quantity of the curve it belongs to, the composite requirement of the nest it sits in, or the share of local absorption it is expected to carry. One absolute floor for the whole model would be chosen by the smallest product in it and then applied to the largest: add one obscure product with a reference quantity of 0.001 to an economy measured in hundreds, and every variable inherits a floor of 10^-9, where gradients of the form rho x^rho-1 are large enough to give an interior-point solver trouble. Passing qfloor explicitly overrides the rule with a single absolute floor.\ntol scales with the data. The threshold below which a quantity is not reported defaults to 10^-3 of the smallest reference quantity in the economy, so that it means the same thing whether the model is written in tonnes or in million tonnes, and never hides a flow that matters for the smallest product.\nStarting point. D and S start at mq_0, their shifted reference quantities; z at 1; CES inputs at bar a_n; and trade flows at 0. The solver moves a start that violates a bound back inside it.\nThe unit-elastic case. At eta = 1 the exponent 1 - 1eta is zero and the power form of the benefit term is undefined; the code switches to a log D when 1 - 1eta  10^-8, which also avoids the cancellation that the power form would suffer just either side of 1.\nSolver status. It is carried on Results.status, and by default anything but OPTIMAL, LOCALLY_SOLVED or one of the two ALMOST_ statuses is an error: an infeasible or unbounded economy returns tables that look exactly like those of a solved one, so returning them quietly is the dangerous choice. on_failure = \"warn\" or \"ignore\" restores the older behaviour. The ALMOST_ statuses, where the solver met its acceptable but not its target tolerance, warn and return.\nFeasibility tolerance. Ipopt's constr_viol_tol is absolute, and its default of 10^-4 can be coarser than the quantities of an economy written in small units — in which case a violated balance passes as satisfied and an infeasible model is reported as solved. It is therefore set to 10^-2 of qfloor, and the acceptable tolerance to qfloor itself. Another solver needs the same treatment. Either can be overridden through solver_options.\nOther solvers. Any JuMP solver that supports nonlinear constraints and duals can be passed with the solver keyword, and its options with solver_options.","title":"Numerical details"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Tests","page":"Code implementation","text":"test/runtests.jl checks the solution against conditions that hold at the equilibrium:\n\na one-product, two-region market: the exporting region is the cheap one, the price gap equals the transport cost, prices equal the inverse demand and supply curves, and regional balances hold;\na two-stage chain (a CES process feeding a Leontief process): the Leontief output price equals coefficient × input price + value-added cost, the CES input ratio satisfies the cost-minimisation condition, and the CES output price equals the composite unit cost + value-added cost;\norigin groups: a group as substitutable as its parent changes nothing, the composite price matches the two-level CES index, a tightening group pulls its members' prices together, structures and elasticities differ by destination as specified, the : specification serves as the fallback, and inconsistent groupings are rejected;\ndemand elasticities on both sides of 1: the equilibrium price equals the inverse demand for eta from 0.3 to 3, the unit-elastic case matches the limit of the power form, a less elastic demand moves prices more, and non-positive elasticities and reference points are rejected;\nprices keep their sign: an unwanted by-product that must be incinerated has a price equal to minus the cost of burning it, and adding a free disposal process brings it back to zero;\nshare calibration: armington_shares normalises per destination and reproduces the base-year sourcing through the CES demand condition, while differing from the observed shares themselves; ces_calibration inverts the CES demand condition, takes its limits at sigma = 0, 1 and infty, and a demand nest built from it, flat or nested, has the base year itself as its equilibrium;\nArmington trade: sigma = infty reproduces the homogeneous solution exactly, the error against it falls monotonically as sigma grows, a finite sigma produces cross-hauling and producer-price gaps wider than the transport cost, the composite price equals the CES price index of the delivered prices, the variety mix follows the CES demand condition, a supply shock in one region moves prices and flows in the other, and the specification errors are caught;\nelasticities of 1 and below: at sigma = 0, 03 and 1 the process, Armington and final-demand nests satisfy the CES first-order condition and price index (or their limits), the sigma = 0 and sigma = 1 branches agree with an elasticity just beside them, a region whose own supply grows imports more of the other variety below 1 (complements) and less above it (substitutes), a Cobb–Douglas group inside a Leontief composite prices as the two-level index, and only variables raised to a power are floored;\nnested Armington calibration: armington_calibration, on the flows and prices of a solved economy with a pooled group, rebuilds one that returns its prices, production and consumption, counts each composite in the units of the flows, and refuses a pool member that would have been the cheapest without shipping; ces_calibration's scale prices a bundle at the average unit price, and a vacost given by region gives zero profit at each region's own cost;\nphysical accounts: a region's composite is set against the varieties it received, every use of it is credited with their ratio so that the uses add up to what was received, homogeneous products need no correction, and each nest's physical inputs are set against its recipe;\nlimits: a cap, a minimum, an equality, a range and a limit with quantities on both sides hold at the solution; the rent of a binding cap equals the change in the objective when the cap moves, a minimum's rent is negative, the macro and the explicit constructor give the same equilibrium, a group's exports leave the group, and unknown names, quantities that do not exist, duplicate names and anything not linear are refused; a limit on one variable becomes a bound on it, the supply, activity and trade tables carry the rents of the limits their quantities are part of, Markets.scope names the same regions and variables before the solve as Results.limits after it, a family from @limits has one member per combination of its indices, named after them, and the colon selects every region or product while \"all\" is a name like any other;\nmaterial accounts: every region's material balance closes, and the world's — extraction equals consumption plus losses; the content of primary supply is the one given, region by region, what a region uses is the mix of what it received, a process loses its share and divides the rest among its outputs by their weights, a homogeneous product carries the content of where it was made, and inconsistent specifications are refused;\ntrade imputation: impute_flows meets production, use and known totals (single flows and group totals), keeps the gravity pattern up to one factor per origin and one per destination, scales uses to production, and refuses totals that contradict each other;\nthe forest example: written with uncalibrated numbers, it solves with all prices positive, world exports equal to world imports, and a storm that lowers prices most in France, then in the EU, then in the far blocs; calibrated on partial data, it lands within a per cent of every price and quantity and a few per cent of every customs flow; calibrated on complete data, it returns them to the precision of the solver.\n\nRun them with:\n\nusing Pkg; Pkg.test(\"Markets\")","title":"Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Building-this-documentation","page":"Code implementation","text":"From the package folder:\n\njulia --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(path=\".\"); Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=docs docs/make.jl\n\nThe pages are written to docs/build/. On GitHub, the CI workflow builds them and deploys them to the gh-pages branch.","title":"Building this documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/#Modelling-choices","page":"Modelling choices","text":"These pages explain what the model assumes and why. The exact program that the code builds is on the Code implementation page.\n\nEquilibrium, curves and welfare: why one optimisation gives the market equilibrium, the shape of the demand and supply curves, how everything other than the price moves them, and what the welfare the model reports can and cannot be used for.\nProcesses and substitution: transformations and their input nests, joint products, and substitution between products in final demand.\nTaxes and trade: wedges between prices, transport between regions, and imperfect substitution between origins.\nAccounts, calibration and scope: what the model leaves unconstrained, the physical accounts it keeps, calibration to a base year, and what lies outside the engine.","title":"Modelling choices"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/#Where-these-ideas-come-from","page":"Modelling choices","text":"The works behind the choices on these pages, and the code they can be read in, are collected on the References page: a theory list cited as Author (year), and an implementation list cited as [C1], [C2] and so on, pointing at repositories where the same concept is written out and runs.","title":"Where these ideas come from"},{"category":"section","location":"#Markets.jl","page":"Home","text":"warning: Pre-release: under active development and validation\nThis project is at a proof-of-concept stage and has not been released. A substantial part of it was generated with the assistance of an AI model and is still being reviewed. Both the modelling framework (its assumptions and equations) and its software implementation are currently undergoing verification and validation.Results have not yet been validated and should not be used for publications, policy advice, or operational decisions.\nThe API may change without notice and without backward compatibility.\n\nMarkets.jl solves a spatial partial-equilibrium model of an economic sector made of an arbitrary set of products, regions and transformation processes. You describe the economy with data — demand and supply curves, processes that turn some products into others, transport costs between regions — and a single optimisation returns production, consumption, trade and prices for every region and product.\n\nThe engine knows no specific product or sector. The same data types describe any sector with primary supply, transformation and trade: an oil refinery splitting crude into petrol, diesel and fuel oil, a steel chain from iron ore to cars, a fishery under quota, a dairy turning milk into cheese. Two worked examples ship with the package: the dairy, as the smallest model that is still a market, and the forest-products sector (roundwood → sawnwood, panels, pulp, paper and pellets, with sawmill residues recycled and smallwood either burnt or milled) at full size.\n\nGitHub: https://github.com/sylvaticus/Markets.jl","title":"Markets.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features","page":"Home","text":"Market equilibrium as an optimisation. The competitive equilibrium is computed as the maximum of net social surplus. Quantities are the primal solution; prices are the duals of the material-balance constraints.\nMulti-stage transformation with joint products. A process can have several outputs (e.g. a main product and a residue), and residues can be inputs to other processes.\nSmooth input substitution. Inputs grouped in a CES nest substitute for each other continuously as relative prices change; fixed-proportion (Leontief) inputs are also available.\nLinked regional markets. Regions trade at a transport cost, so equilibrium prices differ across regions by at most that cost.\nImperfect substitution between origins, where you want it. Give a product an Armington elasticity and its regional varieties become imperfect substitutes: regions cross-haul and every region's price responds to supply and demand everywhere. An infinite elasticity, the default, is the homogeneous case above.\nLimits, in one API. Plant capacities, port and pipeline ceilings, quotas, licences, resources shared between products, minimum shares: any linear constraint on the model's quantities is a Limit, written with @limit as the inequality it stands for, and a binding one reports its rent — on the limit, and on every quantity it charges.\nSubstitution in final demand. Products that compete for the same use are grouped in a nest, so one product's price moves another's demand — and substitutes outside the model enter as curve shifters.\nTaxes and subsidies. Wedges on production, consumption, transformation or trade, per unit or ad valorem, with the revenue of each reported.\nTidy results. Every result is a DataFrame.","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/sylvaticus/Markets.jl\")   # this model\n\nPkg.add(\"Markets\") would install the registered v0.0.1 instead: an older and entirely different formulation, which solved price–quantity equations under a least-squares objective and never converged. Its documentation is kept under the v0.0.2 entry of the version selector.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"using Markets\ninclude(joinpath(pkgdir(Markets), \"examples\", \"minimal\", \"minimal_market.jl\"))\nresult.prices\n\nThat script is the Two regions and a dairy page, generated from the source with Literate.jl, as is the larger forest example.","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#How-this-documentation-is-organised","page":"Home","text":"Using the module — how to describe an economy, solve it and read the results.\nExamples — Two regions and a dairy first, the smallest model that is still a market; then the forest sector at full size, in three pages: writing the model, calibrating it with partial data, and what complete data and calibration change.\nModelling choices — what the model assumes and why: partial equilibrium, surplus maximisation, spatial trade, CES substitution, dynamics.\nCode implementation — the exact mathematical program the code builds, how prices are recovered, numerical details, and the API reference.\nReferences — two lists: the papers behind each concept, cited as Author (year), and open-source code in which the same concept can be read, cited as [C1], [C2] and so on.","title":"How this documentation is organised"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_full_data/#The-forest-sector:-complete-data,-and-what-calibration-changes","page":"Complete data, and what calibration changes","text":"The first page built the model from the numbers at hand before any calibration, and the second from incomplete, rounded statistics. This page builds it from complete and consistent data — perfect information, which no real application has — and then asks the question the three pages exist for: how much do the scenarios depend on which of the three you start from?\n\nwarning: For illustrative purposes only\nThe data are plausible, but they are not statistics: complete, consistent data are never observed. They are a base year built to be an equilibrium, so that this page can show what a calibration on perfect information gives.\n\nusing Markets, DataFrames, DelimitedFiles\n\nfrance  = [\"SEF\", \"SWF\", \"GEF\", \"OF\"]\nblocs   = [\"EU\", \"NA\", \"AS\", \"RW\"]\nregions = [france; blocs]\nproducts = [\"swr\", \"hwr\", \"smallwood\", \"chips\", \"pulp\",\n            \"sawn_sw\", \"sawn_hw\", \"panel\", \"plywood\", \"paper\", \"pellets\"]\n\ndata        = pkgdir(Markets, \"examples\", \"forest\", \"data\")\nresults_dir = pkgdir(Markets, \"examples\", \"forest\", \"results\")\n\nfunction read_table(path)\n    cells, header = readdlm(path, ',', header = true)\n    cell(c) = c isa AbstractString ? (isempty(c) ? missing : String(c)) : c\n    return DataFrame([String(h) => identity.(cell.(cells[:, j]))\n                      for (j, h) in enumerate(vec(header))])\nend\nlookup(table, value, keys...) =\n    Dict(Tuple(row[k] for k in keys) => row[value] for row in eachrow(table))","title":"The forest sector: complete data, and what calibration changes"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_full_data/#Everything-there-is-to-know","page":"Complete data, and what calibration changes","text":"Complete data differ from the partial ones in one thing above all: trade. Customs report what crosses a border; complete data give every flow between every pair of regions, French regions included. Everything else is the same kind of observation, only exact:\n\nproduction      = read_table(joinpath(data, \"full\", \"production.csv\"))\nproducer_prices = read_table(joinpath(data, \"full\", \"producer_prices.csv\"))\nconsumption     = read_table(joinpath(data, \"full\", \"consumption.csv\"))\ntrade           = read_table(joinpath(data, \"full\", \"trade.csv\"))\ninput_mix       = read_table(joinpath(data, \"full\", \"input_mix.csv\"))\ncapacities      = read_table(joinpath(data, \"full\", \"capacity.csv\"))\nfreight         = read_table(joinpath(data, \"freight.csv\"))\ndistances       = read_table(joinpath(data, \"distance.csv\"))\nfirst(trade[in.(trade.from, Ref(france)) .& in.(trade.to, Ref(france)), :], 6)\n\u001b[1m6×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m product \u001b[0m\u001b[1m from   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m to     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m quantity \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\n─────┼───────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ swr      SEF     SWF     0.145893\n   2 │ swr      SEF     GEF     0.05081\n   3 │ swr      SEF     OF      0.108489\n   4 │ swr      SWF     SEF     0.161769\n   5 │ swr      SWF     GEF     0.105979\n   6 │ swr      SWF     OF      0.279521\n\nCounted observation by observation, the three pages work from:\n\ndata first page second page this page\nharvest and roadside price, by region 48 48 48\noutput of the mills, by region — 62 62\nproducer prices of the mills' products, by region — 62 62\nconsumer prices 7, one per product derived derived\nconsumption, by region 56 56 56\nuse, by region — 88 implied\ntrade a gravity rule 220 customs flows 595 flows\nthe mix of wood in the mills guessed 14 14\nthe cost of the mills guessed derived derived\ncapacities, freight, distances 47 47 47\n\nThe second page is the realistic one; this one is the limit it tends to.","title":"Everything there is to know"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_full_data/#The-same-calibration,-without-the-gaps","page":"Complete data, and what calibration changes","text":"The steps are those of the second page, explained there, with one fewer: nothing needs imputing. What a region keeps of its own production is still not a trade flow, and is still production less exports.\n\ndist = lookup(distances, :distance, :from, :to)\ndistance(a, b) = a == b ? 0.0 : get(dist, (a, b), get(dist, (b, a), 0.0))\nrate = Dict(zip(freight.product, freight.cost))\ntransport_costs = Dict((p, a, b) => rate[p] * distance(a, b)\n                       for p in products, a in regions, b in regions if a != b)\n\nlog_duty = 0.25\ntaxes = [Tax(name = \"rw_log_duty\", on = \"trade\", product = [\"swr\", \"hwr\"], from = \"RW\",\n             rate = log_duty, relative = true)]\nduty(p, from) = from == \"RW\" && p in (\"swr\", \"hwr\") ? log_duty : 0.0\nevacuation = @limit(\"landes_evacuation\",\n                    exports(region = \"SWF\", product = [\"swr\", \"smallwood\"]) <= 18.0)\ngas_reference = 30.0\n\nmade     = lookup(production, :quantity, :region, :product)\nπˢ       = lookup(producer_prices, :price, :region, :product)\nconsumed = lookup(consumption, :quantity, :region, :product)\n\nflows = lookup(trade, :quantity, :product, :from, :to)\nexported(r, p) = sum(x for ((q, o, _), x) in flows if q == p && o == r; init = 0.0)\nfor ((r, p), q) in made\n    flows[(p, r, r)] = q - exported(r, p)\nend\n\ndelivered(p, o, r) = o == r ? πˢ[(o, p)] :\n                     πˢ[(o, p)] * (1 + duty(p, o)) + transport_costs[(p, o, r)]\nπᶜ = Dict{Tuple{String,String},Float64}()\nfor r in regions, p in products\n    bought = [(o, x) for ((q, o, d), x) in flows if q == p && d == r && x > 0]\n    isempty(bought) && continue\n    πᶜ[(r, p)] = sum(x * delivered(p, o, r) for (o, x) in bought) / sum(last, bought)\nend\n\nThe behaviour, chosen as on the other pages:\n\nsupply_elasticity = Dict(\n    \"swr\"       => Dict(\"SEF\" => 0.40, \"SWF\" => 0.45, \"GEF\" => 0.40, \"OF\" => 0.40,\n                        \"EU\"  => 0.60, \"NA\"  => 0.60, \"AS\"  => 0.55, \"RW\" => 0.55),\n    \"hwr\"       => Dict(\"SEF\" => 0.35, \"SWF\" => 0.35, \"GEF\" => 0.35, \"OF\" => 0.35,\n                        \"EU\"  => 0.50, \"NA\"  => 0.50, \"AS\"  => 0.50, \"RW\" => 0.50),\n    \"smallwood\" => Dict(\"SEF\" => 0.50, \"SWF\" => 0.55, \"GEF\" => 0.50, \"OF\" => 0.50,\n                        \"EU\"  => 0.60, \"NA\"  => 0.60, \"AS\"  => 0.55, \"RW\" => 0.55))\nη_construction, η_fuel, η_paper = 0.45, 0.35, 0.30\nσ_lumber, σ_construction, σ_fuel = 5.0, 1.6, 3.0\nlumber = [\"sawn_sw\", \"panel\", \"plywood\"]\nfuels  = [\"smallwood\", \"pellets\"]\ngas_cross_elasticity = 0.35\nσ_mills = Dict(\"plymill\" => 1.8, \"panelmill\" => 2.5, \"pulpmill\" => 1.8, \"pelletmill\" => 3.0)\nσ_blocs = Dict(\"swr\" => 6.0, \"hwr\" => 6.0, \"smallwood\" => 7.0, \"chips\" => 7.0,\n               \"pulp\" => 5.0, \"sawn_sw\" => 3.5, \"sawn_hw\" => 3.0, \"panel\" => 3.0,\n               \"plywood\" => 2.5, \"paper\" => 4.0, \"pellets\" => 6.0)\nσ_europe = Dict(p => 3σ for (p, σ) in σ_blocs)\nσ_france = 30.0\neurope(p) = OriginNest(sigma = σ_europe[p], origins = [\"EU\"],\n                       nests = [OriginNest(sigma = σ_france, origins = france)])\n\nTrade shares, supply and demand curves:\n\narmington = [armington_calibration(\n                 product = p, sigma = σ_blocs[p], nests = [europe(p)],\n                 flows   = Dict((o, r) => x for ((q, o, r), x) in flows if q == p),\n                 prices  = Dict((o, r) => delivered(p, o, r)\n                                for o in regions, r in regions if haskey(πˢ, (o, p))))\n             for p in products]\n\nsupply = [SupplyCurve(product = p, region = r, p0 = πˢ[(r, p)], q0 = made[(r, p)],\n                     elasticity = supply_elasticity[p][r])\n          for p in keys(supply_elasticity) for r in regions]\n\nfunction construction_demand(r)\n    inner = ces_calibration(quantities = [consumed[(r, p)] for p in lumber],\n                            prices     = [πᶜ[(r, p)] for p in lumber], sigma = σ_lumber)\n    outer = ces_calibration(quantities = [consumed[(r, \"sawn_hw\")], inner.quantity],\n                            prices     = [πᶜ[(r, \"sawn_hw\")], inner.price],\n                            sigma = σ_construction)\n    return DemandCurve(region = r, p0 = outer.price, q0 = outer.quantity,\n        elasticity = η_construction,\n        product = DemandNest(sigma = σ_construction,\n            products = [\"sawn_hw\"], shares = [outer.shares[1]],\n            nests = [DemandNest(sigma = σ_lumber, share = outer.shares[2],\n                                products = lumber, shares = inner.shares)]))\nend\nfunction fuel_demand(r, gas = gas_reference)\n    fuel = ces_calibration(quantities = [consumed[(r, p)] for p in fuels],\n                           prices     = [πᶜ[(r, p)] for p in fuels], sigma = σ_fuel)\n    return DemandCurve(region = r, p0 = fuel.price, q0 = fuel.quantity, elasticity = η_fuel,\n        shift = exogenous_shift(gas = (level = gas, reference = gas_reference,\n                                       elasticity = gas_cross_elasticity)),\n        product = DemandNest(sigma = σ_fuel, products = fuels, shares = fuel.shares))\nend\nbuild_demand(gas = gas_reference) =\n    [[construction_demand(r) for r in regions];\n     [fuel_demand(r, gas) for r in regions];\n     [DemandCurve(product = \"paper\", region = r, p0 = πᶜ[(r, \"paper\")],\n                 q0 = consumed[(r, \"paper\")], elasticity = η_paper) for r in regions]]\ndemand = build_demand()\n\nand the mills, their bundles from the observed mix and their costs from their margins:\n\nfunction bundle(process)\n    mix = input_mix[input_mix.process .== process, :]\n    c = ces_calibration(quantities = mix.quantity,\n                        prices = [πᶜ[(r, p)] for (r, p) in zip(mix.region, mix.product)],\n                        sigma = σ_mills[process])\n    return ces(composite = sum(mix.quantity), products = mix.product, shares = c.shares,\n               sigma = σ_mills[process], phi = c.scale)\nend\ntechnology = [\n    (\"sawmill_sw\", [leontief(product = \"swr\", coeff = 1.0)], [\"sawn_sw\" => 0.50, \"chips\" => 0.35]),\n    (\"sawmill_hw\", [leontief(product = \"hwr\", coeff = 1.0)], [\"sawn_hw\" => 0.45, \"chips\" => 0.35]),\n    (\"plymill\",    [bundle(\"plymill\")],                      [\"plywood\" => 1.0,  \"chips\" => 0.8]),\n    (\"panelmill\",  [bundle(\"panelmill\")],                    [\"panel\"   => 1.0]),\n    (\"pulpmill\",   [bundle(\"pulpmill\")],                     [\"pulp\"    => 1.0]),\n    (\"papermill\",  [leontief(product = \"pulp\", coeff = 1.10)], [\"paper\" => 1.0]),\n    (\"pelletmill\", [bundle(\"pelletmill\")],                   [\"pellets\" => 1.0]),\n]\nprice_index(δ, p, σ) = sum(δ .* p .^ (1 - σ))^(1 / (1 - σ))\ninput_cost(n::Nest, r) = length(n.products) == 1 ?\n    n.composite * πᶜ[(r, only(n.products))] :\n    n.composite * price_index(n.shares, [πᶜ[(r, p)] for p in n.products], n.sigma) / n.phi\nmargin(inputs, outputs, r) = sum(y * πˢ[(r, k)] for (k, y) in outputs) -\n                             sum(input_cost(n, r) for n in inputs)\noperating(outputs) = [r for r in regions if get(made, (r, first(first(outputs))), 0.0) > 0]\nplant_limits = [@limit(\"$(row.process) plant $(row.region)\",\n                       activity(region = row.region, process = row.process) <= row.capacity)\n                for row in eachrow(capacities)]\nprocesses = [Process(name = name, inputs = inputs, outputs = outputs,\n                     regions  = operating(outputs),\n                     vacost   = Dict(r => margin(inputs, outputs, r) for r in operating(outputs)))\n             for (name, inputs, outputs) in technology]\n\ncomplete = MarketData(; regions, products, demand, supply, processes,\n                        transport_costs, armington, taxes,\n                        limits = [plant_limits; evacuation])\nres = solve_market(complete; solver_options = (tol = 1e-10,), tol = 1e-9)\n\nThe solver's tolerance is tighter than the default, and so is the one below which the result tables leave a flow out, only so that the comparison that follows is sharp.","title":"The same calibration, without the gaps"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_full_data/#The-data,-returned","page":"Complete data, and what calibration changes","text":"Consistent data are an equilibrium of the calibrated model, and a convex program has only one: the model must give them back, to the precision of the solver. That is benchmark replication, the standard test that the data and the structure agree:\n\nmodelled(table, value, keys...) = lookup(table, value, keys...)\nrelative_gaps(model, observed) = sort([abs(get(model, k, 0.0) / v - 1) for (k, v) in observed])\nshipped = Dict(k => x for (k, x) in flows if k[2] != k[3])\n\nDataFrame((observed = name, entries = length(observed),\n           largest_gap = last(relative_gaps(model, observed)))\n          for (name, model, observed) in (\n              (\"producer prices\", modelled(res.prices, :producer_price, :region, :product), πˢ),\n              (\"consumer prices\", modelled(res.prices, :price, :region, :product), πᶜ),\n              (\"production\",  modelled(res.production, :quantity, :region, :product), made),\n              (\"consumption\", modelled(res.consumption, :quantity, :region, :product), consumed),\n              (\"trade\",       modelled(res.trade, :quantity, :product, :from, :to), shipped)))\n\u001b[1m5×3 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m observed        \u001b[0m\u001b[1m entries \u001b[0m\u001b[1m largest_gap \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String          \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64     \u001b[0m\n─────┼───────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ producer prices       86   2.56482e-9\n   2 │ consumer prices       88   2.01923e-9\n   3 │ production            86   6.05252e-8\n   4 │ consumption           56   5.26678e-9\n   5 │ trade                602   7.36551e-7\n\nEvery price, quantity and flow comes back. The model knew none of them: it was given curves, shares and costs, and found the base year as the point where they balance.","title":"The data, returned"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/forest_full_data/#Three-reference-runs,-two-scenarios","page":"Complete data, and what calibration changes","text":"The storm in the Landes and the dearer gas, once more:\n\nreconfigure(d; kwargs...) =\n    MarketData(; (f => get(kwargs, f, getfield(d, f)) for f in fieldnames(MarketData))...)\nsalvage = [s.region == \"SWF\" && s.product == \"swr\" ?\n               SupplyCurve(s; shift = 3.0) : s\n           for s in supply]\nstorm = solve_market(reconfigure(complete, supply = salvage))\ngas   = solve_market(reconfigure(complete, demand = build_demand(60.0)))\n\nThe other two pages saved their reference runs and scenarios. Put side by side with this one, in the same shape:\n\nfunction outcome(result, scenario)\n    t = outerjoin(select(result.prices, :region, :product, :price, :producer_price),\n                  rename(result.production, :quantity => :production),\n                  select(result.consumption, :region, :product, :quantity => :consumption),\n                  on = [:region, :product])\n    return insertcols!(t, 1, :scenario => scenario)\nend\n\ncases = [\"uncalibrated (first page)\"  => read_table(joinpath(results_dir, \"uncalibrated.csv\")),\n         \"partial data (second page)\" => read_table(joinpath(results_dir, \"partial.csv\")),\n         \"complete data (this page)\"  => vcat(outcome(res, \"reference\"), outcome(storm, \"storm\"),\n                                              outcome(gas, \"gas\"))]\n\nFor each, how far its reference run sits from the complete data, and what the two scenarios do to it:\n\nvalue(t, scenario, r, p, column) =\n    (rows = t[(t.scenario .== scenario) .& (t.region .== r) .& (t.product .== p), column];\n     isempty(rows) ? missing : only(rows))\nchange(t, scenario, r, p, column) =\n    100 * (value(t, scenario, r, p, column) / value(t, \"reference\", r, p, column) - 1)\nmedian_and_largest(gaps) = (round(100 * gaps[cld(end, 2)]; digits = 1),\n                            round(100 * last(gaps); digits = 1))\nspan(xs) = string(round(minimum(xs); digits = 1), \" to \", round(maximum(xs); digits = 1))\n\ncomparison = DataFrame(\n    (case = name,\n     producer_prices_gap = median_and_largest(relative_gaps(\n         Dict((r, p) => value(t, \"reference\", r, p, :producer_price) for (r, p) in keys(πˢ)), πˢ)),\n     production_gap = median_and_largest(relative_gaps(\n         Dict((r, p) => coalesce(value(t, \"reference\", r, p, :production), 0.0)\n              for (r, p) in keys(made)), made)),\n     storm_SWF = round(change(t, \"storm\", \"SWF\", \"swr\", :price); digits = 1),\n     storm_elsewhere = span(change(t, \"storm\", r, \"swr\", :price) for r in regions if r != \"SWF\"),\n     gas_pellets  = span(change(t, \"gas\", r, \"pellets\", :consumption) for r in regions),\n     gas_firewood = span(change(t, \"gas\", r, \"smallwood\", :consumption) for r in regions))\n    for (name, t) in cases)\n\u001b[1m3×7 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m case                       \u001b[0m\u001b[1m producer_prices_gap \u001b[0m\u001b[1m production_gap \u001b[0m\u001b[1m storm_\u001b[0m ⋯\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String                     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Tuple…              \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Tuple…         \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float6\u001b[0m ⋯\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ uncalibrated (first page)   (5.4, 24.7)          (8.5, 117.0)        -1 ⋯\n   2 │ partial data (second page)  (0.1, 0.7)           (0.1, 0.5)           -\n   3 │ complete data (this page)   (0.0, 0.0)           (0.0, 0.0)           -\n\u001b[36m                                                               4 columns omitted\u001b[0m\n\nThe gaps are percentages, median and largest; the scenarios are percentage changes from each case's own reference run: the log price in SWF and in the other regions under the storm, and the pellets and firewood consumed under the dearer gas.\n\nThe partial data give practically the results of perfect information. Their reference run sits within a per cent of the complete data — the rounding — and the scenarios agree to a tenth or two of a point, although every flow touching a French region was imputed rather than observed. Detail that is missing costs little as long as the totals around it are right.\n\nThe uncalibrated reference run is another matter. Its prices are 5% off in the median and a quarter at worst, and the output of some mills is off by double — the French ones, whose costs and recipes were guessed. Where a scenario runs into those errors, its result changes with them:\n\nthe storm takes the softwood price in SWF down by 12% instead of 5%, because the uncalibrated run already ships about 13 of the 18 Mm³ the Landes roads allow, against about 10 in the data, so the limit binds sooner and the salvage has nowhere to go;\nthe dearer gas moves pellets and firewood by different amounts, because the guessed mills have different margins and different room in their plants.\n\nWhere the scenario runs into nothing of the kind, the uncalibrated run gets it about right: the storm's effect outside SWF agrees within a few tenths of a point, because constant-elasticity curves give the same percentage response wherever they are evaluated.\n\nSo calibration matters, but not everywhere equally, and perfect data are not what it takes: a reference run in the right place — on the right totals, at the right prices, at the right distance from the limits that matter — is enough to get the scenarios right.\n\nThe general lessons — what a scenario is robust to, what it is not, and where to spend the effort on data — are drawn in Calibrating with partial data.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Three reference runs, two scenarios"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/spatequ/#Takayama-and-Judge:-two-products,-three-regions","page":"Takayama and Judge (GAMS spatequ)","text":"The spatial equilibrium of Takayama and Judge (1964) is where models like this one started: regions with their own supply and demand, a cost of moving goods between them, and an equilibrium found by maximising the surplus net of transport. Its numerical example is spatequ in the GAMS model library, contributed by Phan Sy Hieu, and this page ports it.\n\nIt is small enough to check by hand, which is the point: a port is only worth something once it is shown to reproduce what it ports.\n\nusing Markets, DataFrames","title":"Takayama and Judge: two products, three regions"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/spatequ/#The-data","page":"Takayama and Judge (GAMS spatequ)","text":"Three regions, two commodities, and straight-line supply and demand: quantity as a function of price, Q = α + β·P.\n\nregions  = [\"Reg1\", \"Reg2\", \"Reg3\"]\nproducts = [\"Com1\", \"Com2\"]\n\nThe GAMS tables give the intercepts, and a 2×2 matrix of price coefficients per region, with cross-price terms.\n\nalpha_d = Dict((\"Reg1\", \"Com1\") => 200, (\"Reg1\", \"Com2\") => 300,\n               (\"Reg2\", \"Com1\") => 100, (\"Reg2\", \"Com2\") => 200,\n               (\"Reg3\", \"Com1\") => 160, (\"Reg3\", \"Com2\") => 250)\nbeta_d  = Dict((\"Reg1\", \"Com1\") => [-10, 1], (\"Reg1\", \"Com2\") => [1, -10],\n               (\"Reg2\", \"Com1\") => [-5, 1],  (\"Reg2\", \"Com2\") => [1, -20],\n               (\"Reg3\", \"Com1\") => [-8, 1],  (\"Reg3\", \"Com2\") => [1, -10])\nalpha_s = Dict((r, c) => (c == \"Com1\" ? -50 : -60) for r in regions, c in products)\nbeta_s  = Dict((\"Reg1\", \"Com1\") => [10, 0.5], (\"Reg1\", \"Com2\") => [0.5, 15],\n               (\"Reg2\", \"Com1\") => [20, 0.5], (\"Reg2\", \"Com2\") => [0.5, 25],\n               (\"Reg3\", \"Com1\") => [10, 0.5], (\"Reg3\", \"Com2\") => [0.5, 15])\n\nWhat the GAMS model actually solves. Its demand equation is\n\nDEM(r,c)..  AlphaD(r,c) + sum(cc, BetaD(r,c,cc)*P(r,c)) =e= Qd(r,c);\n\nand it multiplies every coefficient by P(r,c), the product's own price, not by P(r,cc); the supply equation does the same. As written, then, the cross-price terms are folded into the own-price slope, the two commodities are independent markets, and the slope of each is the sum of its row: −10 + 1 = −9 for Com1 in Reg1. That is what its LP and its complementarity version solve, and what is ported here. (A demand system with cross-price terms between two products would be a DemandNest in this package, whose substitution is a CES rather than a free linear matrix.)\n\nslope(row) = sum(row)\ndemand = [DemandCurve(product = c, region = r, alpha = alpha_d[(r, c)],\n                     beta = slope(beta_d[(r, c)]))\n          for r in regions for c in products]\nsupply = [SupplyCurve(product = c, region = r, alpha = alpha_s[(r, c)],\n                     beta = slope(beta_s[(r, c)]))\n          for r in regions for c in products]\ndemand[1]\nDemandCurve(product = \"Com1\", region = \"Reg1\", alpha = 200.0, beta = -9.0)\n\nTransport costs per unit, the same each way:\n\ncost = Dict((\"Reg1\", \"Reg2\") => (2, 3), (\"Reg1\", \"Reg3\") => (2, 3),\n            (\"Reg2\", \"Reg3\") => (1, 2))\ntransport_costs = Dict{Tuple{String,String,String},Float64}()\nfor ((a, b), (c1, c2)) in cost, (from, to) in ((a, b), (b, a))\n    transport_costs[(\"Com1\", from, to)] = c1\n    transport_costs[(\"Com2\", from, to)] = c2\nend","title":"The data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/spatequ/#The-equilibrium","page":"Takayama and Judge (GAMS spatequ)","text":"market = MarketData(regions = regions, products = products, demand = demand,\n                    supply = supply, transport_costs = transport_costs)\nresult = solve_market(market)\nresult.prices\n\u001b[1m6×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m product \u001b[0m\u001b[1m price    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m producer_price \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64        \u001b[0m\n─────┼───────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ Reg1    Com1     11.0            11.0\n   2 │ Reg1    Com2     11.6364         11.6364\n   3 │ Reg2    Com1      9.0             9.0\n   4 │ Reg2    Com2      8.63636         8.63636\n   5 │ Reg3    Com1     10.0            10.0\n   6 │ Reg3    Com2     10.6364         10.6364\n\nReg2 is where both commodities are cheapest: its supply responds most to the price, and its demand is the smallest. Both move out of it to the two other regions, whose prices sit exactly one transport cost above.\n\nresult.trade\n\u001b[1m4×6 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m product \u001b[0m\u001b[1m from   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m to     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m quantity \u001b[0m\u001b[1m capacity \u001b[0m\u001b[1m rent    \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ Com1     Reg2    Reg1     35.5          Inf      0.0\n   2 │ Com1     Reg2    Reg3     35.0          Inf      0.0\n   3 │ Com2     Reg2    Reg1     74.9091       Inf      0.0\n   4 │ Com2     Reg2    Reg3     49.4091       Inf      0.0","title":"The equilibrium"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/spatequ/#Checking-the-port","page":"Takayama and Judge (GAMS spatequ)","text":"The equilibrium of the GAMS model is characterised by its complementarity version: every quantity on its curve, every region in balance, no price gap wider than the cost of closing it, and an exact gap wherever goods move. Each condition can be checked on the result directly.\n\nprice_of(r, c) = only(result.prices[(result.prices.region .== r) .& (result.prices.product .== c), :price])\nquantity(df, r, c) = (rows = df[(df.region .== r) .& (df.product .== c), :quantity];\n                      isempty(rows) ? 0.0 : only(rows))\nshipped(c, from, to) = (rows = result.trade[(result.trade.product .== c) .& (result.trade.from .== from) .&\n                                            (result.trade.to .== to), :quantity];\n                        isempty(rows) ? 0.0 : only(rows))\nchecks = DataFrame(region = String[], product = String[], price = Float64[],\n                   demand_gap = Float64[], supply_gap = Float64[], balance_gap = Float64[])\nfor r in regions, c in products\n    p = price_of(r, c)\n    qd, qs = quantity(result.consumption, r, c), quantity(result.production, r, c)\n    inflow  = sum(shipped(c, o, r) for o in regions if o != r)\n    outflow = sum(shipped(c, r, o) for o in regions if o != r)\n    push!(checks, (r, c, p,\n                   qd - (alpha_d[(r, c)] + slope(beta_d[(r, c)]) * p),\n                   qs - (alpha_s[(r, c)] + slope(beta_s[(r, c)]) * p),\n                   qs + inflow - outflow - qd))\nend\nchecks\n\u001b[1m6×6 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m region \u001b[0m\u001b[1m product \u001b[0m\u001b[1m price    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m demand_gap  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m supply_gap  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m balance_gap \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64     \u001b[0m\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ Reg1    Com1     11.0      2.23068e-10  4.01471e-10  -1.10441e-8\n   2 │ Reg1    Com2     11.6364   1.15222e-10  3.22302e-10  -1.02088e-8\n   3 │ Reg2    Com1      9.0      1.56511e-10  3.81419e-10   1.81206e-8\n   4 │ Reg2    Com2      8.63636  1.32544e-9   3.9816e-10    1.89559e-8\n   5 │ Reg3    Com1     10.0      1.94717e-10  4.78146e-10  -7.07648e-9\n   6 │ Reg3    Com2     10.6364   1.45945e-10  3.70008e-10  -8.74707e-9\n\nThe gaps are at the solver's tolerance. And between any two regions, the price difference never exceeds the transport cost, and equals it on every route used:\n\narbitrage = DataFrame(product = String[], from = String[], to = String[],\n                      price_gap = Float64[], cost = Float64[], shipped = Float64[])\nfor ((c, from, to), τ) in sort(collect(transport_costs))\n    push!(arbitrage, (c, from, to, price_of(to, c) - price_of(from, c), τ, shipped(c, from, to)))\nend\narbitrage\n\u001b[1m12×6 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m product \u001b[0m\u001b[1m from   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m to     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m price_gap \u001b[0m\u001b[1m cost    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m shipped \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ Com1     Reg1    Reg2         -2.0      2.0   0.0\n   2 │ Com1     Reg1    Reg3         -1.0      2.0   0.0\n   3 │ Com1     Reg2    Reg1          2.0      2.0  35.5\n   4 │ Com1     Reg2    Reg3          1.0      1.0  35.0\n   5 │ Com1     Reg3    Reg1          1.0      2.0   0.0\n   6 │ Com1     Reg3    Reg2         -1.0      1.0   0.0\n   7 │ Com2     Reg1    Reg2         -3.0      3.0   0.0\n   8 │ Com2     Reg1    Reg3         -1.0      3.0   0.0\n   9 │ Com2     Reg2    Reg1          3.0      3.0  74.9091\n  10 │ Com2     Reg2    Reg3          2.0      2.0  49.4091\n  11 │ Com2     Reg3    Reg1          1.0      3.0   0.0\n  12 │ Com2     Reg3    Reg2         -2.0      2.0   0.0\n\nThose are the conditions the GAMS complementarity model P2R3_MCP imposes, so this is its solution. What took four model statements there is one MarketData here, and the same economy can now be given processes, limits, taxes or imperfect substitution between origins without being written again.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Checking the port"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#References","page":"References","text":"Two lists, kept apart on purpose, because they answer two different questions about any piece of a model.\n\nWhere does this idea come from? is answered by the theory list, cited as Author (year) — the paper that introduced the concept, or the one that settled how it is used.\n\nWhere can I see it in working code? is answered by the implementation list, cited as [C1], [C2] and so on — a repository, preferably open source, in which the same concept is written out and runs. Reading a CES nest in an article and reading one in a solver's input are different kinds of understanding, and a modelling package is in a position to offer both.\n\nSo a sentence in these pages may read: the spatial price equilibrium of Samuelson (1952), implemented for the forest sector in [C3]. The first takes you to the argument, the second to the code.\n\nEntries are listed alphabetically within each list. Every DOI and repository below was checked against CrossRef or the GitHub API when the page was written; where no DOI exists — papers of the 1950s mostly predate them — the journal, volume and pages are given instead.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#Theory","page":"References","text":"","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#armington1969","page":"References","text":"A theory of demand for products distinguished by place of production. Staff Papers — International Monetary Fund 16(1), 159–178. doi:10.2307/3866403\n\nThe origin of treating goods from different places as imperfect substitutes. Used throughout Imperfect substitution between origins (Armington).","title":"Armington, P. S. (1969)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#acms1961","page":"References","text":"Capital-labor substitution and economic efficiency. The Review of Economics and Statistics 43(3), 225–250. doi:10.2307/1927286\n\nThe constant-elasticity-of-substitution function itself, which this package uses three times over: for process inputs, for origins and for substitutes in final demand.","title":"Arrow, K. J., Chenery, H. B., Minhas, B. S. & Solow, R. M. (1961)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#buongiorno2015","page":"References","text":"Income and time dependence of forest product demand elasticities and implications for forecasting. Silva Fennica 49(5), 1395. doi:10.14214/sf.1395\n\nEvidence on the size of forest-product demand elasticities, and so on the inelastic range this package deliberately supports; see Demand and supply curves.","title":"Buongiorno, J. (2015)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#gfpm2003","page":"References","text":"The Global Forest Products Model: structure, estimation, and applications. Academic Press. doi:10.1016/b978-012141362-0/50004-2 (the chapter stating the formulation)\n\nThe reference forest-sector partial-equilibrium model, and the lineage this package's example belongs to.","title":"Buongiorno, J., Zhu, S., Zhang, D., Turner, J. & Tomberlin, D. (2003)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#enke1951","page":"References","text":"Equilibrium among spatially separated markets: solution by electric analogue. Econometrica 19(1), 40–47. doi:10.2307/1907907\n\nThe problem posed, one year before Samuelson turned it into an optimisation — and solved, memorably, by building the circuit.","title":"Enke, S. (1951)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#ferris1997","page":"References","text":"Engineering and economic applications of complementarity problems. SIAM Review 39(4), 669–713. doi:10.1137/S0036144595285963\n\nThe survey of what complementarity formulations can express that an optimisation cannot, which is the case for leaving the route this package takes; see Why processes, and not supply and demand per transformation.","title":"Ferris, M. C. & Pang, J.-S. (1997)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#glpp2026","page":"References","text":"Source taxes versus end-of-chain taxes in general equilibrium. Working paper, July 2026.\n\nWhen taxing an externality where it arises and taxing it as embodied in final goods are equivalent, and why the second needs prices that depend on each good's content. See Where along the chain to tax.","title":"Gerlagh, R., Lorang, E., Pommeret, A. & Pottier, A. (2026)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#howitt1995","page":"References","text":"Positive mathematical programming. American Journal of Agricultural Economics 77(2), 329–342. doi:10.2307/1243543\n\nCalibrating a programming model so that the observed activity is an interior optimum rather than a corner held by a constraint — the standard remedy for the constant-returns behaviour discussed in Why processes, and not supply and demand per transformation.","title":"Howitt, R. E. (1995)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#latta2013","page":"References","text":"A review of recent developments and applications of partial equilibrium models of the forest sector. Journal of Forest Economics 19(4), 350–360. doi:10.1016/j.jfe.2013.06.006\n\nWhat the forest-sector partial-equilibrium literature contains and how its models are built, which is the lineage this package's example belongs to.","title":"Latta, G. S., Sjølie, H. K. & Solberg, B. (2013)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#lorang2026","page":"References","text":"Resource extraction taxes versus waste taxes in general equilibrium. Working paper, April 2026.\n\nMaterial balances kept jointly with market balances by letting the material intensity of every good follow from the allocation, which is what material_accounts does after the solve; and the efficiency gap between taxing material at extraction and at disposal. See Material accounts and Where along the chain to tax.","title":"Lorang, E. & Gerlagh, R. (2026)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#jump2023","page":"References","text":"JuMP 1.0: recent improvements to a modeling language for mathematical optimization. Mathematical Programming Computation 15(3), 581–589. doi:10.1007/s12532-023-00239-3\n\nThe modelling layer this package is written in; see [C1].","title":"Lubin, M., Dowson, O., Garcia, J., Huchette, J. & Legat, B. (2023)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#mathiesen1985","page":"References","text":"Computation of economic equilibria by a sequence of linear complementarity problems. Mathematical Programming Studies, 144–162. doi:10.1007/bfb0121030\n\nEquilibrium as a complementarity problem — the alternative to the optimisation route taken here, and the one that would make ad valorem tariffs exact; see Why ad valorem rates on trade are special.","title":"Mathiesen, L. (1985)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#mccarl1980","page":"References","text":"Price endogenous mathematical programming as a tool for sector analysis. American Journal of Agricultural Economics 62(1), 87–102. doi:10.2307/1239475\n\nThe case for representing a sector as a programming problem whose prices are duals, including what integrability demands of it. Directly on the choice made in Why processes, and not supply and demand per transformation.","title":"McCarl, B. A. & Spreen, T. H. (1980)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#mcdaniel2003","page":"References","text":"A review of Armington trade substitution elasticities. Économie internationale 94–95(2), 301–313. doi:10.3917/ecoi.094.0301\n\nWhat the Armington elasticity has actually been estimated to be, and how widely the estimates vary — worth reading before choosing a sigma.","title":"McDaniel, C. A. & Balistreri, E. J. (2003)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#rutherford1995","page":"References","text":"Extension of GAMS for complementarity problems arising in applied economic analysis. Journal of Economic Dynamics and Control 19(8), 1299–1324. doi:10.1016/0165-1889(94)00831-2\n\nMPSGE: equilibrium models written as complementarity problems, with CES and Armington nests as primitives. Implemented in [C4].","title":"Rutherford, T. F. (1995)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#samuelson1952","page":"References","text":"Spatial price equilibrium and linear programming. American Economic Review 42, 283–303. (Predates DOIs; available on JSTOR.)\n\nThe result this package rests on: that the competitive spatial equilibrium is the maximum of net social surplus. See The equilibrium as surplus maximisation.","title":"Samuelson, P. A. (1952)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#sato1967","page":"References","text":"A two-level constant-elasticity-of-substitution production function. The Review of Economic Studies 34(2), 201–218. doi:10.2307/2296809\n\nNesting one CES inside another, which is how this package lets substitution differ between pairs — between origins, and between products in demand — without an inconsistent matrix of elasticities.","title":"Sato, K. (1967)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#takayama1964","page":"References","text":"Equilibrium among spatially separated markets: a reformulation. Econometrica 32(4), 510–524. doi:10.2307/1910175\n\nThe quadratic-programming formulation of the spatial equilibrium, and the standard reference for solving it numerically. Extended in their 1971 book Spatial and Temporal Price and Allocation Models (North-Holland).","title":"Takayama, T. & Judge, G. G. (1964)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#vdmp2016","page":"References","text":"Volume preserving CES and CET formulations. Paper presented at the 19th Annual Conference on Global Economic Analysis, Washington DC, June 2016. Center for Global Trade Analysis, Purdue University.\n\nThe additive CES and CET, which keep imperfect substitution and make the aggregate a plain sum, and how far the standard forms drift from one. See What it would take to make the composites add up.","title":"van der Mensbrugghe, D. & Peters, J. C. (2016)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#vantongeren2001","page":"References","text":"Global models applied to agricultural and trade policies: a review and assessment. Agricultural Economics 26(2), 149–172. doi:10.1111/j.1574-0862.2001.tb00060.x\n\nA classification of sector models by what they assume rather than by what they cover, and the source for which paradigm prevails in which community.","title":"van Tongeren, F., van Meijl, H. & Surry, Y. (2001)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#wachter2006","page":"References","text":"On the implementation of an interior-point filter line-search algorithm for large-scale nonlinear programming. Mathematical Programming 106(1), 25–57. doi:10.1007/s10107-004-0559-y\n\nThe algorithm behind Ipopt, this package's default solver — and the reason quantities raised to a power below one need a floor away from zero; see Numerical details. Implemented in [C2].","title":"Wächter, A. & Biegler, L. T. (2006)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#Implementations","page":"References","text":"","title":"Implementations"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#C1","page":"References","text":"https://github.com/jump-dev/JuMP.jl\n\nThe modelling language this package is built on: every variable, constraint and objective in src/model.jl is a JuMP expression, and the duals that become prices are read back through it. Described in Lubin et al. (2023).","title":"[C1] JuMP.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#C2","page":"References","text":"https://github.com/coin-or/Ipopt — EPL-2.0\n\nThe interior-point solver used by default, through JuMP. Any solver handling nonlinear constraints and returning duals can replace it. Described in Wächter & Biegler (2006).","title":"[C2] Ipopt"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#C3","page":"References","text":"https://github.com/TI-Forest-Sector-Modelling/TiMBA — AGPL-3.0 — doi:10.21105/joss.08034\n\nThe closest open-source relative of this package: a spatial partial equilibrium of the global forest sector, in Python, built on the GFPM formulation and solved — as here — by maximising social surplus period by period. Read it to see the same economics with different choices made about dynamics, trade and data.","title":"[C3] TiMBA"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#C4","page":"References","text":"https://github.com/julia-mpsge/MPSGE.jl — MIT\n\nThe complementarity route in Julia, implementing Rutherford's (1995) MPSGE. Nested CES and Armington structures are primitives of the language rather than constraints one writes out, and prices are variables rather than duals — which is what lets it treat an ad valorem tax exactly where this package has to iterate.","title":"[C4] MPSGE.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#C5","page":"References","text":"https://github.com/chkwon/PATHSolver.jl — MIT\n\nThe mixed-complementarity solver those formulations are solved with, wrapped for Julia; Complementarity.jl provides the modelling layer above it.","title":"[C5] PATHSolver.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/egypt/#Egyptian-agriculture,-1980","page":"Egyptian agriculture (GAMS egypt)","text":"In the late 1970s the government of Egypt and the UNDP drew up a master plan for the country's water: how much the Nile could supply, month by month, and what it would be worth to invest in more. At its centre was an agricultural sector model by Gerald Kutcher, published with the World Bank's sector models and kept, in a compact version, as egypt in the GAMS model library.\n\nIt belongs to the same family as this package: farmers choose what to grow, region by region, and the national market sets prices where the surplus of consumers and producers together is largest. This page ports it, checks the port against a line-by-line transcription of the original, and then asks it the question it was built for.\n\nKutcher, G. P. (1980), The Agro-Economic Model, Master Plan for Water Resources Development, UNDP-EGY-73/024.\n\nusing Markets, DataFrames, DelimitedFiles","title":"Egyptian agriculture, 1980"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/egypt/#The-data","page":"Egyptian agriculture (GAMS egypt)","text":"The GAMS tables are in examples/egypt/data, one CSV file each, in long format: one value per row, keyed as in the original.\n\ndata = pkgdir(Markets, \"examples\", \"egypt\", \"data\")\nfunction read_table(file)\n    cells, header = readdlm(joinpath(data, file), ',', header = true)\n    text(c) = c isa AbstractString ? String(c) : c\n    return DataFrame([String(h) => identity.(text.(cells[:, j])) for (j, h) in enumerate(vec(header))])\nend\n# a table as a dictionary from its keys to its value, which is the last column\nfunction table(file)\n    t = read_table(file)\n    keys = names(t)[1:end-1]\n    key(row) = length(keys) == 1 ? row[keys[1]] : Tuple(row[k] for k in keys)\n    return Dict(key(row) => float(row[end]) for row in eachrow(t))\nend\n\nFive regions, named after the Nile barrages that water them, in three agricultural zones; the farming calendar starts in October.\n\nfarms  = [\"u-egypt\", \"m-egypt\", \"e-delta\", \"m-delta\", \"w-delta\"]\nzone   = Dict(\"u-egypt\" => \"upper\", \"m-egypt\" => \"middle\", \"e-delta\" => \"delta\",\n              \"m-delta\" => \"delta\", \"w-delta\" => \"delta\")\nmonths = [\"oct\", \"nov\", \"dec\", \"jan\", \"feb\", \"mar\", \"apr\", \"may\", \"jun\", \"jul\", \"aug\", \"sep\"]\n\nThe crops: yields by region, per feddan (0.42 ha); land, labour and water needed in each month, by zone; fodder left for the animals, by zone; and the rest of what a feddan costs.\n\nyields   = table(\"yield.csv\")       # (crop, region) => t/feddan\nindprod  = table(\"indprod.csv\")     # (crop, region) => t/feddan of an industrial by-product\nfeed     = table(\"feed.csv\")        # (crop, zone)   => t/feddan of fodder\nland     = table(\"land.csv\")        # (crop, zone, month) => share of the month the field is taken\nlaborm   = table(\"laborm.csv\")      # (crop, zone, month) => man-days per feddan\nwater    = table(\"water.csv\")       # (crop, zone, month) => water per feddan\ntractor  = table(\"tractor.csv\")     # (crop, zone)   => tractor hours per feddan\ncropdat  = table(\"cropdat.csv\")     # (crop, item)   => seed, inputs, protein and starch content\n\nThe regions: their area (1000 feddans), farmers (thousands), and the protein and starch their herds need (1000 t); monthly wages; the area that may be under vegetables in each season; upper bounds on cotton, citrus and sugarcane; and what it costs to carry straw between three of the regions.\n\nagrreg         = table(\"agrreg.csv\")    # (region, item)\nwages          = table(\"regwagm.csv\")   # (region, month) => £E per day\nvegetable_area = table(\"veg.csv\")       # (region, season)\nupbnds         = table(\"upbnds.csv\")    # (crop, region)\ntransfer_cost  = Dict(k => v for (k, v) in table(\"tranc.csv\") if v > 0)\n\nThe national market: base price, consumption and price elasticity of every marketed crop, the prices at which it can be imported and exported, and how much may be.\n\ndemdat       = table(\"demdat.csv\")      # (crop, item)\ntrade_limits = table(\"tlimit.csv\")      # (crop, \"imports\"/\"exports\") => 1000 t\nmarketed     = unique(first.(collect(keys(demdat))))\n\nThe scalars and small sets are in the GAMS code itself. Some crops are sold under another name (summer and nili maize are both maize); cotton, soybeans and sesame also yield vegetable oil; part of what is sold is lost in storage.\n\nreclassified = Dict(\"s-maize\" => \"maize\", \"n-maize\" => \"maize\", \"s-tomato\" => \"s+n-tomato\",\n                    \"n-tomato\" => \"s+n-tomato\", \"s-potato\" => \"potato\", \"n-potato\" => \"potato\")\noil          = Dict(\"cotton\" => 0.175, \"soyabeans\" => 0.175, \"sesame\" => 0.470)\nlosses       = Dict(\"wheat\" => 0.090, \"barley\" => 0.114, \"horse-bean\" => 0.152,\n                    \"lentils\" => 0.188, \"w-tomato\" => 0.100, \"cotton\" => 0.540,\n                    \"s-maize\" => 0.049, \"s-tomato\" => 0.100, \"sorghum\" => 0.055,\n                    \"w-onion\" => 0.057, \"rice\" => 0.045, \"groundnut\" => 0.038,\n                    \"s-potato\" => 0.285, \"citrus\" => 0.100)\nseasons      = [\"winter\", \"summer\", \"nili\"]\nvegetables   = Dict(\"winter\" => [\"w-veget\", \"w-tomato\", \"w-onion\"],\n                    \"summer\" => [\"s-veget\", \"s-tomato\", \"s-potato\"],\n                    \"nili\"   => [\"n-veget\", \"n-tomato\", \"n-potato\"])\ngreen        = [\"lo-berseem\", \"sh-berseem\", \"sugarcane\", \"s-maize\", \"n-maize\", \"sorghum\"]\ndry          = [\"wheat\", \"barley\", \"rice\"]\ntransported  = [\"wheat\", \"barley\"]      # whose straw can be carried between regions\nbyproduct_price = Dict(\"horse-bean\" => 21.75, \"rice\" => 5.75)\nworking_days    = 20      # per month\nnitrogen_price  = 178     # £E per t\nphosphate_price = 200     # £E per t\ntractor_hour    = 1.5     # £E\nconcentrate_price = 1000  # £E per t of protein or starch bought in\ngreen_dry_ratio = 0.06723\nmeat_and_milk   = 749576  # a constant in the objective, the value of livestock output\n\n(Three of the losses — summer maize, tomato and potato — are on names that are never sold, so in the original they never apply. The port keeps that.)","title":"The data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/egypt/#From-GAMS-to-Markets.jl","page":"Egyptian agriculture (GAMS egypt)","text":"The original is a linear program in its own terms: cropping activities, balances, and an objective that adds up surplus. Each piece has a counterpart here.\n\negypt.gms here\nxcrop(r,c), area under a crop a Process per crop and region, run at that level\nyld, feed, losses in storage the outputs of the process, net of the losses\nnetcs(c,r), inputs other than labour its value-added cost\nlabbal, flab, tlab labour of each month as a product, made by family and hired labour\nlandb, waterb, vegetb, xcrop.up Limits on the activities\nnutb, anut fodder fed to a herd whose size is fixed, or concentrates bought in\ngfodb a limit on the fodder produced and carried\ndemand on a grid of ten points a linear DemandCurve through the same base point\npe, pm, tlimit a flat world demand and supply, limits on the flows\ntrans, stran trade in straw at its transfer cost\n\nTwo regions are added to the five: \"egypt\", the national market where all the crops are sold and imports arrive, and \"world\".\n\nregions  = [farms; \"egypt\"; \"world\"]\nfodders  = sort(unique(first.(collect(keys(feed)))))\nlabour   = [\"labour $tm\" for tm in months]\nproducts = [marketed; [\"$c fodder\" for c in fodders]; \"protein\"; \"starch\"; labour]\n\nCrops. A crop is grown in a region if it yields something there, a product for the market or fodder. Its outputs per feddan are what is sold, net of storage losses, and its fodder; its inputs the labour of each month; its cost the tractor hours, seed, fertiliser and pesticide, less the value of its industrial by-product.\n\nfunction crop_outputs(c, r)\n    out = Pair{String,Float64}[]\n    y = get(yields, (c, r), 0.0)\n    if y > 0\n        sold = get(reclassified, c, c)\n        push!(out, sold => y * (1 - get(losses, sold, 0.0)))\n        haskey(oil, c) && push!(out, \"veg-oil\" => y * oil[c])\n    end\n    f = get(feed, (c, zone[r]), 0.0)\n    f > 0 && push!(out, \"$c fodder\" => f)\n    return out\nend\ncrop_cost(c, r) = get(tractor, (c, zone[r]), 0.0) * tractor_hour +\n    get(cropdat, (c, \"misc\"), 0.0) + get(cropdat, (c, \"pest\"), 0.0) +\n    get(cropdat, (c, \"n-fer\"), 0.0) * nitrogen_price +\n    get(cropdat, (c, \"p-fer\"), 0.0) * phosphate_price -\n    get(indprod, (c, r), 0.0) * get(byproduct_price, c, 0.0)\ncrops = sort(unique(first.(collect(keys(land)))))\ngrown = [(c, r) for r in farms for c in crops if !isempty(crop_outputs(c, r))]\ncrop_name(c, r) = \"$c in $r\"\ncrop_processes = [Process(name = crop_name(c, r), regions = [r],\n                          inputs = [leontief(product = \"labour $tm\",\n                                             coeff = laborm[(c, zone[r], tm)] / working_days)\n                                    for tm in months if get(laborm, (c, zone[r], tm), 0.0) > 0],\n                          outputs = crop_outputs(c, r),\n                          # the original charges these only where the crop has a yield\n                          vacost = get(yields, (c, r), 0.0) > 0 ? crop_cost(c, r) : 0.0)\n                  for (c, r) in grown]\ncrop_processes[1]\nProcess(\"barley in u-egypt\", Nest[Nest(0.25, [\"labour nov\"], [1.0], 0.0, 1.0), Nest(0.075, [\"labour dec\"], [1.0], 0.0, 1.0), Nest(0.025, [\"labour jan\"], [1.0], 0.0, 1.0), Nest(0.025, [\"labour feb\"], [1.0], 0.0, 1.0), Nest(0.025, [\"labour mar\"], [1.0], 0.0, 1.0), Nest(0.5, [\"labour apr\"], [1.0], 0.0, 1.0), Nest(0.2, [\"labour may\"], [1.0], 0.0, 1.0)], [\"barley\" => 1.24926, \"barley fodder\" => 1.37], [\"u-egypt\"], 29.509999999999998)\n\nLabour. Family labour costs the regional wage; hired labour twice as much, and there is no limit to it. Labour is measured in thousands of man-months, so a man-day costs a twentieth of it.\n\nlabour_processes = [Process(name = \"$who labour $tm\", inputs = Nest[], outputs = [\"labour $tm\" => 1.0],\n                            regions = farms,\n                            vacost = Dict(r => factor * wages[(r, tm)] * working_days for r in farms))\n                    for (who, factor) in ((\"family\", 1), (\"hired\", 2)) for tm in months]\n\nAnimals. The herds of each region need a given amount of protein and starch. It comes from the fodder, at its protein and starch content, or from concentrates bought at £E 1000 a tonne; what the fodder offers beyond the need is wasted. The herd is a process whose activity a limit below holds at one.\n\nfeeding = [Process(name = \"feed $c\", inputs = [leontief(product = \"$c fodder\", coeff = 1.0)],\n                   outputs = [n => cropdat[(c, n)] / 100 for n in (\"protein\", \"starch\")\n                              if get(cropdat, (c, n), 0.0) > 0])\n           for c in fodders]\nconcentrates = [Process(name = \"concentrate $n\", inputs = Nest[], outputs = [n => 1.0],\n                        vacost = concentrate_price, regions = farms) for n in (\"protein\", \"starch\")]\nherds = [Process(name = \"herd $r\", regions = [r], outputs = Pair{String,Float64}[],\n                 inputs = [leontief(product = n, coeff = agrreg[(r, n)]) for n in (\"protein\", \"starch\")])\n         for r in farms]\n\nWhat is not used is thrown away. The original lets production exceed sales, and nutrients exceed the herds' needs; in a market, that is free disposal.\n\ndisposals = [[disposal(product = c, regions = [\"egypt\"]) for c in marketed];\n             [disposal(product = n, regions = farms) for n in (\"protein\", \"starch\")]]\nprocesses = [crop_processes; labour_processes; feeding; concentrates; herds; disposals]\n\nThe national market. Demand is a straight line through the base price and consumption, with the base elasticity, exactly as the original computes its coefficients. The original never lets the price leave the range from 0.6 to 1.666 times the base price, which bounds consumption: a limit below keeps that range.\n\ndemand = [DemandCurve(product = c, region = \"egypt\", p0 = demdat[(c, \"base-p\")],\n                     q0 = demdat[(c, \"domes-cons\")], elasticity = -demdat[(c, \"elas\")],\n                     form = :linear)\n          for c in marketed]\ndemand[1]\nDemandCurve(product = \"n-veget\", region = \"egypt\", p0 = 70.0, q0 = 730.0, elasticity = 0.77, form = :linear)\n\nThe world sells at the import price and buys at the export price, in any quantity, for the crops the original lets be imported or exported.\n\nimportable = [c for c in marketed if get(trade_limits, (c, \"imports\"), 0.0) > 0]\nexportable = [c for c in marketed if get(trade_limits, (c, \"exports\"), 0.0) > 0]\nsupply = [SupplyCurve(product = c, region = \"world\", p0 = demdat[(c, \"import-p\")], q0 = 1,\n                     elasticity = Inf) for c in importable]\nappend!(demand, [DemandCurve(product = c, region = \"world\", p0 = demdat[(c, \"export-p\")], q0 = 1,\n                            elasticity = Inf) for c in exportable])\n\nRoutes. Crops go from the farms to the national market at no cost, as in the original, which has a single national balance; imports come in and exports go out through it; straw moves between three regions at its transfer cost.\n\ntransport_costs = Dict{Tuple{String,String,String},Float64}()\nfor c in marketed, r in farms\n    transport_costs[(c, r, \"egypt\")] = 0.0\nend\nfor c in importable\n    transport_costs[(c, \"world\", \"egypt\")] = 0.0\nend\nfor c in exportable\n    transport_costs[(c, \"egypt\", \"world\")] = 0.0\nend\nfor c in transported, ((r, rp), cost) in transfer_cost\n    transport_costs[(\"$c fodder\", r, rp)] = cost\nend","title":"From GAMS to Markets.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/egypt/#The-constraints,-as-limits","page":"Egyptian agriculture (GAMS egypt)","text":"Everything else in the original is a constraint on the activities: land in each month, water over the year, the area under vegetables in each season, the ceilings on cotton, citrus and sugarcane. Each is a family of Limits, written as the original writes it.\n\ngrows(c, r) = (c, r) in grown\nland_limits = @limits \"land\" [r in farms, tm in months] sum(\n    land[(c, zone[r], tm)] * activity(region = r, process = crop_name(c, r))\n    for c in crops if grows(c, r) && haskey(land, (c, zone[r], tm))) <= agrreg[(r, \"area\")]\nwater_limit = @limit \"water\" sum(\n    water[(c, zone[r], tm)] * activity(region = r, process = crop_name(c, r))\n    for (c, r) in grown for tm in months if haskey(water, (c, zone[r], tm))) <= 30000 * 1000\nvegetable_limits = @limits \"vegetables\" [s in seasons, r in farms] sum(\n    activity(region = r, process = crop_name(c, r))\n    for c in vegetables[s] if grows(c, r)) <= 1.5 * vegetable_area[(r, s)]\narea_limits = [@limit(\"area[$c, $r]\", activity(region = r, process = crop_name(c, r)) <= a)\n               for ((c, r), a) in upbnds if grows(c, r)]\n\nFamily labour is limited to 40% of the farmers in any month, and each herd is fed exactly once.\n\nfamily_limits = @limits \"family labour\" [r in farms, tm in months] activity(\n    region = r, process = \"family labour $tm\") <= 0.4 * agrreg[(r, \"farmers\")]\nherd_limits = @limits \"herd\" [r in farms] activity(region = r, process = \"herd $r\") == 1\n\nGreen fodder must not outweigh dry fodder by more than a given ratio, where dry fodder counts the straw carried in and not the straw carried out. A limit can name the production of a product, and its imports and exports, only where they exist, hence the conditions.\n\nmakes(r, p) = any(proc -> (proc.regions isa Colon || r in proc.regions) &&\n                          any(first(o) == p for o in proc.outputs), processes)\nmoves(r, p) = any(k -> k[1] == p && (k[2] == r || k[3] == r), keys(transport_costs))\nfodder_limits = @limits \"green fodder\" [r in farms] green_dry_ratio * sum(\n        production(region = r, product = \"$c fodder\") for c in green if makes(r, \"$c fodder\")) -\n    sum(production(region = r, product = \"$c fodder\") for c in dry if makes(r, \"$c fodder\")) -\n    sum(imports(region = r, product = \"$c fodder\") - exports(region = r, product = \"$c fodder\")\n        for c in transported if moves(r, \"$c fodder\"); init = zero(LimitExpression)) <= 0\n\nWhat can be traded with the world is limited for some crops, and consumption stays where the original's grid of prices keeps it.\n\ntrade_caps = [@limit(\"$dir[$c]\", flow(product = c, from = from, to = to) <= cap)\n              for ((c, dir), cap) in trade_limits if isfinite(cap) && cap > 0\n              for (from, to) in (dir == \"imports\" ? (\"world\", \"egypt\") : (\"egypt\", \"world\"),)]\nslope(c)  = demdat[(c, \"base-p\")] / demdat[(c, \"domes-cons\")] / demdat[(c, \"elas\")]\nchoke(c)  = demdat[(c, \"base-p\")] - slope(c) * demdat[(c, \"domes-cons\")]\nat_price(c, factor) = (factor * demdat[(c, \"base-p\")] - choke(c)) / slope(c)\nconsumption_range = @limits \"consumption\" [c in marketed] max(0.0, at_price(c, 1.666)) <=\n    consumption(region = \"egypt\", product = c) <= at_price(c, 0.6)\nlimits = [land_limits; water_limit; vegetable_limits; area_limits; family_limits; herd_limits;\n          fodder_limits; trade_caps; consumption_range]\nlength(limits)\n187","title":"The constraints, as limits"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/egypt/#The-equilibrium","page":"Egyptian agriculture (GAMS egypt)","text":"The solver is asked for a relative precision of 10⁻⁷ rather than its usual 10⁻⁸: the problem is nearly an LP, with many crops tied at the margin, and depending on the platform the last digit can cost Ipopt more than it is worth.\n\negypt = MarketData(regions = regions, products = products, demand = demand, supply = supply,\n                   processes = processes, limits = limits, transport_costs = transport_costs)\nresult = solve_market(egypt; solver_options = (tol = 1e-7,))\nresult.status\nLOCALLY_SOLVED::TerminationStatusCode = 4\n\nWhat is grown where, in thousands of feddans:\n\ncrop_of = Dict(crop_name(c, r) => c for (c, r) in grown)\nareas = filter(row -> haskey(crop_of, row.process), result.activity)\nareas.crop = [crop_of[p] for p in areas.process]\narea_table = unstack(select(areas, :crop, :region, :level), :region, :level; fill = 0.0)\nsort!(area_table, :crop)\n\u001b[1m26×6 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m crop       \u001b[0m\u001b[1m u-egypt  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m m-egypt   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m e-delta  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m m-delta   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m w-delta \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\n─────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ barley       92.609     0.0        0.0       0.0         0.0\n   2 │ citrus       17.0      34.0       75.0      35.7149      0.0\n   3 │ cotton      175.0       0.0      341.0     416.0       160.0\n   4 │ flax          0.0       0.0       55.3313    0.0         0.0\n   5 │ groundnut    40.8793    0.0        0.0       0.0         0.0\n   6 │ horse-bean    0.0       0.0      243.971   145.152       0.0\n   7 │ lentils     208.682     0.0        0.0       0.0         0.0\n   8 │ lo-berseem  125.608   318.282    293.455   323.936       0.0\n  ⋮  │     ⋮          ⋮          ⋮         ⋮          ⋮         ⋮\n  20 │ sorghum     142.784   261.781      0.0       0.0         0.0\n  21 │ soyabeans     0.0       0.0        0.0       0.0       119.38\n  22 │ sugarcane   250.0       0.0        0.0       0.0         0.0\n  23 │ w-onion       0.0       8.01679    0.0      81.4357      0.0\n  24 │ w-tomato      0.0      94.8792   193.5       7.78671     0.0\n  25 │ w-veget      45.5263    0.0        0.0      36.7776    127.5\n  26 │ wheat       417.618   451.262      0.0     219.743       0.0\n\u001b[36m                                                      11 rows omitted\u001b[0m\n\nThe national market: price, consumption, and what crosses the border.\n\nflow_of(c, from, to) = (rows = result.trade[(result.trade.product .== c) .& (result.trade.from .== from) .&\n                                            (result.trade.to .== to), :quantity];\n                        isempty(rows) ? 0.0 : only(rows))\nnational = DataFrame(crop = marketed,\n    base_price = [demdat[(c, \"base-p\")] for c in marketed],\n    price = [only(result.prices[(result.prices.region .== \"egypt\") .& (result.prices.product .== c), :price])\n             for c in marketed],\n    base_consumption = [demdat[(c, \"domes-cons\")] for c in marketed],\n    consumption = [only(result.consumption[(result.consumption.region .== \"egypt\") .&\n                                           (result.consumption.product .== c), :quantity])\n                   for c in marketed],\n    imports = [flow_of(c, \"world\", \"egypt\") for c in marketed],\n    exports = [flow_of(c, \"egypt\", \"world\") for c in marketed])\n\u001b[1m23×7 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m crop       \u001b[0m\u001b[1m base_price \u001b[0m\u001b[1m price    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m base_consumption \u001b[0m\u001b[1m consumption \u001b[0m\u001b[1m import\u001b[0m ⋯\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64          \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float6\u001b[0m ⋯\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ n-veget           70.0   32.3405             730.0      954.84    0.0   ⋯\n   2 │ horse-bean       199.0  116.548              254.0      318.008   0.0\n   3 │ rice              81.0   84.6896            2382.0     2301.71    0.0\n   4 │ soyabeans        210.0   84.7124             110.0      154.0     0.0\n   5 │ w-tomato          87.0   57.8557            1006.0     1258.75    0.0   ⋯\n   6 │ barley            86.0   79.6152             114.0      115.693   0.0\n   7 │ veg-oil          455.0  500.5                395.0      347.6    36.497\n   8 │ sesame           600.0  176.461               97.0      135.8     0.0\n  ⋮  │     ⋮           ⋮          ⋮             ⋮               ⋮          ⋮   ⋱\n  17 │ w-veget          100.0   26.0845            1055.0     1371.5     0.0   ⋯\n  18 │ maize            123.0  112.169             3231.0     3424.46    0.0\n  19 │ citrus            85.0   84.5503            1102.0     1111.56    0.0\n  20 │ s-veget           65.0   40.9138            2333.0     2981.38    0.0\n  21 │ ot-legumes       240.0  139.117               55.0       68.86    0.0   ⋯\n  22 │ flax              30.0   29.5193             171.0      173.74    0.0\n  23 │ w-onion           62.0   13.6378             568.0      713.408   0.0\n\u001b[36m                                                    2 columns and 8 rows omitted\u001b[0m\n\nAnd what the scarce resources are worth: the rent of each limit that binds, in £E per unit. Land is scarce at the turn of the seasons, in November and in May, when the crops of one season are still in the ground and those of the next want it; family labour at the peaks of work; cotton, citrus and sugarcane at their ceilings; and several exports at their caps. Water is not: the crops the market asks for need 28.9 of the 30 million units available.\n\nbinding = filter(row -> row.rent > 1e-6, result.limits)\nselect(binding, :limit, :value, :rent)\n\u001b[1m67×3 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m limit                   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m value    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m rent       \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String                  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64    \u001b[0m\n─────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ land[u-egypt, nov]       1195.0     51.6771\n   2 │ land[u-egypt, mar]       1195.0     13.8107\n   3 │ land[u-egypt, jun]       1195.0     69.3306\n   4 │ land[u-egypt, aug]       1195.0     58.0033\n   5 │ land[m-egypt, nov]       1009.0     80.4126\n   6 │ land[m-egypt, feb]       1009.0      6.32861\n   7 │ land[m-egypt, may]       1009.0     75.275\n   8 │ land[m-egypt, jun]       1009.0     64.6342\n  ⋮  │            ⋮                ⋮          ⋮\n  61 │ consumption[sesame]       135.8    183.539\n  62 │ consumption[potato]      1608.84    11.4534\n  63 │ consumption[s+n-tomato]  2126.8      0.956829\n  64 │ consumption[groundnut]     35.0      0.824577\n  65 │ consumption[w-veget]     1371.5     33.9155\n  66 │ consumption[ot-legumes]    68.86     4.88282\n  67 │ consumption[w-onion]      713.408   23.5622\n\u001b[36m                                      52 rows omitted\u001b[0m","title":"The equilibrium"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/egypt/#Checking-the-port","page":"Egyptian agriculture (GAMS egypt)","text":"egypt_gams.jl transcribes the original line by line into JuMP: the same variables, the same equations, the same objective, on the data loaded above. With grid = false its demand curves are the exact lines rather than ten points on each, which is the only change, and which the port shares.\n\ninclude(joinpath(pkgdir(Markets), \"examples\", \"egypt\", \"egypt_gams.jl\"))\nexact = egypt_gams(grid = false)\n(transcription = exact.objective - meat_and_milk, port = result.objective,\n difference = exact.objective - meat_and_milk - result.objective)\n(transcription = 3.385741617573048e6, port = 3.3857415936368406e6, difference = 0.02393620740622282)\n\nThe objectives agree, and so do the prices:\n\ncomparison = DataFrame(crop = marketed, transcription = [exact.price[c] for c in marketed],\n                       port = national.price)\ncomparison.difference = comparison.port .- comparison.transcription\ncomparison\n\u001b[1m23×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m crop       \u001b[0m\u001b[1m transcription \u001b[0m\u001b[1m port     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m difference  \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64       \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64     \u001b[0m\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ n-veget           32.3405   32.3405  -9.0874e-6\n   2 │ horse-bean       116.548   116.548    6.55109e-6\n   3 │ rice              84.6896   84.6896  -3.45094e-7\n   4 │ soyabeans         84.7124   84.7124   8.12801e-5\n   5 │ w-tomato          57.8557   57.8557   2.62829e-6\n   6 │ barley            79.6152   79.6152  -7.96955e-7\n   7 │ veg-oil          500.5     500.5     -4.29355e-9\n   8 │ sesame           176.461   176.461    8.54359e-6\n  ⋮  │     ⋮             ⋮           ⋮           ⋮\n  17 │ w-veget           26.0845   26.0845   1.78604e-6\n  18 │ maize            112.169   112.169    1.93676e-6\n  19 │ citrus            84.5503   84.5503   3.05061e-6\n  20 │ s-veget           40.9138   40.9138   1.31662e-5\n  21 │ ot-legumes       139.117   139.117    6.69826e-5\n  22 │ flax              29.5193   29.5193  -1.69058e-5\n  23 │ w-onion           13.6378   13.6378   6.30635e-7\n\u001b[36m                                          8 rows omitted\u001b[0m\n\nThe original itself solves the grid. Ten points per curve, joined by chords, put welfare a little below the curve between the points, so the grid's solution sits near the exact one but not on it:\n\noriginal = egypt_gams(grid = true)\nDataFrame(crop = marketed, original = [original.price[c] for c in marketed],\n          exact = [exact.price[c] for c in marketed])\n\u001b[1m23×3 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m crop       \u001b[0m\u001b[1m original \u001b[0m\u001b[1m exact    \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ n-veget      31.9688   32.3405\n   2 │ horse-bean  117.515   116.548\n   3 │ rice         83.5312   84.6896\n   4 │ soyabeans    84.184    84.7124\n   5 │ w-tomato     58.2634   57.8557\n   6 │ barley       80.7136   79.6152\n   7 │ veg-oil     500.5     500.5\n   8 │ sesame      168.665   176.461\n  ⋮  │     ⋮          ⋮         ⋮\n  17 │ w-veget      26.3612   26.0845\n  18 │ maize       110.222   112.169\n  19 │ citrus       85.0      84.5503\n  20 │ s-veget      40.9611   40.9138\n  21 │ ot-legumes  138.842   139.117\n  22 │ flax         29.957    29.5193\n  23 │ w-onion      13.741    13.6378\n\u001b[36m                        8 rows omitted\u001b[0m","title":"Checking the port"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/egypt/#The-question-it-was-built-for","page":"Egyptian agriculture (GAMS egypt)","text":"What is water worth, and what happens if there is less of it? Take a fifth of it away, solving to the same precision as before:\n\ndrier = MarketData(regions = regions, products = products, demand = demand, supply = supply,\n                   processes = processes, transport_costs = transport_costs,\n                   limits = [l.name == \"water\" ? @limit(\"water\", sum(\n                                 water[(c, zone[r], tm)] * activity(region = r, process = crop_name(c, r))\n                                 for (c, r) in grown for tm in months\n                                 if haskey(water, (c, zone[r], tm))) <= 0.8 * 30000 * 1000) : l\n                             for l in limits])\ndry_result = solve_market(drier; solver_options = (tol = 1e-7,))\nwater_rent(res) = only(res.limits[res.limits.limit .== \"water\", :rent])\n(water_rent = water_rent(result), with_a_fifth_less = water_rent(dry_result),\n welfare_lost = result.objective - dry_result.objective)\n(water_rent = 0.0, with_a_fifth_less = 0.06575881618538783, welfare_lost = 160668.75771934213)\n\nWater now binds, and earns a rent; the transcription, given the same water, agrees on it and on the welfare:\n\nexact_drier = egypt_gams(grid = false, water_available = 0.8 * 30000 * 1000)\n(transcription = (water_rent = exact_drier.water_rent,\n                  objective = exact_drier.objective - meat_and_milk),\n port = (water_rent = water_rent(dry_result), objective = dry_result.objective))\n(transcription = (water_rent = 0.0657588148453839, objective = 3.225072847881503e6), port = (water_rent = 0.06575881618538783, objective = 3.2250728359174985e6))\n\nThe crops that give way are the thirsty summer ones, rice above all; the winter crops, wheat and berseem, which need least water, take back some of the land:\n\ntotal_area(res) = (a = filter(row -> haskey(crop_of, row.process), res.activity);\n                   combine(groupby(transform(a, :process => ByRow(p -> crop_of[p]) => :crop), :crop),\n                           :level => sum => :area))\nchange = outerjoin(rename(total_area(result), :area => :now), rename(total_area(dry_result), :area => :drier),\n                   on = :crop)\nchange.now = coalesce.(change.now, 0.0); change.drier = coalesce.(change.drier, 0.0)\nchange.difference = change.drier .- change.now\nsort!(filter(row -> abs(row.difference) > 1, change), :difference)\n\u001b[1m23×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m crop       \u001b[0m\u001b[1m now      \u001b[0m\u001b[1m drier     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m difference \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64    \u001b[0m\n─────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ rice        1131.63    522.116   -609.516\n   2 │ n-maize      477.004     0.0     -477.004\n   3 │ sh-berseem  1268.7     823.171   -445.528\n   4 │ s-tomato     181.355     0.0     -181.355\n   5 │ cotton      1092.0     978.59    -113.41\n   6 │ sorghum      404.565   319.811    -84.754\n   7 │ horse-bean   389.123   316.684    -72.4385\n   8 │ lentils      208.682   171.435    -37.2472\n  ⋮  │     ⋮          ⋮          ⋮          ⋮\n  17 │ sugarcane    250.0     248.161     -1.83923\n  18 │ n-veget      135.175   133.358     -1.81698\n  19 │ barley        92.609    94.1931     1.58412\n  20 │ wheat       1088.62   1252.72     164.1\n  21 │ s-maize     1384.45   1558.51     174.054\n  22 │ n-tomato      75.145   270.459    195.314\n  23 │ lo-berseem  1061.28   1508.15     446.868\n\u001b[36m                                     8 rows omitted\u001b[0m\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"The question it was built for"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/trade/#Taxes-and-trade","page":"Taxes and trade","text":"","title":"Taxes and trade"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/trade/#Taxes,-subsidies-and-other-wedges","page":"Taxes and trade","text":"A tax drives a wedge between what one side of a transaction pays and what the other receives. Tax places one on production, on consumption, on a transformation or on a trade route, as an amount per unit or as a fraction, and a subsidy is a negative rate.\n\nIn a surplus-maximising program a wedge enters exactly as a cost does: a tax on a route is indistinguishable, to the optimiser, from a more expensive route. The first-order conditions of the taxed program are then the equilibrium conditions of the taxed market, so the quantities and prices that come out are the right ones. A specific tariff of 10 on a route with a freight cost of 5 opens a price gap of exactly 15; a value-added tax of 25% puts the consumer price 25% above the market price and moves the quantity accordingly.\n\nwarning: The objective is no longer welfare\nThe program treats the tax as a cost consumed, when it is a transfer received, so the objective of a taxed model is not welfare and comparing it with an untaxed one overstates the loss. With per-unit rates it falls short by exactly the revenue; a relative rate on supply or demand scales a whole curve, and the gap is then not the revenue. Results.welfare is welfare in every case — the surplus at the solution, without the taxes — and Results.taxes reports what each instrument raises; who ultimately gets it is outside the model.","title":"Taxes, subsidies and other wedges"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/trade/#Why-ad-valorem-rates-on-trade-are-special","page":"Taxes and trade","text":"Three of the four bases take an ad valorem rate exactly, because the thing the rate multiplies is known before the model is solved: a tax on production scales the supply curve, one on consumption scales the demand curve, one on a process scales its value-added cost. A tariff is different — it is a fraction of the price at the origin, which is a dual of the problem and therefore not available to the objective that produces it.\n\nThe honest routes out are to restate the model as a complementarity problem, where prices are variables — the approach of Mathiesen (1985) and Rutherford (1995), available in Julia as [C4] on the solver [C5] — or to iterate. This package iterates: solve_market solves repeatedly, each time holding the tariff at the money equivalent implied by the previous solution's producer prices, and stops when the two agree. The fixed point is the ad valorem equilibrium — at it, the price gap on a used route is freight plus the rate times the origin price, which is what a tariff means — and everything else in the model is still solved exactly, in one pass.","title":"Why ad valorem rates on trade are special"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/trade/#Where-along-the-chain-to-tax","page":"Taxes and trade","text":"An externality carried by the goods — the carbon in wood, the material that ends up as waste — can be taxed where it arises, at the source, or where it ends up, embodied in what is consumed, at the end of the chain. Gerlagh, Lorang, Pommeret & Pottier (2026) show that the two are equivalent only if the prices of goods can depend on their embodied content, so that a buyer facing the end-of-chain tax pays less for a cleaner unit of the same product and the producer is rewarded for making it. Where buyers see only the average content of a product group, the end-of-chain tax falls on that average, producers have no reason to change their recipes, and the source tax is the more efficient instrument — by a factor of ten for iron, in the calibrated world model of Lorang & Gerlagh (2026).\n\nIn this package a Tax is levied per unit, or as a fraction of the value, of a product: a tax on primary supply (on = \"supply\") is a source tax, and behaves as the efficient one; a tax on consumption (on = \"demand\") taxes the product and not its content, which is the group-average case. Where recipes are fixed the two coincide, as the content per unit of every good is fixed too; where processes choose their inputs from a ces nest, or users their origins from an Armington composite, they do not. A consumption tax is then a second-best proxy for pricing the content, and the model will show it as such.","title":"Where along the chain to tax"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/trade/#Trade-between-regions","page":"Taxes and trade","text":"Regions trade the same physical product at a cost per unit of quantity shipped. This is the spatial price equilibrium:\n\nprices of a product in two regions differ by at most the transport cost between them;\non a route that is used, the price gap equals the transport cost.\n\nTransport costs are per unit of quantity because shipping a tonne of ore costs roughly the same whether the ore is rich or poor. Bulky, low-value products — gravel, cement, ore — therefore have high transport costs relative to their value, which limits their trade.\n\nSince products are identical whatever their origin, two regions never ship the same product to each other — there is no cross-hauling, though a region may carry it through on the cheapest path between two others — and each region buys from the cheapest source available to it. Trade patterns are therefore sharper than observed ones: a small cost advantage can capture an entire market. This is the default; the next section is the way out of it.","title":"Trade between regions"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/trade/#Imperfect-substitution-between-origins-(Armington)","page":"Taxes and trade","text":"Observed trade does not look like that. Countries import and export the same product at the same time, market shares move gradually when prices change, and a region's own product keeps a share of its home market even when it is not the cheapest. The standard answer, Armington (1969), is to treat the varieties of a product coming from different origins as imperfect substitutes: \"wine from France\" and \"wine from Chile\" are related but distinct goods.\n\nListing a product in the Armington specifications of the economy turns this on for it. Each destination r then buys quantities X_o of the variety of every origin o it can buy from (its own, plus every origin with a transport route into it) and uses them through a CES composite\n\nA_r le left( sum_o delta_or^1sigma X_or^rho right)^1rho\nqquad rho = fracsigma - 1sigma\n\nwhich is the same aggregator as a CES input nest, applied to origins instead of input products. Final demand and the processes of region r then draw on the composite A_r, not on the individual varieties.\n\nThree things change.\n\nThere are now two prices per product and region. Local producers are paid pi^s_r, the value of their own variety, while local users pay pi^c_r, the price of the composite, which is the CES price index of the delivered prices of all origins:\n\npi^c_r = left( sum_o delta_or left(pi^s_o + tau_orright)^1-sigma right)^1(1-sigma)\n\nThe two are reported side by side in the prices table as producer_price and price. They differ because a region blends its own variety with imported ones: an expensive producer can keep selling at a high price while its customers pay much less on average.\n\nRegions cross-haul, and their prices are linked everywhere. Because each destination wants some of every variety, a region usually imports and exports the same product at once, and market shares respond smoothly to relative prices with elasticity sigma. Prices are no longer tied together by the transport cost: a shift in supply or demand anywhere passes into every other region's composite price, with a strength set by sigma and by the shares, and producer prices can differ across regions by far more than the cost of shipping between them.\n\nThe shares matter, and they are not the observed shares. delta_or is the share origin o would have in destination r if all delivered prices were equal, so it carries the home bias and the trade pattern that prices alone do not explain. Base-year delivered prices are never equal — transport alone sees to that — so feeding an observed trade matrix in as delta does not reproduce the base year. Calibration means inverting the CES demand condition X_o = A delta_o (P^cq_o)^sigma:\n\ndelta_or propto X_or q_or^sigma\nqquadtextequivalentlyqquad\ndelta_or propto s_or q_or^sigma-1\n\nfrom base-year quantities X or value shares s and delivered prices q. armington_shares does this. The gap is not small: an origin with an 80% observed share at a below-average delivered price calibrates to a delta noticeably under 80%, because part of its success is explained by its price rather than by preference. Left unspecified, every origin available to a destination gets an equal share, which is neutral but rarely realistic.\n\nTwo consequences of calibrated shares are worth knowing:\n\nAn origin with no share is not in the composite. A route that carried nothing in the base year carries nothing ever after, whatever prices do. This is the well-known small-shares problem of Armington models: they cannot create new trade relationships, only grow and shrink existing ones. Give a plausible small share to a route you want to be able to open.\nThe composite is not the physical sum. A_r is a CES aggregate of the varieties, and with shares summing to one it is at most their sum, with equality only when the mix is the one the shares describe. Production, trade and the balances are in physical units throughout; it is only the composite that is an index. The scale of an Armington specification multiplies the composite, and armington_calibration sets it so that the composite equals the physical sum at the base year: base-year data in m³ then balance exactly, and the composite is priced at the average delivered unit value. Away from the base year the composite drifts from the sum again, to either side, as any CES does; the results report both — see Physical accounts.","title":"Imperfect substitution between origins (Armington)"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/trade/#Shares-and-elasticity-answer-different-questions","page":"Taxes and trade","text":"The two parameters are easy to confuse, and they pull in different directions:\n\ndelta_or sets how much of origin o a destination takes when delivered prices are equal. A regulatory or commercial penalty against an origin — a standard its products meet only partly, a certification few of its producers hold — belongs here, as a low share.\nsigma sets how readily buyers switch when relative prices move. It is about technical interchangeability, not about preference. McDaniel & Balistreri (2003) review what it has actually been estimated to be, and how widely those estimates differ — worth reading before settling on a number.\n\nA low sigma is therefore not a barrier against an origin. It means buyers cannot get away from it: as sigma to 1 the value shares stay at delta whatever the prices, so an expensive origin keeps its share instead of losing it. To represent a barrier, lower its share, or add its compliance cost to the transport cost of that route; to represent products that are hard to swap, lower the elasticity.","title":"Shares and elasticity answer different questions"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/trade/#Groups-of-origins","page":"Taxes and trade","text":"One sigma per destination still says that every origin substitutes equally well for every other. Real markets are not like that: two regions whose products share grades, standards or certification are near-interchangeable, while a third whose products need re-grading, or meet the importer's non-tariff measures only partly, is a distant substitute for both.\n\nA single CES cannot express this — equal pairwise substitution is what CES means. The structure that can is a nest of nests, after Sato (1967): group the origins that are interchangeable, give the group its own higher sigma, and let the group as a whole substitute against the rest at the lower sigma of the level above. In this package that is an OriginNest inside an Armington:\n\nArmington(product = \"cars\", destination = \"EU\", sigma = 2.5,\n          nests = [OriginNest(sigma = 12, origins = [\"EU\", \"US\"])])\n\nBuyers in the EU swap EU for US cars readily (sigma = 12) and replace either with Chinese cars only slowly (sigma = 25).\n\nThis generalises: the groups may hold groups, and the structure is a tree whose leaves are origins. The substitution between any two origins is then the elasticity of the smallest group containing both. That is not an arbitrary choice of representation, it is the only consistent one. An arbitrary matrix sigma_oo is not integrable to any cost function: if two origins are perfect substitutes they are the same good, so they must substitute identically against any third, and more generally, among any three origins the two that join higher in the tree must share the same elasticity. A tree enforces that by construction; a matrix does not.\n\nTwo consequences worth knowing:\n\nA group whose sigma equals its parent's is a no-op — it says the members are no closer to each other than to the outside. The package requires a group to be at least as substitutable inside as outside, and rejects the reverse as a specification error.\nAs a group's internal sigma grows, its members' prices are pulled together and the group behaves as one pooled variety: the Samuelson case, recovered inside a nest. At sigma = infty it is that case — the members are added up, the group enters the level above at the cheapest delivered price of its members, and between themselves they neither cross-haul nor differ in price by more than the freight. This is how a set of regions that are one market internally, such as the regions of a country, sit inside a world model as a single distinct variety.","title":"Groups of origins"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/trade/#Different-markets,-different-structures","page":"Taxes and trade","text":"Technical standards and non-tariff measures are set by the importer, so they are asymmetric: the EU may treat Chinese cars as a distant substitute while Chinese buyers treat European cars as a close one. A specification therefore applies to the destinations given in its destination field, with one for every destination (:) as the fallback, and the elasticity, the groups and the shares may all differ between importing markets.\n\nA product is nonetheless either homogeneous everywhere or imperfectly substitutable everywhere: it has one variety per origin, or none. And a region that cannot produce a product at all — no supply curve, and no process making it there — has no variety of it: it is nobody's origin, not even its own, and its producer_price is reported as missing while it buys at the composite price like anyone else.","title":"Different markets, different structures"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/trade/#The-limit-\\sigma-\\to-\\infty","page":"Taxes and trade","text":"As sigma grows the composite becomes a plain sum of the varieties and its price index tends to the cheapest delivered price: the model returns to the homogeneous, Samuelson spatial price equilibrium of the previous section. This is not an approximation — at sigma = infty, which is the default for every product not listed, the engine builds exactly the homogeneous formulation, with one balance and one price per region and product.\n\nSo the two regimes are the ends of one scale, and sigma is where a product sits on it: low sigma for goods whose origin matters to buyers (branded or quality-differentiated products: cars, wine, machinery), high sigma for commodities that are graded and interchangeable (crude oil of a given grade, wheat, copper).","title":"The limit sigma to infty"},{"category":"section","location":"modelling/trade/#What-it-costs","page":"Taxes and trade","text":"The Armington formulation is bigger: a variety variable per (product, origin, destination), a composite variable and two balances per (product, region), instead of one trade variable per route and one balance, plus one further variable and constraint per group of origins. And sigma, the groups and the shares are extra parameters to estimate, which is why they are opt-in per product rather than global.","title":"What it costs"}]
}
